window.ECON={"chapters":[{"id":"1","title":"Introduction","intro":"Macroeconomics is defined as that branch of economics which studies economic issues or economic problems at the level of an economy as a whole.","lessons":[{"title":"Meaning, origin and principal concerns","body":"Macroeconomics is defined as that branch of economics which studies economic issues or economic problems at the level of an economy as a whole. The term Macro comes from Makros, meaning large. Keynes’ The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money (1936) established macroeconomics as a separate branch. Its principal concerns are employment, inflation, growth of output and depression. Keynes stressed government intervention when inadequate demand reduces output and employment.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"1.1 ABOUT MACROECONOMICS"},{"title":"Microeconomics versus macroeconomics","body":"Microeconomics studies scarcity and choice at the level of an individual, household, firm or industry; allocation of resources is its central issue. Macroeconomics studies these problems for the economy as a whole; determination of overall output and employment is its central issue. Micro uses partial equilibrium analysis, studying one market while holding other markets constant. Macro uses general equilibrium analysis, examining simultaneous equilibrium across markets. An industry is an aggregate, but studying one industry is still microeconomics.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"1.2 HOW MACROECONOMICS DIFFERS FROM MICROECONOMICS?"},{"title":"Micro–macro paradox and interdependence","body":"What is logical at the micro level may not be logical at the macro level. Saving may improve one person’s future prosperity; if everyone saves more by spending less, aggregate demand, investment, output and employment may fall. Macro affects micro when an RBI interest-rate change affects one producer’s borrowing. Micro affects macro when individual increases in spending together raise aggregate demand.","formula":"","kind":"Purple-box concept","textbookHeading":"Micro-Macro Paradox · Micro-Macro Interdependence"},{"title":"Scope and significance","body":"Macroeconomics studies national income, employment, general prices, growth, money, banking, government budgets and international transactions. It helps explain an economy’s functioning and formulate policies for growth with stability. Keep the level of analysis clear: the price of one product is micro; the general price level is macro.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"1.3 SCOPE AND SIGNIFICANCE OF MACROECONOMICS"}],"subject":"macro","number":"1"},{"id":"2","title":"Some Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics","intro":"Final goods are those goods which have crossed the boundary line of production and are ready for use by their final users.","lessons":[{"title":"Final goods, intermediate goods and end use","body":"Final goods are those goods which have crossed the boundary line of production and are ready for use by their final users. They lead to final consumption expenditure or investment expenditure. Intermediate goods are purchased by one firm from another for resale or for use as raw material in producing other goods. Classification depends on end use, not the physical nature of the good: milk bought by a household is final; milk bought by an ice-cream producer is intermediate. Unsold additions to inventory count as investment.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.1 MEANING OF END-USE CLASSIFICATION OF GOODS"},{"title":"Consumption goods versus capital goods","body":"Consumption goods directly satisfy human wants. They include durable goods, non-durable goods, semi-durable goods and services. Capital goods are fixed assets of producers, repeatedly used in production: durable-use producer goods such as plant and machinery. A household car is a durable consumption good; a taxi used to produce transport services is a capital good. Both are final goods. A capital good is not intermediate merely because it helps produce something.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.1.1 Final Goods · Consumption Goods · Capital Goods"},{"title":"Consumption expenditure and investment","body":"Consumption expenditure includes expenditure by households, government and non-profit private institutions. Investment is a process of adding to the stock of capital. Fixed investment adds to fixed assets; inventory investment changes stocks of raw materials, semi-finished goods and finished goods. New residential construction is investment. Buying existing shares transfers financial ownership; it does not itself create new capital goods. Inventory investment can be negative when closing stock is less than opening stock.","formula":"Inventory investment = Closing stock − Opening stock\nGross investment = Fixed investment + Inventory investment","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.2 CONSUMPTION EXPENDITURE · 2.3 INVESTMENT"},{"title":"Gross investment, net investment and depreciation","body":"Net investment refers to the increase in the stock of capital during an accounting year. Gross investment includes replacement of worn-out capital. Depreciation, also called consumption of fixed capital, refers to loss of value of fixed assets in use through normal wear and tear, normal accidental damage and expected obsolescence. Unexpected destruction is a capital loss rather than normal depreciation. A depreciation reserve fund is kept to cope with depreciation losses.","formula":"Net investment = Gross investment − Depreciation\nDepreciation per year = (Capital value − Scrap value) ÷ Estimated life","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.3.3 Gross Investment, Net Investment and the Concept of Depreciation"},{"title":"Stocks and flows","body":"Stocks are quantities measured at a point of time; flows are quantities measured over a specified period of time. Wealth, money supply, capital and bank balances are stocks. Income, consumption, investment and saving are flows. Capital is a stock; investment is the flow adding to it. “Money supply on 31 March” is a stock even though money changes hands repeatedly.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.4 STOCKS AND FLOWS"},{"title":"Four sectors and circular flow of income","body":"The household sector supplies factor services and consumes goods. The producing sector produces goods and services. Government acts as a welfare agency and producer; the external sector links the domestic economy with the rest of the world. Real flows are flows of goods and factor services; money flows are payments and receipts in the opposite direction. Circular flow of income refers to the circularity of production, income and expenditure across sectors. In a simple two-sector model, production generates income and income generates expenditure.","formula":"Production → Income generation → Expenditure → Production\nValue of output = Factor income = Final expenditure","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.5 FOUR SECTORS · 2.6 INTERSECTORAL FLOWS · 2.7 CIRCULAR FLOW OF INCOME"},{"title":"Types of consumption goods","textbookHeading":"2.1.1 (a) Consumption Goods or Consumer Goods","body":"Durable-use consumer goods serve consumers repeatedly over a long period, such as cars and refrigerators. Semi-durable goods serve over a shorter period, such as clothes. Non-durable goods are used up in a single act of consumption, such as food. Services are intangible acts satisfying wants, such as teaching or medical treatment. Durability alone does not make an item a capital good: capital goods are used by producers in production.","kind":"Exam points","formula":""},{"title":"Why final goods are counted and intermediate goods are not","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Significance of the Distinction between Intermediate Goods and Final Goods","body":"Intermediate inputs are embodied in final output. Counting both adds the same value more than once. If cloth worth ₹5,000 becomes shirts worth ₹7,000, final output is ₹7,000, not ₹12,000. Under the value-added method, the cloth producer’s value and the shirt producer’s additional ₹2,000 are each counted once. It is incorrect to say intermediate goods contribute nothing: their contribution is included in final value.","kind":"Ability Zone","formula":""}],"subject":"macro","number":"2"},{"id":"3","title":"National Income and Related Aggregates","intro":"National income is the sum total of factor incomes earned by normal residents of a country during the period of an accounting year.","lessons":[{"title":"National income, factor incomes and transfer incomes","body":"National income is the sum total of factor incomes earned by normal residents of a country during the period of an accounting year. Factor incomes are rewards for factor services: compensation of employees, rent, interest and profit. Transfer incomes such as gifts, charity and old-age pensions are unilateral receipts without a current productive service in return; they are excluded. An employer’s retirement pension earned through service differs from an old-age welfare pension.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.1 CONCEPT OF NATIONAL INCOME"},{"title":"Normal residents and domestic territory","body":"A normal resident ordinarily resides in a country and has a centre of economic interest there. Citizenship alone does not determine residence. Tourists and visitors for medical treatment do not become residents merely through travel. Diplomats remain residents of their home countries; border workers are residents where they live. Domestic territory is economic territory: Indian embassies abroad belong to India’s domestic territory; foreign embassies in India do not. Check the Ability Zone for ships, aircraft, fishing vessels and other special cases.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.1.3 Normal Residents · 3.2.2 Domestic Territory"},{"title":"Domestic income versus national income","body":"Domestic income is the sum total of factor incomes generated within the domestic territory, whether earned by residents or non-residents. National income belongs to normal residents, wherever earned. Net factor income from abroad is factor income received from the rest of the world minus factor income paid to the rest of the world. Remittances that are gifts are transfers, not NFIA.","formula":"NNP at FC = NDP at FC + NFIA\nNFIA = Factor income from abroad − Factor income to abroad","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.2 DOMESTIC AND NATIONAL CONCEPTS OF INCOME"},{"title":"The three conversion bridges","body":"Use three independent adjustments. Gross to net: subtract depreciation. Domestic to national: add NFIA. Market price to factor cost: subtract net indirect taxes, which equal indirect taxes minus subsidies. Reverse the sign when travelling backwards. A negative NFIA reduces national income; negative NIT means subsidies exceed indirect taxes. Do not deduct indirect taxes again if NIT is already supplied.","formula":"NNPFC = GDPMP − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA\nGDPMP = NNPFC + Depreciation + NIT − NFIA","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.3 GROSS AND NET · 3.4 MARKET PRICE AND FACTOR COST"},{"title":"Eight related aggregates","body":"GDPMP, GDPFC, NDPMP and NDPFC are domestic measures; GNPMP, GNPFC, NNPMP and NNPFC are national measures. Gross measures include consumption of fixed capital; net measures exclude it. Market-price measures include net indirect taxes; factor-cost measures exclude them. National income means NNPFC; domestic income means NDPFC. Identify all three labels before substituting a number.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.5 AGGREGATES RELATED TO NATIONAL INCOME"},{"title":"Nominal GDP, real GDP and GDP deflator","body":"Nominal GDP is valued at current-year prices; real GDP is valued at base-year prices. Real GDP helps isolate changes in physical production from changes in prices. Nominal GDP may rise with unchanged output simply because prices rise. The GDP deflator is the ratio of nominal GDP to real GDP multiplied by 100. Use the price index as a percentage index, not as a decimal, in the formula below.","formula":"Nominal GDP = Σ(Current price × Current quantity)\nReal GDP = Σ(Base-year price × Current quantity)\nGDP deflator = Nominal GDP ÷ Real GDP × 100\nReal GDP = Nominal GDP ÷ GDP deflator × 100","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.6 NOMINAL AND REAL GDP"},{"title":"GDP and welfare: why output is an incomplete measure","body":"A rise in real GDP can expand available goods and services, but it does not ensure an equal rise in welfare. The textbook highlights distribution of income, composition of GDP, non-monetary transactions and externalities. Unequal distribution can leave most people without gains; more military goods may not improve consumption; unpaid household work is omitted; pollution imposes an unpriced cost. Positive externalities are benefits not paid for; negative externalities are costs not compensated.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.7 GDP AND WELFARE"},{"title":"Special cases of residents and territory","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Normal Residents of a Country · Components of Domestic Territory","body":"Normal Residents: citizens of another country can be residents here when their centre of economic interest is here; officials and diplomats serving abroad remain residents of their home country; border workers are residents where they live. Domestic Territory: include a country’s embassies, consulates and military establishments abroad, ships and aircraft operated by its residents between countries, and fishing vessels and floating platforms operated by residents in international waters under the accounting conventions. Exclude foreign embassies and international organisations located inside its political borders. Residence and territory are separate tests.","kind":"Ability Zone","formula":""}],"subject":"macro","number":"3"},{"id":"4","title":"Measurement of National Income","intro":"Value added is value of output minus intermediate consumption.","lessons":[{"title":"Value added method: measure production once","body":"Value added is value of output minus intermediate consumption. Value of output equals sales plus change in stock, with own-account production added where applicable. Sum GVA at market price across producing units to obtain GDPMP. Deduct depreciation and NIT, then add NFIA for national income. Alternatively, sum sectoral NVAFC and add NFIA.","formula":"Value of output = Sales + Closing stock − Opening stock\nGVA MP = Value of output − Intermediate consumption\nNNPFC = ΣGVA MP − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.2 VALUE ADDED METHOD (OR PRODUCT METHOD)"},{"title":"Double counting and value-added precautions","body":"Double counting means counting the value of the same output more than once. Avoid it by counting final goods only or summing value added at each stage. If wheat sells for 100, flour for 160 and bread for 250, total value added is 100 + 60 + 90 = 250, not 510. Include own-account construction and imputed rent of owner-occupied houses. Exclude resale value of second-hand goods, but include the current broker’s service. Do not count intermediate goods separately from the final product.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.2.2 Precautions · 4.2.3 Problem of Double Counting"},{"title":"Income method: compensation, surplus and mixed income","body":"Income method measures domestic income as the sum total of factor incomes generated within domestic territory. Compensation of employees includes wages and salaries in cash and kind and employers’ social-security contributions. Operating surplus comprises rent and royalty, interest and profit. Profit includes corporate tax, dividends and retained earnings; these are components, so do not add them again if total profit is given. Mixed income of the self-employed combines labour and property income that cannot be separated.","formula":"NDPFC = Compensation of employees + Operating surplus + Mixed income\nOperating surplus = Rent and royalty + Interest + Profit\nNational income = NDPFC + NFIA","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.3 INCOME METHOD"},{"title":"Income-method precautions","body":"Include only incomes earned from current productive services. Exclude transfer receipts, capital gains and windfall gains. Exclude interest on consumption loans and interest on public debt when it represents a transfer rather than payment for a productive service, following the textbook treatment. Include imputed rent and production for self-consumption where measured. Do not count sale proceeds of shares or second-hand goods as factor income; the current commission or brokerage is a service income.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.3.2 Precautions Regarding Income Method"},{"title":"Expenditure method and its precautions","body":"Expenditure method measures final expenditure on domestically produced output. Add private final consumption expenditure, government final consumption expenditure, gross domestic capital formation and net exports. Gross domestic capital formation includes fixed capital formation and change in stocks. Exclude financial-asset purchases, transfers and second-hand purchases; include current brokerage. Subtract imports because imported content may already be included in consumption or investment. Do not add change in stock twice if gross domestic capital formation already includes it.","formula":"GDPMP = PFCE + GFCE + GDCF + (X − M)\nNNPFC = PFCE + GFCE + GDCF + (X − M) − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.4 EXPENDITURE METHOD"},{"title":"Reconciliation and treatment of tricky items","body":"The three methods measure the same current production from different sides: value added creates factor income, and income finances final expenditure. In an exam, classify each item before calculating. Government services are counted even when provided free; employer provident-fund contributions belong in compensation. Scholarships and welfare pensions are transfers. Foreign factor earnings require a residence/territory check; the purchase of a newly produced house is investment. Read the supplied transaction table for item-by-item domestic and national treatment.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.5 RECONCILIATION OF THREE METHODS · 4.6 TREATMENT OF DIFFERENT ITEMS"},{"title":"Steps under the three measurement methods","textbookHeading":"4.2.1 Steps · 4.3.1 Steps · 4.4.1 Steps","body":"Value Added Method: identify and classify producing units; estimate each unit’s output and intermediate consumption; obtain sectoral value added; aggregate and make depreciation, tax and NFIA adjustments to national income. Income Method: identify producing enterprises; classify factor incomes into compensation, operating surplus and mixed income; sum them to obtain domestic income; add NFIA. Expenditure Method: identify final expenditure by sectors; classify consumption, investment and net exports; sum to GDPMP; convert to NNPFC. Keep the starting aggregate clear because the adjustments depend on whether it is gross/net and market price/factor cost.","kind":"Exam points","formula":""}],"subject":"macro","number":"4"},{"id":"5","title":"Money","intro":"Money is generally defined as a thing that is commonly accepted as a medium of exchange.","lessons":[{"title":"Money, barter and the evolution of money","body":"Money is generally defined as a thing that is commonly accepted as a medium of exchange. Barter exchanges goods directly for goods. Its difficulties include lack of double coincidence of wants, lack of a common measure of value, difficulty storing wealth and difficulty making deferred payments. Money separates selling from buying: the seller need not want the precise good the buyer offers. Evolution moves through commodity money, metallic money, paper money and bank/credit money.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"5.1 MEANING AND EVOLUTION OF MONEY"},{"title":"Fiat, fiduciary and legal-tender money","body":"Fiat money is money issued by authority of the government. Fiduciary money is accepted on trust between payer and payee. Legal tender must be accepted in discharge of debts within the applicable legal conditions; a cheque depends on acceptance and is not legal tender. Full-bodied money has face value equal to intrinsic commodity value; credit money has face value greater than intrinsic value. Follow the textbook distinctions, not the everyday use of the word “cash”.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"5.2 FORMS OF MONEY"},{"title":"Primary and secondary functions of money","body":"The primary functions are medium of exchange and measure of value (unit of account). Secondary functions are standard of deferred payments, store of value and transfer of value. Buying a book illustrates medium of exchange; quoting its price illustrates measure of value; paying a loan next year illustrates deferred payment; keeping purchasing power for later illustrates store of value. Inflation reduces the purchasing power stored in money.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"5.3 FUNCTIONS OF MONEY"},{"title":"Money supply and its components","body":"Money supply is the stock of money held by the public at a particular point of time. Interbank deposits and money held by the government/banking system are excluded from money held by the public. M1 comprises currency with the public, demand deposits with commercial banks and other deposits with the RBI. Demand deposits are withdrawable on demand. A cheque transfers a deposit; it is not an additional independent stock of money.","formula":"M1 = Currency with public + Demand deposits with banks + Other deposits with RBI","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"5.4 SUPPLY OF MONEY"},{"title":"Ability Zone: broader measures and liquidity","body":"The textbook also gives M2, M3 and M4 for reference. M2 adds post-office savings-bank deposits to M1. M3 adds net time deposits with commercial banks to M1 and is called aggregate monetary resources. M4 adds total post-office deposits, excluding National Savings Certificates, to M3. Liquidity means convertibility into cash: money is the most liquid asset. The textbook’s monetary-system notes discuss paper currency, limited/unlimited legal tender and the minimum reserve system; the original page is available to read exactly.","formula":"","kind":"Ability Zone","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Measures of Money Supply · Liquidity"},{"title":"Why money solves barter’s difficulties","textbookHeading":"5.1.3 Barter System of Exchange · 5.3 Functions of Money","body":"Lack of Double Coincidence of Wants: money is accepted as an intermediary, so you can sell to one person and buy from another. Lack of a Common Measure of Value: prices expressed in money make values comparable. Difficulty in Storing Wealth: money offers a convenient store, though inflation can erode it. Difficulty in Deferred Payments: future payments can be fixed in monetary units. Difficulty in Transfer of Value: purchasing power can be transferred without moving bulky goods. In theory answers, state the barter difficulty first and then the function that addresses it.","kind":"Exam points","formula":""}],"subject":"macro","number":"5"},{"id":"6","title":"Banking","intro":"Commercial banks accept deposits and advance loans with the objective of earning profit.","lessons":[{"title":"Commercial banks and their functions","body":"Commercial banks accept deposits and advance loans with the objective of earning profit. Deposits include current, savings and fixed deposits. Primary functions are accepting deposits and lending; other functions include agency services and general-utility services. Banks mobilise saving and create demand deposits through lending. A central bank is the apex institution regulating the monetary and financial system; its objective is growth with stability rather than commercial profit.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"6.1 COMMERCIAL BANKS"},{"title":"Credit creation: follow every round","body":"Suppose an initial cash deposit of 1,000 enters the banking system and the reserve ratio is 20%. The first bank keeps 200 and lends 800. If 800 is redeposited, the next bank keeps 160 and lends 640. Repeated lending and redepositing expand total deposits to 5,000. These results assume no cash leakage, no excess reserves and loans redeposited in the banking system. Total deposits include the initial deposit; additional credit excludes it.","formula":"Deposit multiplier = 1 ÷ Reserve ratio\nTotal deposits = Initial deposit ÷ Reserve ratio\nAdditional credit = Total deposits − Initial deposit","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"6.2 MONEY CREATION BY THE COMMERCIAL BANKS"},{"title":"Central bank: seven functions","body":"The textbook lists issuer of currency, banker to the government, bankers’ bank and supervisor, lender of last resort, custodian of foreign exchange, clearing-house function and control of credit. As government banker it holds accounts, receives money, makes payments and manages borrowing. As bankers’ bank it holds bank reserves and supports banks. Clearing settles interbank claims. Lender of last resort provides emergency assistance when normal sources cannot meet a bank’s needs.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK"},{"title":"Quantitative instruments: rates and open market operations","body":"Bank rate, repo rate and reverse repo rate influence the cost and placement of funds. The textbook distinguishes bank-rate lending without collateral from repo lending against securities under a repurchase agreement. Raising lending rates tends to raise borrowing cost and reduce credit demand; lowering them works in the opposite direction. RBI sales of securities absorb liquidity and reduce credit creation; RBI purchases release liquidity and increase it. These are textbook mechanisms, not a claim about today’s policy rates.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"6.4.1 Quantitative Instruments of Credit Control"},{"title":"Reserve ratios and qualitative instruments","body":"CRR is the percentage of deposits banks must keep with the RBI. SLR requires banks to maintain prescribed liquid assets such as cash, gold and approved securities. Higher reserve requirements constrain lending; lower requirements release lending capacity. Margin requirement is the difference between collateral value and loan value. Higher margins reduce permitted loans. Rationing fixes credit quotas, while moral suasion uses persuasion and pressure to influence bank lending. Quantitative instruments affect overall credit; qualitative instruments target its allocation.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"6.4.1 Quantitative Instruments · Qualitative Instruments"},{"title":"Ability Zone: when monetary tools succeed","body":"Repo-rate policy depends on banks’ reliance on RBI loans, responsiveness of credit demand, the wider structure of interest rates and availability of non-bank funds. Open market operations require a functioning securities market and an effective link between reserve changes and lending. Explaining a policy requires the transmission chain: policy change → bank funds or borrowing cost → credit → expenditure → aggregate demand.","formula":"","kind":"Ability Zone","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Success of Repo Rate · Success of Open Market Operations"},{"title":"Issuer of currency and banker to government","textbookHeading":"6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK · Functions","body":"Issuer of Currency: the central bank has the principal note-issuing authority; in India, the ₹1 note and coins are issued by the Government of India. Centralised issue promotes uniformity and control. Banker to Government: the RBI keeps government deposits, receives payments, makes disbursements, advances funds within the applicable arrangements and manages public debt. Financial adviser is a related role. Distinguish issuing authority from circulation: government-issued coins enter circulation through the monetary system.","kind":"Exam points","formula":""},{"title":"Bankers’ bank, lender of last resort and clearing house","textbookHeading":"6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK · Functions","body":"Bankers’ Bank and Supervisory Role: commercial banks maintain required cash balances with the central bank, which regulates their functioning. Lender of Last Resort: the central bank offers financial accommodation in emergencies when other sources are unavailable. Clearing House: it settles interbank claims by adjusting banks’ accounts and facilitates fund transfers. Custodian of Foreign Exchange: it manages the country’s foreign-exchange reserves and can intervene in the exchange market. Control of Credit: it regulates the cost and availability of credit to support growth with stability.","kind":"Exam points","formula":""}],"subject":"macro","number":"6"},{"id":"7","title":"Aggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concepts","intro":"Aggregate demand is the sum total of demand for all goods and services during an accounting year, measured as planned expenditure.","lessons":[{"title":"Aggregate demand and aggregate supply","body":"Aggregate demand is the sum total of demand for all goods and services during an accounting year, measured as planned expenditure. In a two-sector economy AD = C + I; a closed economy with government adds G; an open economy adds X − M. Aggregate supply refers to production planned by producers. In the textbook’s short-run model with constant prices and spare capacity, AS is identified with income/output and represented by the 45° line.","formula":"AD = C + I + G + (X − M)\nAS = Y = C + S","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"7.1 CONCEPT OF AD · 7.2 COMPONENTS OF AD · 7.3 CONCEPT OF AS"},{"title":"Consumption function: autonomous and induced consumption","body":"Consumption function is the functional relationship between consumption and income. Autonomous consumption is consumption when income is zero; induced consumption is the part that varies with income. In C = C̄ + bY, the vertical intercept is C̄ and the slope b is MPC. Consumption rises with income, but normally by less than the rise in income. A change in income moves along a given function; a change in autonomous consumption shifts it.","formula":"C = C̄ + bY\nMPC = ΔC ÷ ΔY\nInduced consumption = bY","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"7.4 CONSUMPTION FUNCTION"},{"title":"APC and MPC: totals versus increments","body":"Average propensity to consume is total consumption divided by total income; marginal propensity to consume is additional consumption divided by additional income. APC may exceed one when consumption exceeds income. MPC is the slope of the consumption curve; APC is not. With positive autonomous consumption and constant MPC below one, APC falls as income rises. At zero income APC is undefined.","formula":"APC = C ÷ Y\nMPC = ΔC ÷ ΔY","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"APC vs. MPC"},{"title":"Saving function and the break-even point","body":"Saving is income not consumed. Derive S by subtracting the entire consumption function from Y. The saving function has a negative intercept equal to autonomous consumption and a slope equal to MPS. At break-even income C = Y and S = 0: APC is one and APS is zero. Below it saving is negative; above it saving is positive. Break-even is not necessarily equilibrium, because equilibrium also depends on investment.","formula":"S = Y − C = −C̄ + (1 − b)Y\nBreak-even income = C̄ ÷ (1 − b)","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"SAVING FUNCTION · Break-even Point"},{"title":"APS, MPS and the two identities","body":"Average propensity to save is S/Y; marginal propensity to save is ΔS/ΔY. APS can be negative when households dissave. MPS is the slope of the saving function. Since income is split between consumption and saving, APC + APS = 1 and MPC + MPS = 1. Do not confuse ΔS (change in saving) with AS (aggregate supply): similar letters represent different concepts.","formula":"APS = S ÷ Y; MPS = ΔS ÷ ΔY\nAPC + APS = 1; MPC + MPS = 1","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"APS vs. MPS · Relationship between Propensity to Consume and Propensity to Save"},{"title":"Investment and deriving one function from the other","body":"In the simple model autonomous investment is independent of income, so its curve is horizontal. Induced investment responds to changes in income or activity. The expected profitability of investment and rate of interest influence investment decisions. To obtain C from S, use C = Y − S; to obtain S from C, use S = Y − C. The two slopes add to one and their vertical intercepts have equal magnitudes with opposite signs.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"INVESTMENT · ABILITY ZONE: Derivation of C-function from S-function"},{"title":"Ranges of APC, MPC, APS and MPS","textbookHeading":"Comparative Table of APC, MPC, APS and MPS · Critical Questions","body":"APC can exceed one when C exceeds Y; it equals one at break-even and is below one when saving is positive. APS is negative when saving is negative, zero at break-even and positive when saving is positive; with positive consumption it remains below one. MPC and MPS range from zero to one in the textbook model and add to one. At the endpoints, MPC = 1 implies MPS = 0 and all additional income is consumed; MPC = 0 implies MPS = 1 and all additional income is saved. Do not infer a marginal ratio from a single total-income observation.","kind":"Exam points","formula":""}],"subject":"macro","number":"7"},{"id":"8","title":"Short Run Equilibrium Output","intro":"The textbook’s short run assumes constant technology and a given capital stock; changes in employment change output.","lessons":[{"title":"Short run and equilibrium output","body":"The textbook’s short run assumes constant technology and a given capital stock; changes in employment change output. Equilibrium output is the level where intended production equals intended expenditure. Producers have no unplanned change in inventories and no incentive to change output. In the two-sector economy Y = C + I and Y = C + S, so the same equilibrium condition can be written S = I.","formula":"Y = AD = C + I\nC + S = C + I ⇒ S = I\nY* = (C̄ + I) ÷ (1 − MPC)","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"8.1 CONCEPT OF SHORT RUN · 8.2 CONCEPT OF EQUILIBRIUM OUTPUT"},{"title":"How inventories restore equilibrium","body":"When AD is below AS, goods remain unsold and actual inventories exceed desired inventories. Producers cut output and employment until planned saving equals planned investment. When AD exceeds AS, inventories fall unexpectedly and producers expand output, provided spare capacity exists. The direction is easy to reverse by mistake: excess supply causes inventory accumulation; excess demand causes inventory depletion.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Equilibrium GDP · Inventory Adjustment"},{"title":"Ex-ante versus ex-post saving and investment","body":"Ex-ante means planned; ex-post means realised. Planned saving and planned investment need not be equal away from equilibrium. Actual saving and actual investment are equal by accounting identity because realised investment includes unintended inventory changes. Equilibrium requires equality of planned S and planned I, not merely the ex-post identity.","formula":"","kind":"Purple-box concept","textbookHeading":"Ex-ante Saving and Ex-ante Investment"},{"title":"Effective demand and underemployment equilibrium","body":"Effective demand is aggregate demand at the equilibrium level of income/output. Equilibrium can occur below full employment: firms’ production plans can match spending plans while workers and productive capacity remain idle. An increase in autonomous expenditure shifts AD upward and raises equilibrium output in the model with spare capacity; a fall lowers it.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Effective Demand · Underemployment Equilibrium"},{"title":"Investment multiplier: size and derivation","body":"Investment multiplier is the ratio of change in income to change in investment. Starting with ΔY = ΔC + ΔI and ΔC = MPC × ΔY gives ΔY(1 − MPC) = ΔI. Higher MPC means more income is respent, so the multiplier is larger. Higher MPS means more income leaks into saving, so the multiplier is smaller. With MPC zero, k = 1; as MPC approaches one, k becomes very large.","formula":"k = ΔY ÷ ΔI = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ MPS\nΔY = k × ΔI","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"8.5 INVESTMENT MULTIPLIER"},{"title":"Multiplier mechanism, assumptions and reverse operation","body":"An investment injection is someone’s income. They consume a fraction, which becomes someone else’s income; successive rounds generate ΔI, MPC × ΔI, MPC² × ΔI and so on. Saving reduces each next round. The simple multiplier assumes constant MPC, available spare capacity, constant prices, and no additional leakages such as imports or taxes. A fall in investment works in reverse, producing a multiplied fall in income. The final increase in saving equals the initial increase in investment.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Multiplier Mechanism"},{"title":"Break-even income versus equilibrium income","textbookHeading":"Consumption Function · Equilibrium Output","body":"Break-even Income: consumption equals income, so saving is zero; use C = Y. Equilibrium Income: planned output equals planned expenditure, so saving equals investment; use Y = C + I or S = I. With positive autonomous investment and positive MPS, equilibrium income exceeds break-even income. The two coincide only when autonomous investment is zero in the simple model. If you are asked for dissaving at zero income, solve for autonomous consumption and report saving as its negative.","kind":"Exam points","formula":"Break-even Y = C̄/MPS\nEquilibrium Y = (C̄ + I)/MPS"}],"subject":"macro","number":"8"},{"id":"9","title":"Problem of Deficient Demand and Excess Demand","intro":"Full employment means those able and willing to work at the existing wage obtain work.","lessons":[{"title":"Full employment and unemployment","body":"Full employment means those able and willing to work at the existing wage obtain work. Voluntary unemployment reflects unwillingness to work at the existing wage; involuntary unemployment means willing workers cannot find jobs. The textbook also distinguishes natural unemployment: frictional unemployment during job transitions and structural unemployment caused by changing industries, skills or production techniques. Underemployment equilibrium has AD = AS with unutilised capacity.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"9.1 SOME CRITICAL CONCEPTS"},{"title":"Deficient demand and deflationary gap","body":"Deficient demand occurs when aggregate demand is less than the aggregate supply corresponding to full employment. The deflationary gap is the shortfall of AD from the level required to maintain full employment; measure it vertically at full-employment output. It reduces output and employment and creates involuntary unemployment. The gap in expenditure is not the same as the shortfall in equilibrium income: the multiplier links the two.","formula":"Deflationary gap = Yf − AD at Yf\nRequired increase in autonomous expenditure = (Yf − Y*) ÷ k","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"DEFICIENT DEMAND · Deflationary Gap"},{"title":"Excess demand and inflationary gap","body":"Excess demand occurs when AD exceeds the demand required to sustain full employment. The inflationary gap is the excess of AD over full-employment AS, measured at Yf. Once resources are fully employed, real output cannot expand in the simple model; excess spending raises the general price level. A calculated equilibrium above Yf is a signal of inflationary pressure, not evidence that the economy can actually produce beyond its capacity.","formula":"Inflationary gap = AD at Yf − Yf","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"EXCESS DEMAND · Inflationary Gap"},{"title":"Fiscal measures: expenditure, taxation and borrowing","body":"To correct deficient demand, raise government expenditure, reduce taxes, reduce public borrowing and increase borrowing from the central bank, following the textbook framework. These measures raise spending power or public expenditure. For excess demand, reverse the direction. Explain the chain: lower taxes → higher disposable income → more consumption → higher AD. Borrowing from the public can withdraw purchasing power; borrowing from the central bank can expand it.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Fiscal Policy"},{"title":"Monetary measures: cost and availability of credit","body":"For deficient demand, lower bank/repo/reverse-repo rates, lower CRR and SLR, buy securities, lower margins, relax credit rationing and encourage lending. For excess demand, raise rates and reserve requirements, sell securities, raise margins and restrict lending. Monetary policy is the central bank’s policy; fiscal policy is the government’s revenue and expenditure policy. Always connect the instrument to credit and then to aggregate demand.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Monetary Policy"}],"subject":"macro","number":"9"},{"id":"10","title":"Government Budget and the Economy","intro":"Government budget is a statement of expected receipts and expected expenditure of the government for the coming financial year, revealing its budgetary policy.","lessons":[{"title":"Government budget and its objectives","body":"Government budget is a statement of expected receipts and expected expenditure of the government for the coming financial year, revealing its budgetary policy. Objectives include GDP growth, allocation of resources, redistribution of income and wealth, economic stability, management of public enterprises and employment generation. Public infrastructure can encourage private investment; progressive taxation and social spending can reduce inequality. Fiscal discipline means managing public finances responsibly to avoid unsustainable deficits and debt.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"10.1 CONCEPT OF GOVERNMENT BUDGET · 10.2 OBJECTIVES"},{"title":"Revenue receipts versus capital receipts","body":"Revenue receipts neither create a liability nor reduce assets. They include tax revenue and non-tax revenue such as fees, fines, interest, profits/dividends and grants. Capital receipts create a liability or reduce assets: borrowing creates liabilities, while loan recovery and disinvestment reduce assets. Do not classify a receipt by size or regularity alone; use the liability/asset test.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"10.3.1 Budget Receipts"},{"title":"Direct taxes, indirect taxes and non-tax revenue","body":"Direct taxes have impact and incidence on the same person, such as income tax and corporation tax. Indirect taxes allow the burden to be shifted, such as GST. A fee is a payment for a specific government service; a fine penalises an offence. A tax is compulsory and does not promise a direct equivalent service. Progressive taxes take a larger proportion as income rises; proportional taxes keep the same proportion; regressive burdens fall relatively more heavily on lower incomes.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Tax Revenue · Non-tax Revenue"},{"title":"Revenue expenditure versus capital expenditure","body":"Revenue expenditure neither creates assets nor reduces liabilities. Salaries, pensions, subsidies and interest payments are examples. Capital expenditure creates assets or reduces liabilities: construction of a highway, purchase of equipment, lending by government and repayment of loan principal. Repayment of principal is capital expenditure; interest is revenue expenditure. Grants to states remain revenue expenditure for the granting government even if the recipient uses them to build assets, following the textbook convention.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"10.3.2 Budget Expenditure"},{"title":"Revenue deficit, fiscal deficit and primary deficit","body":"Revenue deficit is the excess of revenue expenditure over revenue receipts. Fiscal deficit is total expenditure minus total receipts excluding borrowings; it measures borrowing requirements. Primary deficit deducts interest payments from fiscal deficit and indicates the current borrowing requirement excluding past-debt interest. A zero revenue deficit does not guarantee zero fiscal deficit. A zero primary deficit means borrowing covers interest payments.","formula":"Revenue deficit = Revenue expenditure − Revenue receipts\nFiscal deficit = Total expenditure − Revenue receipts − Non-debt capital receipts\nPrimary deficit = Fiscal deficit − Interest payments","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"10.4 BUDGET DEFICIT: REVENUE DEFICIT, FISCAL DEFICIT AND PRIMARY DEFICIT"},{"title":"Implications, balanced and unbalanced budgets","body":"A revenue deficit indicates government dissaving: borrowing may finance current consumption rather than asset creation. Fiscal deficits may raise debt and interest burdens and crowd out private borrowing; financing expenditure can also support demand and productive capacity, depending on its use. A balanced budget has receipts equal to expenditure; a surplus budget has receipts above expenditure and can restrain inflation; a deficit budget can support demand in recession. Consider both the size and composition of expenditure.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"10.5 BALANCED AND UNBALANCED BUDGET"},{"title":"Ability Zone: public, private, common and club goods","body":"Public goods are non-rival and non-excludable, such as national defence. Private goods are rival and excludable. Common goods are rival but non-excludable, such as fish in an open pond. Club goods are excludable but non-rival until congestion, such as a subscription service. Rivalry means one person’s use reduces availability to others; excludability means users can be denied access. Public expenditure supports infrastructure, redistribution, stability and an investment-friendly environment.","formula":"","kind":"Ability Zone","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Public Goods, Private Goods, Common Goods and Club Goods"},{"title":"Budget objectives: the headings to reproduce","textbookHeading":"10.2 OBJECTIVES OF GOVERNMENT BUDGET","body":"GDP Growth: create infrastructure and induce private investment through suitable incentives. Allocation of Resources: influence production through taxes, subsidies and public provision. Provision of Public Goods: provide collective services that markets may undersupply. Redistribution of Income and Wealth: use progressive taxation and welfare spending. Balanced Regional Growth: direct infrastructure and incentives to less-developed regions. Employment Opportunities: expand activity and labour demand. Economic Stability: raise spending during deficient demand and restrain it during excess demand. Select and explain the number of objectives requested, using these headings.","kind":"Exam points","formula":""},{"title":"Free-rider problem","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Free Rider Problem","body":"A free rider enjoys the benefits of goods and services without contributing the full or partial cost of providing them. Non-excludability makes it difficult to withhold benefits from non-payers. Private providers may be unable to collect enough revenue to cover costs, so the market can fail to provide a needed public good. National defence and street lighting illustrate why public provision can be necessary.","kind":"Ability Zone","formula":""},{"title":"Tax rates and tax burden are different","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Progressive and Regressive Nature of Taxation is Different from Progressive and Regressive Rates of Taxation","body":"Progressive Rate: the tax rate rises with income. Regressive Rate: the tax rate falls with income. Proportionate Rate: the tax rate stays constant as income changes. Progressive Nature: the real burden is greater on the rich. Regressive Nature: the real burden is greater on the poor. The textbook stresses that a proportionate tax can still have a regressive real burden. When answering, specify whether you mean the numerical rate or the economic burden.","kind":"Ability Zone","formula":""},{"title":"Does a balanced budget leave AD unchanged?","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Does Balanced Budget Leave Aggregate Demand Unaffected in the Economy?","body":"No. Equal tax collection and government expenditure do not necessarily have equal first-round effects on AD. Government expenditure enters AD in full; a tax increase reduces disposable income, and consumption falls by MPC times the tax rise. If G and taxes each rise by ₹100 and MPC is 0.5, expenditure raises AD by ₹100 while the initial consumption loss is ₹50, giving a net initial rise of ₹50. Under the simple lump-sum-tax model, later rounds imply a balanced-budget multiplier of one. Distinguish the initial AD change from the final income change.","kind":"Ability Zone","formula":"Initial ΔAD = ΔG − MPC × ΔT\nIf ΔG = ΔT: initial ΔAD = (1 − MPC)ΔG"}],"subject":"macro","number":"10"},{"id":"11","title":"Foreign Exchange Rate","intro":"Foreign exchange refers to foreign currency.","lessons":[{"title":"Foreign exchange and the quotation convention","body":"Foreign exchange refers to foreign currency. Foreign exchange rate is the price paid in domestic currency for a unit of foreign currency. Throughout these lessons, quote the rate as ₹ per US dollar. A rise from ₹80/$ to ₹84/$ means the rupee buys fewer dollars and has depreciated; a fall to ₹76/$ means rupee appreciation. Always identify which currency is in the numerator before describing a movement.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"11.1 FOREIGN EXCHANGE AND FOREIGN EXCHANGE RATE"},{"title":"Demand, supply and flexible exchange-rate determination","body":"Flexible exchange rate is determined by demand and supply in the foreign exchange market. Demand for foreign exchange comes from imports, overseas investment, foreign travel, loan repayment, income payments, grants and speculation. Supply comes from exports, foreign investment, inward loans, tourism receipts, income receipts, grants and remittances. With ₹/$ on the vertical axis, demand slopes downward and supply upward; their intersection is the equilibrium rate. A higher exchange rate makes foreign goods costlier in rupees and normally reduces import-related foreign-exchange demand.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"11.2.1 Flexible Exchange Rate"},{"title":"Movements versus shifts, appreciation versus depreciation","body":"A change in the exchange rate causes movement along a given demand or supply curve. A change in imports, investment preferences, income or foreign demand shifts a curve. Greater demand for dollars shifts demand right, raising ₹/$ and depreciating the rupee. Greater supply of dollars shifts supply right, lowering ₹/$ and appreciating the rupee. Depreciation can make exports cheaper for foreigners and imports costlier for residents; actual quantity responses depend on demand conditions.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Appreciation and Depreciation of Domestic Currency"},{"title":"Fixed rate, devaluation and revaluation","body":"Fixed exchange rate is set and maintained by the monetary authority. An official rise in ₹/$ is devaluation of the rupee; an official fall is revaluation. Depreciation/appreciation describe market-driven changes under a flexible system. To defend a fixed rate when demand for dollars exceeds supply, the authority sells foreign currency from reserves; when dollar supply exceeds demand it buys foreign currency. An overvalued domestic currency tends to create excess demand for foreign currency at the official rate.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"11.2.2 Fixed Exchange Rate"},{"title":"Managed floating and merits/demerits","body":"Managed floating combines market determination with occasional central-bank intervention to limit undesirable movements; the textbook also calls it dirty floating. Fixed rates reduce exchange uncertainty and can support trade but require reserves and may misallocate resources. Flexible rates need less reserve defence and adjust through markets but create uncertainty and exposure to external shocks. The original comparison covers the full list of merits and demerits.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"11.3 MANAGED FLOATING · 11.4 MERITS AND DEMERITS"},{"title":"Foreign exchange market: transfer, credit and hedging","body":"The foreign exchange market is the market for national currencies. The transfer function moves purchasing power between countries. The credit function supports trade through financing arrangements. Hedging reduces exchange-rate risk. Spot transactions exchange currency for current delivery; forward transactions agree today on a rate for delivery at a future date. A forward contract fixes an exchange rate; it does not guarantee that the future spot rate will equal that rate.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"11.7 FOREIGN EXCHANGE MARKET"},{"title":"Fixed versus flexible rates: named merits and demerits","textbookHeading":"11.4 MERITS AND DEMERITS OF FIXED AND FLEXIBLE EXCHANGE RATE","body":"Fixed Rate—Merits: market stability and mobility of capital; growth of international trade; stable macroeconomic policies; devaluation as a policy tool; a check on inflation. Fixed Rate—Demerits: large reserve requirements; inefficient allocation of resources; limited forex market; discouraged venture capital; possible divergence from equilibrium. Flexible Rate—Merits: fewer reserves required for rate defence; market allocation of resources; larger forex market; international mobility of liquidity; support for venture capital. Flexible Rate—Demerits: weak-currency marginalisation; market uncertainty; difficulty maintaining stable monetary policy; difficulty with bilateral trade agreements; external shocks. These are the textbook’s headings: explain the relevant mechanism instead of only listing them.","kind":"Exam points","formula":""}],"subject":"macro","number":"11"},{"id":"12","title":"Balance of Payments","intro":"Balance of payments is a statement of accounts showing all monetary transactions of a country with the rest of the world during a period of time, generally one year.","lessons":[{"title":"Balance of payments: account and recording rule","body":"Balance of payments is a statement of accounts showing all monetary transactions of a country with the rest of the world during a period of time, generally one year. It records goods, services, income, transfers and asset-related transactions between residents and non-residents. Foreign-exchange receipts are credit items; payments are debit items. The textbook uses current, capital and official-reserves accounts. Keep its school-level account terminology when answering.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"12.1 BoP (or BoP ACCOUNTS)"},{"title":"Current account, trade balance and invisibles","body":"Current account records exports and imports of goods and services, factor income and current transfers. Goods are visible trade or merchandise; services, factor incomes and transfers are invisibles. Balance of trade covers goods only. A gift from abroad is a current transfer; wages for a service are factor income. The supplied purple box treats direct tourist purchases as invisible trade: preserve that convention for this textbook.","formula":"Trade balance = Goods exports − Goods imports\nCurrent-account balance = Trade balance + Net services + Net factor income + Net current transfers","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"12.2.1 Current Account"},{"title":"Capital account and asset/liability changes","body":"Capital account records transactions that change a country’s assets or liabilities in relation to the rest of the world. The textbook includes borrowing, foreign investment, NRI deposits, banking capital and short-term debt. Inward FDI or an external loan is a credit; an outward investment or repayment of foreign loan principal is a debit. Interest on a foreign loan belongs in the current account; the principal belongs in the capital account. FDI involves a lasting productive interest/control; portfolio investment purchases financial securities.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"12.2.2 Capital Account"},{"title":"Official reserves, deficit and surplus","body":"If autonomous foreign-exchange payments exceed autonomous receipts, there is a BoP deficit, financed by accommodating transactions such as a drawdown of reserves or official borrowing. A surplus can increase official reserves. A decrease in reserve assets finances a shortfall; an increase uses foreign exchange. Follow the sign convention explicitly stated in a numerical instead of confusing a positive stock increase with a credit entry.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"12.2.3 Official Reserves Account"},{"title":"Autonomous versus accommodating transactions","body":"Autonomous transactions are undertaken for independent economic motives such as profit, rather than to correct a BoP imbalance; they are above-the-line items. Accommodating transactions are undertaken to finance or correct the imbalance and are below the line. A foreign loan may be autonomous when raised commercially or accommodating when arranged specifically to finance a deficit. Classification depends on purpose, not merely the type of transaction.","formula":"","kind":"Purple-box concept","textbookHeading":"12.3.1 Autonomous and Accommodating Items of BoP Account"},{"title":"Accounting balance versus operational balance","body":"BoP balances in the accounting sense because every transaction has a counterpart and financing entries close the accounts. This does not mean autonomous receipts equal autonomous payments or that the economy has no external problem. A current-account deficit financed by foreign borrowing is consistent with accounting balance and increasing indebtedness. Read the Ability Zone worked table to see a current-account deficit offset by capital inflows.","formula":"","kind":"Ability Zone","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Balance of Payments: Accounting Sense and Operational Sense"},{"title":"Causes and significance of disequilibrium","body":"Economic causes include large development imports, business cycles, domestic inflation, import substitution and changes in trading partners’ costs. Political instability can discourage foreign investment; policy changes can increase imports. Social causes include changes in tastes and cross-border preferences. BoP data show external strengths, weaknesses, debt and reserve pressures and help formulate policy. A trade deficit alone does not tell you the whole BoP position.","formula":"","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"12.3.2 Significance of BoP Accounts · Causes of Disequilibrium"}],"subject":"macro","number":"12"},{"id":"i1","number":"1","subject":"ied","title":"Indian Economy on the Eve of Independence","intro":"","lessons":[{"title":"Before British rule and the colonial transformation","body":"Before British rule: strong handicrafts, especially textiles, alongside a mainly agricultural economy.\n\nColonial policy: turn India into a raw-material supplier and a market for British manufactured goods.\n\nResult: slow growth, low productivity, weak industry and widespread poverty.\n\nThe economy was organised around British needs, which explains the weaknesses in every sector.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.1 Overall Features of Indian Economy","points":["Before British rule: strong handicrafts, especially textiles, alongside a mainly agricultural economy.","Colonial policy: turn India into a raw-material supplier and a market for British manufactured goods.","Result: slow growth, low productivity, weak industry and widespread poverty."],"takeaway":"The economy was organised around British needs, which explains the weaknesses in every sector."},{"title":"Why agriculture stagnated","body":"Exploitative land settlement: high rent and insecure tenure left cultivators with little money or reason to improve land.\n\nLow investment: poor irrigation, old techniques and limited inputs kept yields low.\n\nSmall, fragmented holdings: scattered plots made efficient cultivation difficult.\n\nMost people depended on farming, but dependence is not the same as productivity.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.2 Agricultural Sector on the Eve of Independence","points":["Exploitative land settlement: high rent and insecure tenure left cultivators with little money or reason to improve land.","Low investment: poor irrigation, old techniques and limited inputs kept yields low.","Small, fragmented holdings: scattered plots made efficient cultivation difficult."],"takeaway":"Most people depended on farming, but dependence is not the same as productivity."},{"title":"Commercialisation, subsistence and vulnerability","body":"Subsistence farming: grow mainly for the family’s own use.\n\nCommercial farming: grow mainly for sale, including colonial cash crops such as cotton and jute.\n\nThe problem: pressure to grow cash crops could reduce food cultivation without improving farmers’ incomes.\n\nCommercialisation describes where the crop goes; it does not guarantee that the farmer benefits.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.2 Agricultural Sector · Commercialisation of Agriculture","points":["Subsistence farming: grow mainly for the family’s own use.","Commercial farming: grow mainly for sale, including colonial cash crops such as cotton and jute.","The problem: pressure to grow cash crops could reduce food cultivation without improving farmers’ incomes."],"takeaway":"Commercialisation describes where the crop goes; it does not guarantee that the farmer benefits."},{"title":"Decline of handicrafts: causes and consequences","body":"British machine-made imports competed with Indian handmade goods.\n\nDiscriminatory duties weakened Indian goods in foreign markets while favouring British goods in India.\n\nLoss of royal patronage reduced demand for specialised crafts.\n\nDisplaced artisans moved towards agriculture because modern industry could not absorb them.\n\nDe-industrialisation = handicrafts decline without enough modern industry to replace them.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.3.1 Systematic De-industrialisation","points":["British machine-made imports competed with Indian handmade goods.","Discriminatory duties weakened Indian goods in foreign markets while favouring British goods in India.","Loss of royal patronage reduced demand for specialised crafts.","Displaced artisans moved towards agriculture because modern industry could not absorb them."],"takeaway":"De-industrialisation = handicrafts decline without enough modern industry to replace them."},{"title":"A limited modern industrial base","body":"Cotton mills developed mainly in western India; jute mills were concentrated in Bengal.\n\nIron and steel emerged later, including TISCO in 1907.\n\nCapital-goods industries remained weak, limiting the ability to make machinery.\n\nModern industry grew too slowly to employ displaced artisans.\n\nA few successful industries existed, but the overall industrial base remained narrow.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.3.2 Growth of Modern Industry during 1850–1947","points":["Cotton mills developed mainly in western India; jute mills were concentrated in Bengal.","Iron and steel emerged later, including TISCO in 1907.","Capital-goods industries remained weak, limiting the ability to make machinery.","Modern industry grew too slowly to employ displaced artisans."],"takeaway":"A few successful industries existed, but the overall industrial base remained narrow."},{"title":"Foreign trade and the drain of wealth","body":"Trade pattern: export raw materials; import manufactured goods.\n\nBritish control: trade served Britain’s factories and markets; the Suez Canal strengthened these links.\n\nDrain of wealth: export earnings financed British administration, wars and payments abroad.\n\nExport surplus means exports exceed imports. It helped India only if the resulting resources stayed and supported development.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.4 Foreign Trade on the Eve of Independence","points":["Trade pattern: export raw materials; import manufactured goods.","British control: trade served Britain’s factories and markets; the Suez Canal strengthened these links.","Drain of wealth: export earnings financed British administration, wars and payments abroad."],"takeaway":"Export surplus means exports exceed imports. It helped India only if the resulting resources stayed and supported development."},{"title":"Population conditions and the year 1921","body":"Poor health: high death and infant-mortality rates, low life expectancy, disease and inadequate sanitation.\n\nLow literacy: education reached few people, especially women.\n\n1921: the Year of the Great Divide—population growth became more consistently positive afterwards.\n\nHigh birth and death rates initially kept population growth modest; falling death rates later widened the gap.","formula":"Historical indicators in your textbook: birth rate ≈48 per 1,000; death rate ≈40 per 1,000; infant mortality ≈218 per 1,000 live births; life expectancy ≈32 years; literacy below 16%; female literacy ≈7%.\nInfant mortality concerns deaths below age one per 1,000 live births—not per 1,000 total people.","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.5 Demographic Profile","points":["Poor health: high death and infant-mortality rates, low life expectancy, disease and inadequate sanitation.","Low literacy: education reached few people, especially women.","1921: the Year of the Great Divide—population growth became more consistently positive afterwards."],"takeaway":"High birth and death rates initially kept population growth modest; falling death rates later widened the gap."},{"title":"Occupational imbalance","body":"Occupational structure = how workers are distributed across sectors.\n\nAbout 70–75% worked in the primary sector; industry employed only about 10% in the textbook’s colonial summary.\n\nWeak non-farm opportunities pushed extra workers onto land, lowering average output per worker.\n\nEmployment share tells you where people work; GDP share tells you where output is produced.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.6 Occupational Structure","points":["Occupational structure = how workers are distributed across sectors.","About 70–75% worked in the primary sector; industry employed only about 10% in the textbook’s colonial summary.","Weak non-farm opportunities pushed extra workers onto land, lowering average output per worker."],"takeaway":"Employment share tells you where people work; GDP share tells you where output is produced."},{"title":"Railways and other infrastructure: purpose and effects","body":"Colonial purpose: move raw materials, sell British goods, transport troops and administer India.\n\nInfrastructure built: railways, ports, post and telegraph; rural roads and social facilities remained inadequate.\n\nSide effects: better transport, communication and links between regions.\n\nA balanced answer recognises the benefits while explaining that colonial interests determined the priorities.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.7 Infrastructure · 1.7.1 Positive Impact","points":["Colonial purpose: move raw materials, sell British goods, transport troops and administer India.","Infrastructure built: railways, ports, post and telegraph; rural roads and social facilities remained inadequate.","Side effects: better transport, communication and links between regions."],"takeaway":"A balanced answer recognises the benefits while explaining that colonial interests determined the priorities."}]},{"id":"i2","number":"2","subject":"ied","title":"Indian Economy 1950–1990","intro":"","lessons":[{"title":"Capitalist, socialist and mixed economies","body":"Capitalist economy: private ownership; markets and profit guide production.\n\nSocialist economy: government ownership and planning guide resource use.\n\nMixed economy: public and private sectors coexist; India used public leadership with regulated private enterprise.\n\nIndia wanted industrial growth while also pursuing social welfare and equity.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.1 Economic Systems","points":["Capitalist economy: private ownership; markets and profit guide production.","Socialist economy: government ownership and planning guide resource use.","Mixed economy: public and private sectors coexist; India used public leadership with regulated private enterprise."],"takeaway":"India wanted industrial growth while also pursuing social welfare and equity."},{"title":"Economic planning and Five Year Plans","body":"Planning = deliberately allocating resources towards stated goals over a fixed period.\n\nIndia’s First Five Year Plan began in 1951.\n\nPlans coordinated investment in agriculture, industry and infrastructure when resources were scarce.\n\nCommon goals stayed broadly similar, but the priority given to each sector could change between plans.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.2.1 Concept of Economic Planning","points":["Planning = deliberately allocating resources towards stated goals over a fixed period.","India’s First Five Year Plan began in 1951.","Plans coordinated investment in agriculture, industry and infrastructure when resources were scarce."],"takeaway":"Common goals stayed broadly similar, but the priority given to each sector could change between plans."},{"title":"Growth, modernisation, self-reliance and equity","body":"Growth: increase productive capacity and output.\n\nModernisation: adopt better technology and a progressive social outlook, including women’s participation.\n\nSelf-reliance: reduce dependence on foreign resources for essential needs.\n\nEquity: ensure benefits reach all sections and basic needs are met.\n\nMore GDP does not automatically mean fairer distribution; growth and equity are separate goals.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.2.2 Common Goals of Five Year Plans","points":["Growth: increase productive capacity and output.","Modernisation: adopt better technology and a progressive social outlook, including women’s participation.","Self-reliance: reduce dependence on foreign resources for essential needs.","Equity: ensure benefits reach all sections and basic needs are met."],"takeaway":"More GDP does not automatically mean fairer distribution; growth and equity are separate goals."},{"title":"Land reforms: changing agrarian relations","body":"Abolition of intermediaries: remove rent-collecting middlemen and connect cultivators directly with the state.\n\nLand ceiling: limit holdings and redistribute surplus land.\n\nConsolidation: combine scattered plots; cooperative farming: pool resources.\n\nLimitation: loopholes, litigation and weak implementation reduced the gains.\n\nInstitutional reforms change ownership and tenure; technical reforms change production methods.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.3.2 Land Reforms [Institutional Reforms]","points":["Abolition of intermediaries: remove rent-collecting middlemen and connect cultivators directly with the state.","Land ceiling: limit holdings and redistribute surplus land.","Consolidation: combine scattered plots; cooperative farming: pool resources.","Limitation: loopholes, litigation and weak implementation reduced the gains."],"takeaway":"Institutional reforms change ownership and tenure; technical reforms change production methods."},{"title":"The Green Revolution package","body":"Green Revolution = a sharp rise in agricultural output through HYV technology.\n\nThe package: HYV seeds + irrigation + fertilisers + pesticides + credit.\n\nEarly gains concentrated in wheat and well-irrigated regions; later gains spread to more crops and areas.\n\nSeeds alone cannot deliver the promised yield when irrigation and supporting inputs are missing.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.3.3 Technical Reforms: HYV Technology and Green Revolution","points":["Green Revolution = a sharp rise in agricultural output through HYV technology.","The package: HYV seeds + irrigation + fertilisers + pesticides + credit.","Early gains concentrated in wheat and well-irrigated regions; later gains spread to more crops and areas."],"takeaway":"Seeds alone cannot deliver the promised yield when irrigation and supporting inputs are missing."},{"title":"Green Revolution: gains","body":"Higher yields raised foodgrain production and reduced dependence on imports.\n\nMore marketable surplus supplied people outside agriculture.\n\nBetter food supplies supported buffer stocks and price stability.\n\nHigher farm incomes increased demand for industrial goods.\n\nMarketable surplus = production left for sale after the farmer’s own requirements.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.3.5 Gains of Green Revolution","points":["Higher yields raised foodgrain production and reduced dependence on imports.","More marketable surplus supplied people outside agriculture.","Better food supplies supported buffer stocks and price stability.","Higher farm incomes increased demand for industrial goods."],"takeaway":"Marketable surplus = production left for sale after the farmer’s own requirements."},{"title":"Green Revolution: limitations","body":"Crop imbalance: early gains mainly benefited wheat and rice.\n\nRegional imbalance: well-irrigated regions advanced faster.\n\nIncome inequality: larger farmers could afford the input package more easily.\n\nEnvironmental damage: excessive chemicals and groundwater use harmed soil and water.\n\nEvaluate both the increase in food output and how its gains and costs were distributed.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.3.6 Limitations of Green Revolution","points":["Crop imbalance: early gains mainly benefited wheat and rice.","Regional imbalance: well-irrigated regions advanced faster.","Income inequality: larger farmers could afford the input package more easily.","Environmental damage: excessive chemicals and groundwater use harmed soil and water."],"takeaway":"Evaluate both the increase in food output and how its gains and costs were distributed."},{"title":"The subsidy debate","body":"In favour: subsidies make inputs affordable, encourage new technology and support small farmers.\n\nAgainst: benefits may favour large farmers; government spending rises; cheap inputs can encourage waste.\n\nBetter design: target farmers who need support and discourage excessive input use.\n\nThe issue is who receives the subsidy and what behaviour it encourages.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.3.7 Agricultural Subsidies: Vice or Virtue?","points":["In favour: subsidies make inputs affordable, encourage new technology and support small farmers.","Against: benefits may favour large farmers; government spending rises; cheap inputs can encourage waste.","Better design: target farmers who need support and discourage excessive input use."],"takeaway":"The issue is who receives the subsidy and what behaviour it encourages."},{"title":"Why the public sector led industrialisation","body":"Basic and heavy industries needed large investments and long waiting periods before returns.\n\nPrivate firms often lacked the capital or incentive to undertake them.\n\nThe public sector supplied infrastructure, strategic industries and investment in backward regions.\n\nPublic-sector leadership supported a mixed economy; it did not mean private firms disappeared.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.4.1 Public and Private Sectors in Indian Industrial Development","points":["Basic and heavy industries needed large investments and long waiting periods before returns.","Private firms often lacked the capital or incentive to undertake them.","The public sector supplied infrastructure, strategic industries and investment in backward regions."],"takeaway":"Public-sector leadership supported a mixed economy; it did not mean private firms disappeared."},{"title":"IPR 1956 and industrial licensing","body":"IPR 1956: one group reserved for the state; a second with state leadership and private participation; the remainder open to regulated private enterprise.\n\nIndustrial licensing controlled new factories and expansion.\n\nPurpose: direct scarce resources and promote regional balance; drawback: too many controls weakened competition.\n\nRemember the three groups by the degree of state involvement, rather than as an unexplained list.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.4.2 Industrial Policy Resolution, 1956","points":["IPR 1956: one group reserved for the state; a second with state leadership and private participation; the remainder open to regulated private enterprise.","Industrial licensing controlled new factories and expansion.","Purpose: direct scarce resources and promote regional balance; drawback: too many controls weakened competition."],"takeaway":"Remember the three groups by the degree of state involvement, rather than as an unexplained list."},{"title":"Small-scale industries and the Karve Committee","body":"Karve Committee, 1955: emphasised small industries’ role in rural development.\n\nWhy protect them? They create jobs with relatively little capital and spread production across regions.\n\nSupport: reserved products, concessions and credit.\n\nWeaknesses: limited finance, outdated technology and marketing difficulties.\n\nTheir main advantage was labour-intensive, decentralised growth.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.4.3 Small-Scale Industry","points":["Karve Committee, 1955: emphasised small industries’ role in rural development.","Why protect them? They create jobs with relatively little capital and spread production across regions.","Support: reserved products, concessions and credit.","Weaknesses: limited finance, outdated technology and marketing difficulties."],"takeaway":"Their main advantage was labour-intensive, decentralised growth."},{"title":"Import substitution, tariffs and quotas","body":"Import substitution = make domestically what was previously imported.\n\nTariff: tax imports, making them costlier. Quota: limit their quantity.\n\nBenefit: protect young domestic industries and conserve foreign exchange.\n\nCost: prolonged protection weakens pressure to improve quality and efficiency.\n\nProtection can help an industry get started; permanent shelter can make it complacent.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.5 Trade Policy: Import Substitution","points":["Import substitution = make domestically what was previously imported.","Tariff: tax imports, making them costlier. Quota: limit their quantity.","Benefit: protect young domestic industries and conserve foreign exchange.","Cost: prolonged protection weakens pressure to improve quality and efficiency."],"takeaway":"Protection can help an industry get started; permanent shelter can make it complacent."},{"title":"Appraising the pre-reform strategy","body":"Strategy: public-sector leadership, licensing, small-industry protection and import substitution.\n\nAchievements: a wider industrial base, infrastructure and greater food security.\n\nLimitations: inefficient enterprises, restrictive controls, weak job creation and persistent poverty.\n\nThe pre-reform strategy built capacity, but its limitations created pressure for change.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.6 India’s Growth Strategy during 1950–1990","points":["Strategy: public-sector leadership, licensing, small-industry protection and import substitution.","Achievements: a wider industrial base, infrastructure and greater food security.","Limitations: inefficient enterprises, restrictive controls, weak job creation and persistent poverty."],"takeaway":"The pre-reform strategy built capacity, but its limitations created pressure for change."}]},{"id":"i3","number":"3","subject":"ied","title":"Economic Reforms Since 1991","intro":"","lessons":[{"title":"Why reforms became necessary in 1991","body":"Fiscal pressure: spending exceeded revenue, increasing borrowing and debt.\n\nExternal crisis: foreign-exchange reserves fell to roughly two weeks of imports.\n\nOther problems: inflation and weak public-enterprise returns.\n\nResponse: assistance from IMF/World Bank and a shift towards a more competitive economy.\n\n1991 combined an immediate payments crisis with deeper weaknesses in the old growth strategy.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.1 Need for New Economic Policy","points":["Fiscal pressure: spending exceeded revenue, increasing borrowing and debt.","External crisis: foreign-exchange reserves fell to roughly two weeks of imports.","Other problems: inflation and weak public-enterprise returns.","Response: assistance from IMF/World Bank and a shift towards a more competitive economy."],"takeaway":"1991 combined an immediate payments crisis with deeper weaknesses in the old growth strategy."},{"title":"Liberalisation: meaning and industrial reforms","body":"Liberalisation = reduce direct government controls over producing units.\n\nIndustrial changes: reduce licensing, open more activities to private firms, ease expansion and imports of capital goods.\n\nMechanism: easier entry → more competition → pressure to improve efficiency.\n\nFewer controls does not mean no regulation; safety, competition and other safeguards still matter.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.2 Elements of NEP · 3.2.1 Liberalisation","points":["Liberalisation = reduce direct government controls over producing units.","Industrial changes: reduce licensing, open more activities to private firms, ease expansion and imports of capital goods.","Mechanism: easier entry → more competition → pressure to improve efficiency."],"takeaway":"Fewer controls does not mean no regulation; safety, competition and other safeguards still matter."},{"title":"Financial-sector reforms","body":"Increase competition by allowing more private and foreign participation in finance.\n\nGive banks more operational freedom within safeguards.\n\nShift RBI’s emphasis from detailed control towards facilitation and regulation.\n\nRBI becoming a facilitator does not mean it stopped supervising banks.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.2.1 Liberalisation · Financial Sector Reforms","points":["Increase competition by allowing more private and foreign participation in finance.","Give banks more operational freedom within safeguards.","Shift RBI’s emphasis from detailed control towards facilitation and regulation."],"takeaway":"RBI becoming a facilitator does not mean it stopped supervising banks."},{"title":"Tax reforms, GST and demonetisation","body":"Tax reforms: simplify taxes, rationalise rates, widen the tax base and improve compliance.\n\nGST, 1 July 2017: brought many indirect taxes into a common framework.\n\nDemonetisation, November 2016: withdrew legal-tender status of the then ₹500/₹1,000 notes, aiming to address unaccounted money and encourage formal/digital activity.\n\nKeep a policy’s objective separate from whether it fully achieved that objective.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.2.1 Liberalisation · Fiscal and Tax Reforms","points":["Tax reforms: simplify taxes, rationalise rates, widen the tax base and improve compliance.","GST, 1 July 2017: brought many indirect taxes into a common framework.","Demonetisation, November 2016: withdrew legal-tender status of the then ₹500/₹1,000 notes, aiming to address unaccounted money and encourage formal/digital activity."],"takeaway":"Keep a policy’s objective separate from whether it fully achieved that objective."},{"title":"Devaluation and trade reforms","body":"1991 devaluation: officially lower the rupee’s value to improve export competitiveness and tackle the external crisis.\n\nTrade reforms: reduce tariffs, quantitative restrictions and licensing barriers.\n\nEffect: cheaper exports in foreign-currency terms, costlier imports in rupees and greater competition for domestic firms.\n\nDevaluation is an official change; depreciation is a market-driven fall in currency value.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.2.1 Liberalisation · Foreign Exchange and Trade Reforms","points":["1991 devaluation: officially lower the rupee’s value to improve export competitiveness and tackle the external crisis.","Trade reforms: reduce tariffs, quantitative restrictions and licensing barriers.","Effect: cheaper exports in foreign-currency terms, costlier imports in rupees and greater competition for domestic firms."],"takeaway":"Devaluation is an official change; depreciation is a market-driven fall in currency value."},{"title":"Privatisation and disinvestment","body":"Privatisation: greater private ownership or management of public enterprises.\n\nDisinvestment: sale of government equity; a minority sale need not transfer control.\n\nPossible gains: efficiency, commercial discipline and revenue.\n\nConcerns: undervaluation, job losses and loss of future public income.\n\nAsk whether ownership or control actually changed before calling a sale full privatisation.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.2.2 Privatisation","points":["Privatisation: greater private ownership or management of public enterprises.","Disinvestment: sale of government equity; a minority sale need not transfer control.","Possible gains: efficiency, commercial discipline and revenue.","Concerns: undervaluation, job losses and loss of future public income."],"takeaway":"Ask whether ownership or control actually changed before calling a sale full privatisation."},{"title":"Autonomy and public-enterprise status","body":"Selected public enterprises received greater financial and managerial freedom.\n\nNavratna/Maharatna arrangements aim to support quicker commercial decisions.\n\nThe enterprise can remain government-owned while gaining autonomy.\n\nAutonomy changes decision-making freedom; privatisation changes the private sector’s role in ownership or management.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.2.2 Privatisation · Public Enterprise Autonomy","points":["Selected public enterprises received greater financial and managerial freedom.","Navratna/Maharatna arrangements aim to support quicker commercial decisions.","The enterprise can remain government-owned while gaining autonomy."],"takeaway":"Autonomy changes decision-making freedom; privatisation changes the private sector’s role in ownership or management."},{"title":"Globalisation and outsourcing","body":"Globalisation = deeper integration through trade, investment, technology and services.\n\nOutsourcing = hiring an external provider for particular work.\n\nIndia’s advantages: skilled English-speaking workers, relatively low costs, IT links and time-zone differences.\n\nAn overseas company using an Indian accounting service is outsourcing; the wider cross-border integration is globalisation.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.2.3 Globalisation","points":["Globalisation = deeper integration through trade, investment, technology and services.","Outsourcing = hiring an external provider for particular work.","India’s advantages: skilled English-speaking workers, relatively low costs, IT links and time-zone differences."],"takeaway":"An overseas company using an Indian accounting service is outsourcing; the wider cross-border integration is globalisation."},{"title":"WTO: purpose and debate","body":"WTO began in 1995, succeeding GATT.\n\nRole: multilateral trade rules, negotiations and dispute settlement.\n\nDebate: developing countries face unequal bargaining power and competition from subsidised producers in richer countries.\n\nExplain its intended rules-based role, then assess whether the gains are distributed fairly.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.2.3 Globalisation · World Trade Organisation","points":["WTO began in 1995, succeeding GATT.","Role: multilateral trade rules, negotiations and dispute settlement.","Debate: developing countries face unequal bargaining power and competition from subsidised producers in richer countries."],"takeaway":"Explain its intended rules-based role, then assess whether the gains are distributed fairly."},{"title":"Reforms: favourable effects","body":"Competition and choice: firms face pressure to improve quality; consumers get more options.\n\nInvestment and technology: foreign links can bring finance, knowledge and market access.\n\nGrowth and external strength: the textbook highlights faster growth and stronger forex reserves over particular periods.\n\nUse a cause-and-effect chain, not just “reforms were good”: competition → efficiency → better products.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.3.1 Good Effect of LPG Policies","points":["Competition and choice: firms face pressure to improve quality; consumers get more options.","Investment and technology: foreign links can bring finance, knowledge and market access.","Growth and external strength: the textbook highlights faster growth and stronger forex reserves over particular periods."],"takeaway":"Use a cause-and-effect chain, not just “reforms were good”: competition → efficiency → better products."},{"title":"Reforms: criticisms and uneven gains","body":"Agriculture: reduced support and foreign competition hurt some producers.\n\nIndustry and jobs: import competition and capital-intensive growth limited gains for some firms/workers.\n\nUnequal development: benefits concentrated in certain sectors, cities and income groups.\n\nOther concerns: disinvestment, fiscal priorities, economic dependence and consumerism.\n\nReforms can raise total output while leaving particular people or sectors behind.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.3.2 Bad Effect of LPG Policies","points":["Agriculture: reduced support and foreign competition hurt some producers.","Industry and jobs: import competition and capital-intensive growth limited gains for some firms/workers.","Unequal development: benefits concentrated in certain sectors, cities and income groups.","Other concerns: disinvestment, fiscal priorities, economic dependence and consumerism."],"takeaway":"Reforms can raise total output while leaving particular people or sectors behind."}]},{"id":"i4","number":"4","subject":"ied","title":"Human Capital Formation in India","intro":"","lessons":[{"title":"Human resource, human capital and formation","body":"Human resource: people’s abilities to contribute to production.\n\nHuman capital: the stock of their skills, knowledge and health.\n\nHuman capital formation: adding to that stock through education, training and health investment.\n\nPopulation counts people; human capital asks what they are able to do.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.1.1 Human Resource · 4.1.2 Human Capital Formation","points":["Human resource: people’s abilities to contribute to production.","Human capital: the stock of their skills, knowledge and health.","Human capital formation: adding to that stock through education, training and health investment."],"takeaway":"Population counts people; human capital asks what they are able to do."},{"title":"Human capital versus human development","body":"Human capital view: education and health are means to higher productivity and income.\n\nHuman development view: education, health, freedom and well-being are valuable ends in themselves.\n\nImplication: people deserve health and education even without an immediate measured productivity gain.\n\nSame services, different ultimate purpose: productive returns versus a better human life.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.1.3 Human Capital vs. Human Development","points":["Human capital view: education and health are means to higher productivity and income.","Human development view: education, health, freedom and well-being are valuable ends in themselves.","Implication: people deserve health and education even without an immediate measured productivity gain."],"takeaway":"Same services, different ultimate purpose: productive returns versus a better human life."},{"title":"Physical capital versus human capital","body":"Physical capital: tangible, separable from its owner and saleable as an asset.\n\nHuman capital: embodied in a person; only its services can be sold.\n\nBoth depreciate, but learning and health investment can counter the loss of human capability.\n\nHuman capital also creates social benefits, such as informed citizenship.\n\nA machine can be sold separately; a driver’s skill cannot be separated from the driver.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.1 Physical Capital vs. Human Capital","points":["Physical capital: tangible, separable from its owner and saleable as an asset.","Human capital: embodied in a person; only its services can be sold.","Both depreciate, but learning and health investment can counter the loss of human capability.","Human capital also creates social benefits, such as informed citizenship."],"takeaway":"A machine can be sold separately; a driver’s skill cannot be separated from the driver."},{"title":"Education and health expenditure","body":"Education builds knowledge, skills and earning capacity.\n\nHealth increases efficiency and reduces working time lost to illness.\n\nHealth spending includes preventive care, treatment, awareness, hygiene and sanitation.\n\nThey complement each other: a sick worker cannot fully use even excellent skills.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.2 Sources of Human Capital","points":["Education builds knowledge, skills and earning capacity.","Health increases efficiency and reduces working time lost to illness.","Health spending includes preventive care, treatment, awareness, hygiene and sanitation."],"takeaway":"They complement each other: a sick worker cannot fully use even excellent skills."},{"title":"Training, migration and information","body":"On-the-job training: learn skills needed in actual work.\n\nAdult study: update knowledge and capabilities after initial schooling.\n\nMigration: bear travel/settlement costs to reach better opportunities.\n\nInformation: spend to find suitable education and jobs.\n\nEach is an investment when its cost is expected to produce future benefits.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.2 Sources of Human Capital","points":["On-the-job training: learn skills needed in actual work.","Adult study: update knowledge and capabilities after initial schooling.","Migration: bear travel/settlement costs to reach better opportunities.","Information: spend to find suitable education and jobs."],"takeaway":"Each is an investment when its cost is expected to produce future benefits."},{"title":"How human capital contributes to growth","body":"Change in emotional/material environment: education encourages new ideas and techniques.\n\nHigher productivity of physical capital: skilled workers use machines better.\n\nHigher participation and equality: more people can join and benefit from growth.\n\nThe relationship runs both ways: capabilities support growth, and growth finances better education and health.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.3 Human Capital Formation and Economic Growth","points":["Change in emotional/material environment: education encourages new ideas and techniques.","Higher productivity of physical capital: skilled workers use machines better.","Higher participation and equality: more people can join and benefit from growth."],"takeaway":"The relationship runs both ways: capabilities support growth, and growth finances better education and health."},{"title":"Problems of human capital formation","body":"Rising population stretches education and health resources.\n\nBrain drain reduces domestic access to skilled people.\n\nDeficient manpower planning creates a skills–jobs mismatch.\n\nInsufficient primary-sector training and low academic standards weaken practical capability.\n\nQualifications improve human capital only when they bring usable knowledge and skills.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.4 Problems Facing Human Capital Formation in India","points":["Rising population stretches education and health resources.","Brain drain reduces domestic access to skilled people.","Deficient manpower planning creates a skills–jobs mismatch.","Insufficient primary-sector training and low academic standards weaken practical capability."],"takeaway":"Qualifications improve human capital only when they bring usable knowledge and skills."},{"title":"Education: wider objectives","body":"Good citizenship and broader horizons: people make more informed choices.\n\nScience, technology and resource use: better knowledge improves production.\n\nCulture and personality: education develops people beyond their jobs.\n\nParticipation: more people can contribute to development.\n\nEducation generates both private returns and benefits for society.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.5.1 Importance and Objectives of Education","points":["Good citizenship and broader horizons: people make more informed choices.","Science, technology and resource use: better knowledge improves production.","Culture and personality: education develops people beyond their jobs.","Participation: more people can contribute to development."],"takeaway":"Education generates both private returns and benefits for society."},{"title":"Why government intervenes in education and health","body":"Public intervention widens access when private prices exclude poorer households.\n\nGovernment also finances costly facilities and sets quality standards.\n\nKothari Commission: education spending of 6% of GDP.\n\nRTE Act, 2009: free and compulsory education for ages 6–14.\n\nSpending as a share of GDP and as a share of the government budget have different denominators.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.5.2 Need for Government Intervention","points":["Public intervention widens access when private prices exclude poorer households.","Government also finances costly facilities and sets quality standards.","Kothari Commission: education spending of 6% of GDP.","RTE Act, 2009: free and compulsory education for ages 6–14."],"takeaway":"Spending as a share of GDP and as a share of the government budget have different denominators."},{"title":"Achievements, knowledge economy and remaining gaps","body":"Achievements: more schools, higher education, literacy and technical skills support a knowledge economy.\n\nAccess gaps: illiteracy, gender bias, low rural access and expensive private provision.\n\nQuality gap: weak vocationalisation and insufficient public spending.\n\nSteep education pyramid: fewer people reach each higher level.\n\nAccess gets people into education; quality and relevant skills determine what they gain.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.5 Education Sector: Achievements and Challenges","points":["Achievements: more schools, higher education, literacy and technical skills support a knowledge economy.","Access gaps: illiteracy, gender bias, low rural access and expensive private provision.","Quality gap: weak vocationalisation and insufficient public spending.","Steep education pyramid: fewer people reach each higher level."],"takeaway":"Access gets people into education; quality and relevant skills determine what they gain."}]},{"id":"i5","number":"5","subject":"ied","title":"Rural Development","intro":"","lessons":[{"title":"Rural development and the action plan","body":"Rural development addresses economic, social and environmental problems together.\n\nPriorities: education/health, land and productive resources, infrastructure, credit and marketing.\n\nDiversification and poverty reduction create livelihoods beyond seasonal crop work.\n\nImproving rural life is broader than simply increasing farm output.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.1 Rural Development: Meaning and Significance","points":["Rural development addresses economic, social and environmental problems together.","Priorities: education/health, land and productive resources, infrastructure, credit and marketing.","Diversification and poverty reduction create livelihoods beyond seasonal crop work."],"takeaway":"Improving rural life is broader than simply increasing farm output."},{"title":"Why rural credit is needed","body":"Short-term credit pays for seeds, fertilisers, wages and needs before harvest income arrives.\n\nLong-term credit pays for machinery, irrigation and land improvement.\n\nInstitutional sources: cooperatives, banks, RRBs and land-development banks.\n\nNon-institutional sources: moneylenders, landlords and traders.\n\nThe timing gap between spending on a crop and earning from it explains the need for credit.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.2.1 Rural Credit","points":["Short-term credit pays for seeds, fertilisers, wages and needs before harvest income arrives.","Long-term credit pays for machinery, irrigation and land improvement.","Institutional sources: cooperatives, banks, RRBs and land-development banks.","Non-institutional sources: moneylenders, landlords and traders."],"takeaway":"The timing gap between spending on a crop and earning from it explains the need for credit."},{"title":"NABARD, self-help groups and micro-credit","body":"NABARD, 1982: apex institution supporting and coordinating rural credit/development.\n\nSHGs pool members’ small savings and lend within the group, often linking to banks.\n\nMicro-credit can reach borrowers without conventional collateral and support women’s participation.\n\nSmall loans help inclusion; viable work, skills and market access determine whether incomes improve.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.2.1 Rural Credit · NABARD and Micro-credit","points":["NABARD, 1982: apex institution supporting and coordinating rural credit/development.","SHGs pool members’ small savings and lend within the group, often linking to banks.","Micro-credit can reach borrowers without conventional collateral and support women’s participation."],"takeaway":"Small loans help inclusion; viable work, skills and market access determine whether incomes improve."},{"title":"Weaknesses of rural credit","body":"Unequal access: small farmers may still depend on informal lenders.\n\nDelays, paperwork and collateral make institutional credit hard to obtain.\n\nCrop failure and unproductive borrowing make repayment difficult.\n\nOverdues weaken lenders and restrict future credit.\n\nGood credit needs the right timing, terms and productive use—not just a larger loan.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.2.1 Rural Credit · Problems","points":["Unequal access: small farmers may still depend on informal lenders.","Delays, paperwork and collateral make institutional credit hard to obtain.","Crop failure and unproductive borrowing make repayment difficult.","Overdues weaken lenders and restrict future credit."],"takeaway":"Good credit needs the right timing, terms and productive use—not just a larger loan."},{"title":"Agricultural marketing and its defects","body":"Marketing includes gathering, processing, grading, packing, storage, transport and sale.\n\nDefects: faulty weights, poor information, weak transport/storage and large intermediary margins.\n\nDistress sale: urgent cash needs force farmers to sell cheaply after harvest.\n\nCredit and marketing are linked: debt pressure plus no storage weakens the farmer’s bargaining power.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.2.2 Agricultural Marketing","points":["Marketing includes gathering, processing, grading, packing, storage, transport and sale.","Defects: faulty weights, poor information, weak transport/storage and large intermediary margins.","Distress sale: urgent cash needs force farmers to sell cheaply after harvest."],"takeaway":"Credit and marketing are linked: debt pressure plus no storage weakens the farmer’s bargaining power."},{"title":"Improving agricultural marketing","body":"Regulated markets improve fairness; roads, storage and information reduce losses.\n\nCooperatives pool produce and strengthen bargaining.\n\nMSP, procurement, buffer stocks and PDS support the food-marketing system.\n\nDirect markets reduce intermediaries; contracts offer markets but can create bargaining risks.\n\nDifferent measures fix different problems: storage addresses timing; market information addresses bargaining.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.2.2 Agricultural Marketing · Remedial Measures","points":["Regulated markets improve fairness; roads, storage and information reduce losses.","Cooperatives pool produce and strengthen bargaining.","MSP, procurement, buffer stocks and PDS support the food-marketing system.","Direct markets reduce intermediaries; contracts offer markets but can create bargaining risks."],"takeaway":"Different measures fix different problems: storage addresses timing; market information addresses bargaining."},{"title":"Why rural diversification matters","body":"Crop diversification changes the mix of crops; broader diversification adds allied/non-farm work.\n\nWhy? Spread risk, earn outside the crop season and absorb surplus farm labour.\n\nOptions: animal husbandry, fisheries, horticulture, processing and rural services.\n\nDiversification turns a single seasonal income source into several livelihood opportunities.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.3.1 Diversification of Productive Activities","points":["Crop diversification changes the mix of crops; broader diversification adds allied/non-farm work.","Why? Spread risk, earn outside the crop season and absorb surplus farm labour.","Options: animal husbandry, fisheries, horticulture, processing and rural services."],"takeaway":"Diversification turns a single seasonal income source into several livelihood opportunities."},{"title":"Animal husbandry, fisheries and Operation Flood","body":"Animal husbandry supplies milk, eggs and meat, adding income alongside crops.\n\nOperation Flood: milk cooperatives linked producers and consumers, supporting the White Revolution.\n\nFisheries create jobs but need finance, equipment, storage and protection from pollution.\n\nVeterinary care, feed and better breeds raise livestock productivity.\n\nAllied activities reduce dependence on crops, but still require reliable support services and markets.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.3.1 Diversification · Animal Husbandry and Fisheries","points":["Animal husbandry supplies milk, eggs and meat, adding income alongside crops.","Operation Flood: milk cooperatives linked producers and consumers, supporting the White Revolution.","Fisheries create jobs but need finance, equipment, storage and protection from pollution.","Veterinary care, feed and better breeds raise livestock productivity."],"takeaway":"Allied activities reduce dependence on crops, but still require reliable support services and markets."},{"title":"Horticulture and information technology","body":"Horticulture: fruits, vegetables, flowers and plantation crops can raise incomes and improve nutrition.\n\nPerishability makes cold chains, transport and processing important.\n\nIT provides weather, prices, advice and links to work/markets.\n\nElectricity, connectivity and skills determine who benefits from IT.\n\nProducing a valuable crop is only half the task; getting it to buyers without spoilage is the other half.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.3.1 Diversification · Horticulture and IT","points":["Horticulture: fruits, vegetables, flowers and plantation crops can raise incomes and improve nutrition.","Perishability makes cold chains, transport and processing important.","IT provides weather, prices, advice and links to work/markets.","Electricity, connectivity and skills determine who benefits from IT."],"takeaway":"Producing a valuable crop is only half the task; getting it to buyers without spoilage is the other half."},{"title":"Organic farming: meaning and advantages","body":"Organic farming uses organic inputs and ecological methods rather than synthetic chemicals.\n\nBenefits: healthier soil, less pollution and lower dependence on non-renewable chemical inputs.\n\nTextbook headings also emphasise healthier food, inexpensive technology and potential export premiums.\n\nIndia’s abundant labour can support labour-intensive organic methods.\n\nKeep the environmental benefits clear; premium prices and lower costs are possibilities, not guarantees.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.3.2 Organic Farming","points":["Organic farming uses organic inputs and ecological methods rather than synthetic chemicals.","Benefits: healthier soil, less pollution and lower dependence on non-renewable chemical inputs.","Textbook headings also emphasise healthier food, inexpensive technology and potential export premiums.","India’s abundant labour can support labour-intensive organic methods."],"takeaway":"Keep the environmental benefits clear; premium prices and lower costs are possibilities, not guarantees."},{"title":"Organic farming: constraints and the PRANAM box","body":"Constraints: lower transition yields, high cost per unit and shorter shelf life.\n\nFarmers need training, suitable inputs, certification and marketing.\n\nPRANAM box: encourage alternative nutrients and balanced fertiliser use to protect soil health.\n\nOrganic farming becomes viable when production support is matched by a reliable market.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.3.2 Organic Farming · Limitations and Support","points":["Constraints: lower transition yields, high cost per unit and shorter shelf life.","Farmers need training, suitable inputs, certification and marketing.","PRANAM box: encourage alternative nutrients and balanced fertiliser use to protect soil health."],"takeaway":"Organic farming becomes viable when production support is matched by a reliable market."}]},{"id":"i6","number":"6","subject":"ied","title":"Employment","intro":"","lessons":[{"title":"Workers and economic activity","body":"A worker participates in an economic activity producing goods or services.\n\nSelf-employed and hired workers both count.\n\nUnpaid family helpers in a farm/business can count even without a separate wage.\n\nOwn-family domestic services are generally outside measured employment.\n\nDo not confuse “no wage” with “no economic production”.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.1.1 Who is a Worker? · 6.1.2 Workers and Employment","points":["A worker participates in an economic activity producing goods or services.","Self-employed and hired workers both count.","Unpaid family helpers in a farm/business can count even without a separate wage.","Own-family domestic services are generally outside measured employment."],"takeaway":"Do not confuse “no wage” with “no economic production”."},{"title":"Self-employed, regular and casual workers","body":"Self-employed: operate their own enterprise, such as a shop owner.\n\nRegular salaried: continuing employment with regular pay.\n\nCasual wage labourer: hired for short periods/tasks, such as a daily-wage construction worker.\n\nEmployment status tells you the work relationship; construction or agriculture tells you the sector.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.1.3 Self-employed and Hired Workers","points":["Self-employed: operate their own enterprise, such as a shop owner.","Regular salaried: continuing employment with regular pay.","Casual wage labourer: hired for short periods/tasks, such as a daily-wage construction worker."],"takeaway":"Employment status tells you the work relationship; construction or agriculture tells you the sector."},{"title":"Labour force, workforce and participation","body":"Workforce: people actually employed.\n\nLabour force: employed people plus unemployed people willing and available for work.\n\nLabour supply: the hours/services people offer at a wage.\n\nSomeone not seeking or available for work is outside the labour force, rather than automatically unemployed.\n\nWorker-population ratio uses population; unemployment rate uses labour force. Always check the denominator.","formula":"Labour force = Workforce + Unemployed\nWorker-population ratio = Workers ÷ Population × 100\nUnemployment rate = Unemployed ÷ Labour force × 100","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.1.4 Labour Supply, Labour Force, Workforce","points":["Workforce: people actually employed.","Labour force: employed people plus unemployed people willing and available for work.","Labour supply: the hours/services people offer at a wage.","Someone not seeking or available for work is outside the labour force, rather than automatically unemployed."],"takeaway":"Worker-population ratio uses population; unemployment rate uses labour force. Always check the denominator."},{"title":"Rural–urban and male–female participation","body":"Rural work participation reflects farming, family labour and the need to earn even in low-productivity work.\n\nUrban patterns differ because of education and the available jobs.\n\nWomen face care responsibilities, social barriers and under-recording of economic activity.\n\nA lower measured participation rate does not mean women make no productive contribution.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.2 Size of Workforce · 6.3 Rate of Participation","points":["Rural work participation reflects farming, family labour and the need to earn even in low-productivity work.","Urban patterns differ because of education and the available jobs.","Women face care responsibilities, social barriers and under-recording of economic activity."],"takeaway":"A lower measured participation rate does not mean women make no productive contribution."},{"title":"Workforce across sectors","body":"Primary: agriculture and allied activities.\n\nSecondary: manufacturing and construction. Tertiary: services.\n\nRural workers are more concentrated in farming; urban work is more concentrated outside it.\n\nA high farm employment share with a low farm GDP share signals low average output per worker.\n\nDevelopment needs productive non-farm jobs, not just movement out of agriculture.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.5 Occupational Structure or Distribution of Workforce","points":["Primary: agriculture and allied activities.","Secondary: manufacturing and construction. Tertiary: services.","Rural workers are more concentrated in farming; urban work is more concentrated outside it.","A high farm employment share with a low farm GDP share signals low average output per worker."],"takeaway":"Development needs productive non-farm jobs, not just movement out of agriculture."},{"title":"Jobless growth and technology","body":"Jobless growth: GDP rises faster than employment opportunities.\n\nCapital-intensive technology and productivity gains can raise output without many new jobs.\n\nResponses: labour-intensive activities, small enterprises, relevant skills and rural diversification.\n\nIt means inadequate job creation relative to growth—not necessarily zero new jobs in every year.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.6 Jobless Growth","points":["Jobless growth: GDP rises faster than employment opportunities.","Capital-intensive technology and productivity gains can raise output without many new jobs.","Responses: labour-intensive activities, small enterprises, relevant skills and rural diversification."],"takeaway":"It means inadequate job creation relative to growth—not necessarily zero new jobs in every year."},{"title":"Casualisation of the workforce","body":"Casualisation: a rising share of casual wage work rather than secure regular jobs/self-employment.\n\nSurplus rural labour migrates; limited jobs and weak bargaining power encourage daily-wage hiring.\n\nConsequences: insecurity and fewer benefits.\n\nThe concept describes a process; check actual table years before claiming the casual share always rose.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.7.1 Casualisation","points":["Casualisation: a rising share of casual wage work rather than secure regular jobs/self-employment.","Surplus rural labour migrates; limited jobs and weak bargaining power encourage daily-wage hiring.","Consequences: insecurity and fewer benefits."],"takeaway":"The concept describes a process; check actual table years before claiming the casual share always rose."},{"title":"Formal and informal employment","body":"Formal sector: government/public enterprises and private establishments with ten or more workers in the textbook definition.\n\nInformal sector: smaller private enterprises, farming and self-employment.\n\nInformalisation: more employment without formal protection and social-security benefits.\n\nCasualisation concerns job status; informalisation concerns organisation and protection. They can overlap.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.7.2 Informalisation","points":["Formal sector: government/public enterprises and private establishments with ten or more workers in the textbook definition.","Informal sector: smaller private enterprises, farming and self-employment.","Informalisation: more employment without formal protection and social-security benefits."],"takeaway":"Casualisation concerns job status; informalisation concerns organisation and protection. They can overlap."},{"title":"Open, disguised and seasonal unemployment","body":"Open: willing and able to work, but unable to get a job.\n\nDisguised: extra workers can leave without reducing output; their marginal productivity is zero.\n\nSeasonal: work disappears outside the working season.\n\nEducated/structural: qualifications or skills do not match available opportunities.\n\nIf two people leave a family farm and output stays the same, those two were disguisedly unemployed.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.8 Unemployment · 6.8.1 Types","points":["Open: willing and able to work, but unable to get a job.","Disguised: extra workers can leave without reducing output; their marginal productivity is zero.","Seasonal: work disappears outside the working season.","Educated/structural: qualifications or skills do not match available opportunities."],"takeaway":"If two people leave a family farm and output stays the same, those two were disguisedly unemployed."},{"title":"Why unemployment persists","body":"Slow job growth and rapid population growth widen the employment gap.\n\nSeasonal farming and inadequate irrigation leave people without year-round work.\n\nJoint-family farming can conceal surplus labour; declining cottage industries reduce alternatives.\n\nLow investment, skills mismatch and limited mobility restrict opportunities.\n\nExplain the mechanism after the heading: inadequate irrigation → fewer crop seasons → seasonal unemployment.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.8.2 Causes of Unemployment in India","points":["Slow job growth and rapid population growth widen the employment gap.","Seasonal farming and inadequate irrigation leave people without year-round work.","Joint-family farming can conceal surplus labour; declining cottage industries reduce alternatives.","Low investment, skills mismatch and limited mobility restrict opportunities."],"takeaway":"Explain the mechanism after the heading: inadequate irrigation → fewer crop seasons → seasonal unemployment."},{"title":"Government measures and MGNREGA","body":"Direct employment: government hires workers itself. Indirect: public activity raises demand and jobs in other firms.\n\nMGNREGA: at least 100 days of unskilled manual wage work per financial year for a rural household whose adults volunteer.\n\nWork is to be provided within 15 days of demand; eligible failure attracts unemployment allowance.\n\nSkills, rural livelihoods, roads and small-business support create other opportunities.\n\nThe MGNREGA entitlement is per household, not 100 days separately for every adult.","formula":"","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.9 Government and Employment Generation","points":["Direct employment: government hires workers itself. Indirect: public activity raises demand and jobs in other firms.","MGNREGA: at least 100 days of unskilled manual wage work per financial year for a rural household whose adults volunteer.","Work is to be provided within 15 days of demand; eligible failure attracts unemployment allowance.","Skills, rural livelihoods, roads and small-business support create other opportunities."],"takeaway":"The MGNREGA entitlement is per household, not 100 days separately for every adult."}]}],"questions":[{"chapter":"1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a macroeconomic issue?","options":["One household’s saving","Price of one brand of shoes","Output of one firm","General price level"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The general price level is an economy-wide variable.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q1"},{"chapter":"1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Studying the equilibrium of the steel industry is:","options":["National-income accounting","Microeconomics","Always macroeconomics","Neither branch"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Aggregation alone does not make a study macroeconomic; one industry remains a microeconomic unit.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q2"},{"chapter":"1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Assertion: More saving by every household can reduce total income. Reason: Lower consumption can reduce aggregate demand.","options":["Both true; reason explains assertion","Assertion false; reason true","Assertion true; reason false","Both true; reason does not explain assertion"],"answer":0,"explanation":"This is the micro–macro paradox under demand-constrained conditions.","tag":"Assertion–reason","id":"q3"},{"chapter":"1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Partial equilibrium studies:","options":["Only foreign trade","Only government accounts","All markets simultaneously","One market, holding other markets unchanged"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Partial equilibrium is the textbook’s method of microeconomic analysis.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q4"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A bakery buys flour to make bread. The flour is:","options":["Final consumption","Fixed investment","A capital good","An intermediate good"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Its end use is as a raw material in current production.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q5"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A new machine used repeatedly by a factory is:","options":["A non-durable consumer good","A final capital good","An intermediate good","A transfer payment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Capital goods are final goods used repeatedly to produce other goods.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q6"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which pair consists entirely of stocks?","options":["Capital and wealth","Consumption and expenditure","Income and saving","Investment and output"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Stocks are measured at a point of time; all the other pairs are flows.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q7"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If gross investment is less than depreciation, net investment is:","options":["Equal to gross investment","Positive","Zero","Negative"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Net investment = gross investment − depreciation; the capital stock falls.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q8"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a real flow from households to firms?","options":["Wages","Consumption expenditure","Rent payments","Factor services"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Households supply factor services; firms pay money incomes in the opposite direction.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q9"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A car bought for private household use and an identical car bought to run as a taxi are respectively:","options":["Intermediate and final","Capital and consumer goods","Consumer durable and capital good","Both intermediate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Classification depends on end use, not physical appearance.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q10"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Closing stock is 80 and opening stock is 110. Inventory investment equals:","options":["80","190","−30","30"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Change in stock = 80 − 110 = −30.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q11"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"National income is:","options":["NNPFC","GDPMP","GNPMP","NDPMP"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The textbook defines national income as net national product at factor cost.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q12"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"To convert GDPMP to NDPFC, subtract:","options":["Depreciation and NFIA","Depreciation and NIT","Subsidies only","NFIA only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Gross → net removes depreciation; market price → factor cost removes NIT.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q13"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Factor income paid abroad exceeds factor income received from abroad. NFIA is:","options":["Necessarily equal to GDP","Zero","Negative","Positive"],"answer":2,"explanation":"NFIA = factor income from abroad − factor income to abroad.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q14"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which belongs to India’s domestic territory?","options":["Every foreign branch owned by an Indian citizen","US embassy in New Delhi","Indian embassy in Japan","An Indian tourist’s hotel room abroad"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An Indian embassy abroad is part of India’s economic territory.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q15"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A gift received from a relative abroad is:","options":["Domestic product","A current transfer","Factor income from abroad","Depreciation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A gift does not reward factor services; it is not NFIA.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q16"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Nominal GDP rises 10% while quantities are unchanged. This can be explained by:","options":["A fall in all prices","A fall in the GDP deflator","An increase in real output","An increase in prices"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Nominal GDP uses current prices, so price increases can raise it without more output.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q17"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a limitation of GDP as a welfare index?","options":["It has an accounting period","It ignores income distribution","It includes final goods","It measures current production"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The same GDP can coexist with very different distributions of income.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q18"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Statement 1: Citizenship and normal residence are identical. Statement 2: Domestic income can include factor income earned by non-residents.","options":["Both false","Only 1 true","Both true","Only 2 true"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Residence depends on economic interest and residence conventions; domestic income depends on territory.","tag":"Two statements","id":"q19"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Sales are 500, opening stock 40 and closing stock 70. Value of output is:","options":["530","610","500","470"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Output = sales + change in stock = 500 + 70 − 40 = 530.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q20"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is included in compensation of employees?","options":["Employer contribution to provident fund","Dividend","Old-age welfare pension","Lottery winnings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Employer social-security contributions form part of compensation of employees.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q21"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Operating surplus excludes:","options":["Interest","Rent","Wages","Profit"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Wages are compensation of employees; operating surplus includes rent, interest and profit.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q22"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A dealer sells a second-hand car and charges commission. Current GDP includes:","options":["Resale value only","Commission only","Full resale value and commission","Neither"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The car was counted when first produced; the dealer’s current service is new production.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q23"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"GDCF is given in an expenditure-method question along with change in stock. You should normally:","options":["Add both in full","Ignore all investment","Deduct change in stock","Use GDCF without adding stock again"],"answer":3,"explanation":"GDCF already includes inventory investment; check the label before adding components.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q24"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The same wheat becomes flour and then bread. Counting every stage’s full sales value causes:","options":["Double counting","A transfer payment","NFIA","Depreciation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Intermediate value is already embodied in the final product.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q25"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which purchase is investment expenditure in GDP?","options":["An old bond","Existing shares","A second-hand house’s full value","A newly built house"],"answer":3,"explanation":"New residential construction adds to the capital stock.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q26"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Arrange the value-added steps: A add NFIA; B calculate output; C deduct intermediate consumption; D deduct depreciation and NIT.","options":["B → C → D → A","C → A → B → D","A → B → C → D","B → D → A → C"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Output minus intermediate consumption gives GVA MP; then convert to national income.","tag":"Sequence","id":"q27"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Barter requires:","options":["Paper currency","A central bank","Demand deposits","Double coincidence of wants"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Each party must want the good offered by the other.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q28"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A price tag of ₹300 illustrates money as:","options":["Store of value","Measure of value","Standard of deferred payment","Transfer of ownership"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Money supplies a common unit of account.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q29"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Repaying a loan in fixed rupee instalments illustrates:","options":["Barter","Only store of value","Credit rationing","Standard of deferred payments"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Future obligations are expressed and settled in money.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q30"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is included in M1?","options":["Interbank deposits","Shares held by public","Demand deposits with commercial banks","Time deposits only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"M1 includes currency with public, demand deposits and other deposits with RBI.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q31"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Statement 1: Money supply is a flow. Statement 2: Money held by the public is part of money supply.","options":["Only 2 true","Only 1 true","Both false","Both true"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Money supply is a stock at a point of time.","tag":"Two statements","id":"q32"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Liquidity refers to:","options":["Face value of a note","Rate of inflation","Amount of government debt","Convertibility into cash"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An asset is more liquid when it converts to cash more readily.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q33"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A cheque is:","options":["A second stock of money in addition to the deposit","Unlimited legal tender in every transaction","A capital good","An instruction to transfer a bank deposit"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Counting the cheque and the underlying deposit separately would duplicate the same money.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q34"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"With a 20% reserve ratio, the simple deposit multiplier is:","options":["2","20","0.2","5"],"answer":3,"explanation":"1 ÷ 0.20 = 5 under the simple model’s assumptions.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q35"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"RBI buys government securities from the market. Other things equal, bank liquidity:","options":["Rises","Becomes zero","Is necessarily unchanged","Falls"],"answer":0,"explanation":"RBI purchases inject liquidity, supporting credit creation.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q36"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which action can restrict credit?","options":["Purchase securities","Raise margin requirement","Lower CRR","Lower repo rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A larger margin means a smaller loan for the same collateral.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q37"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"CRR reserves are maintained:","options":["Only with the government treasury","Only as gold in each bank","With the borrower","With the RBI"],"answer":3,"explanation":"CRR is the required percentage of deposits kept with the central bank.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q38"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An RBI emergency loan to a bank unable to borrow elsewhere illustrates:","options":["Portfolio investment","Lender of last resort","Tax collection","Banker to government"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The central bank supports banks when normal sources of funds fail.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q39"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a qualitative instrument?","options":["Rationing of credit","SLR","CRR","Repo rate"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Rationing directs credit to specified uses or sectors.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q40"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If banks keep excess reserves or borrowers keep cash, actual deposit expansion is:","options":["Potentially less than the simple maximum","Exactly infinite","Unaffected by leakage","Always greater than 1/reserve ratio"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The maximum assumes all lendable reserves are used and loans are redeposited.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q41"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"C = 60 + 0.75Y. Autonomous consumption is:","options":["0.25","0.75","60","60Y"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The intercept is consumption at Y = 0.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q42"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The slope of the consumption curve equals:","options":["APS","MPS","APC","MPC"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Slope = ΔC/ΔY, which is MPC.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q43"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"At the break-even point:","options":["MPC = 1 and MPS = 0 necessarily","Saving equals investment necessarily","APC = 0 and APS = 1","APC = 1 and APS = 0"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Break-even means C = Y and S = 0; it does not by itself establish macroeconomic equilibrium.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q44"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If APC is 1.2, APS is:","options":["0.2","−0.2","2.2","1.2"],"answer":1,"explanation":"APC + APS = 1, hence APS = −0.2.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q45"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"In an open economy, AD is:","options":["C + S","Y − S","C + I + G + X + M","C + I + G + X − M"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Net exports are exports minus imports.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q46"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"With positive autonomous consumption and constant MPC below one, APC normally:","options":["Always equals MPC","Falls with income","Always exceeds two","Rises with income"],"answer":1,"explanation":"APC = C̄/Y + MPC; the first term falls as Y rises.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q47"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If S = −80 + 0.2Y, consumption is:","options":["80 + 0.8Y","−80 + 1.2Y","80 + 0.2Y","−80 + 0.8Y"],"answer":0,"explanation":"C = Y − S = 80 + 0.8Y.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q48"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"At zero income APC is:","options":["Equal to MPC","Zero","Undefined","One"],"answer":2,"explanation":"APC = C/Y cannot be calculated with Y = 0.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q49"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If planned AD is below output, unplanned inventories:","options":["Decrease","Stay equal to desired inventories","Become exports automatically","Increase"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Unsold goods accumulate, leading firms to cut production.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q50"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Equilibrium in the two-sector model requires:","options":["C = Y always","Planned S = planned I","Unemployment = zero","APC = MPC"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Planned saving equals planned investment; full employment is not guaranteed.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q51"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Ex-post saving and investment are equal because:","options":["Every plan succeeds","Unplanned inventory investment is included","Government balances its budget","MPC is zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Realised investment includes unintended changes in stocks.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q52"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"MPC rises from 0.6 to 0.8. The multiplier:","options":["Falls to 1","Stays at 4","Rises from 2.5 to 5","Falls from 5 to 2.5"],"answer":2,"explanation":"k = 1/(1 − MPC).","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q53"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Assertion: Equilibrium can coexist with unemployment. Reason: Planned expenditure can equal planned output below capacity.","options":["Assertion false; reason true","Both true; reason explains assertion","Assertion true; reason false","Both true; reason does not explain assertion"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Underemployment equilibrium matches plans without full utilisation.","tag":"Assertion–reason","id":"q54"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An investment decline of 40 with MPC 0.75 causes an income change of:","options":["−160","−10","160","−40"],"answer":0,"explanation":"k = 4; ΔY = 4 × (−40) = −160.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q55"},{"chapter":"9","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The inflationary gap is measured at:","options":["Zero output","Full-employment output","Break-even consumption income only","Any arbitrary income"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It is excess AD over AS at full employment.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q56"},{"chapter":"9","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which policy helps correct deficient demand?","options":["Raise taxes","Buy securities through OMO","Cut government spending","Raise CRR"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Buying securities injects liquidity and supports lending and spending.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q57"},{"chapter":"9","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Beyond full-employment capacity, excess AD principally raises:","options":["The capital stock immediately","Real output without limit","The general price level","Unemployment by definition"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In the simple model real output cannot expand beyond capacity.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q58"},{"chapter":"9","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is fiscal policy?","options":["RBI securities sale","SLR change","Government expenditure increase","Repo-rate reduction"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fiscal policy concerns government revenue and expenditure.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q59"},{"chapter":"9","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Workers willing to work at the existing wage but unable to find work face:","options":["Involuntary unemployment","Only leisure preference","Voluntary unemployment","Full employment"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The inability to obtain work despite willingness distinguishes involuntary unemployment.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q60"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which receipt creates a liability?","options":["A fine","Government borrowing","Income tax","A fee"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Borrowing is a capital receipt because it creates a repayment obligation.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q61"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Recovery of a loan by government is:","options":["Revenue receipt","A tax","Revenue expenditure","Capital receipt"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It reduces the government’s financial asset: outstanding loans.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q62"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is capital expenditure?","options":["Loan-principal repayment","A food subsidy","Interest on past debt","Employee salaries"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Repayment reduces liabilities; interest does not.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q63"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Fiscal deficit measures:","options":["Exports minus imports","Only revenue receipts","Borrowing requirement","Only interest payments"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It is expenditure minus receipts other than borrowings.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q64"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If primary deficit is zero:","options":["Fiscal deficit is zero necessarily","Government debt is zero","Fiscal deficit equals interest payments","Revenue deficit is zero necessarily"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Primary deficit = fiscal deficit − interest payments.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q65"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"National defence is generally:","options":["Rival and excludable","Non-rival and non-excludable","Rival and non-excludable","Non-rival and excludable"],"answer":1,"explanation":"These are the two characteristics of a public good.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q66"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Progressive taxation and spending on low-income households primarily serve:","options":["Credit creation","Redistribution","Exchange-rate hedging","Currency issue"],"answer":1,"explanation":"They can reduce inequality in income and wealth.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q67"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A grant given to a state government for building a hospital is classified by the granting government as:","options":["A loan-principal repayment","Revenue expenditure","Disinvestment","A capital receipt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The textbook convention treats grants as revenue expenditure for the grantor, even when used to create assets.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q68"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The rate rises from ₹80/$ to ₹84/$ under a flexible system. The rupee has:","options":["Appreciated","Been revalued officially","Become a fixed currency","Depreciated"],"answer":3,"explanation":"More rupees are needed per dollar: the rupee’s external value falls.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q69"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"More inward FDI raises the supply of dollars. Other things equal, ₹/$:","options":["Must stay fixed","Rises","Becomes negative","Falls"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A rightward supply shift reduces the price of dollars and appreciates the rupee.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q70"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An official reduction in the external value of a currency under a fixed system is:","options":["A floating appreciation","Devaluation","Revaluation","Appreciation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Devaluation is an official change; depreciation is market driven.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q71"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"When excess demand for dollars threatens a fixed rate, the central bank can:","options":["Buy dollars","Sell dollars from reserves","Ban all domestic saving","Issue foreign shares itself"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Selling dollars supplies the shortage and supports the domestic currency.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q72"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A contract today fixing an exchange rate for payment three months later is a:","options":["Currency reserve ratio","Spot contract","Forward contract","Transfer payment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Forward markets allow future exchange on terms agreed today.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q73"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A rise in ₹/$ makes a $100 import cost:","options":["Zero rupees","Fewer rupees","The same rupees always","More rupees"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The foreign-currency price times a larger rupee/dollar rate gives a larger rupee cost.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q74"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An export of goods is recorded as:","options":["Capital-account debit","Official-reserve loss automatically","Current-account credit","Current-account debit"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Exports bring foreign-exchange receipts and are current-account credits.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q75"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An Indian resident sends a gift abroad. The entry is:","options":["Current-account debit","Domestic factor income","A goods export","Capital-account credit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An outward current transfer is a debit in the current account.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q76"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Interest paid on a foreign loan and repayment of its principal belong respectively to:","options":["Current and capital accounts","Both trade balance","Both transfers","Capital and current accounts"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Interest is factor income; principal changes an external liability.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q77"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A BoP accounting balance proves that:","options":["All autonomous transactions balance","Counterpart and financing entries close the accounts","Foreign borrowing is zero","There is no external deficit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Accounting balance can coexist with an autonomous deficit financed through reserves or loans.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q78"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Accommodating transactions are undertaken:","options":["Only to earn profit","Only as goods exports","To finance or correct an external imbalance","Only by tourists"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Their purpose is adjustment of the BoP imbalance; they are below-the-line items.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q79"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Balance of trade includes:","options":["All current transfers","Goods only","All capital flows","Goods and all services"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Trade balance is merchandise exports minus merchandise imports.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q80"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Under the supplied textbook’s convention, direct purchases by foreign tourists in domestic markets are:","options":["Merchandise exports","Domestic borrowing","Capital-account debits","Invisible trade"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The purple box classifies direct tourist purchases as invisible trade.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q81"},{"chapter":"2","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Gross fixed investment is ₹420 crore. Opening inventories are ₹90 crore and closing inventories ₹120 crore. Depreciation is ₹70 crore. Find inventory investment, gross investment and net investment.","solution":"1. Inventory investment = 120 − 90 = ₹30 crore.\n2. Gross investment = 420 + 30 = ₹450 crore.\n3. Net investment = 450 − 70 = ₹380 crore.\nHeading to remember: Gross Investment, Net Investment and Depreciation.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q82"},{"chapter":"2","type":"numerical","marks":3,"q":"A machine costs ₹12 lakh, has scrap value ₹2 lakh and an expected life of 5 years. Find annual depreciation. If annual gross investment is ₹7 lakh, find net investment.","solution":"Annual depreciation = (12 − 2)/5 = ₹2 lakh.\nNet investment = 7 − 2 = ₹5 lakh.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q83"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"GDPMP = ₹8,000 crore; depreciation = ₹500 crore; indirect taxes = ₹700 crore; subsidies = ₹100 crore; factor income from abroad = ₹180 crore; factor income paid abroad = ₹260 crore. Find NIT, NFIA, NDPFC and national income.","solution":"NIT = 700 − 100 = ₹600 crore.\nNFIA = 180 − 260 = −₹80 crore.\nNDPFC = 8,000 − 500 − 600 = ₹6,900 crore.\nNNPFC = 6,900 − 80 = ₹6,820 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q84"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"National income is ₹6,200 crore, depreciation ₹300 crore, NIT ₹450 crore and NFIA −₹50 crore. Find GDPMP.","solution":"GDPMP = NNPFC + Depreciation + NIT − NFIA\n= 6,200 + 300 + 450 − (−50) = ₹7,000 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q85"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"GDPMP is ₹4,800 crore and GNPMP is ₹4,700 crore. Depreciation is ₹200 crore and NIT is ₹300 crore. Find NFIA and national income.","solution":"NFIA = GNPMP − GDPMP = 4,700 − 4,800 = −₹100 crore.\nNNPFC = GNPMP − Depreciation − NIT = 4,700 − 200 − 300 = ₹4,200 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q86"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Nominal GDP is ₹7,200 crore and real GDP is ₹6,000 crore. Calculate the GDP deflator. If nominal GDP later becomes ₹8,100 crore with deflator 125, find real GDP.","solution":"GDP deflator = 7,200/6,000 × 100 = 120.\nLater real GDP = 8,100/125 × 100 = ₹6,480 crore.\nAn index of 125 means prices are 25% above the base-year level, not necessarily 25% above the immediately preceding year.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q87"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"An economy produces only rice and cloth. Base prices are ₹20/kg and ₹50/metre. Current prices are ₹25/kg and ₹60/metre. Current quantities are 100 kg and 80 metres. Find nominal GDP, real GDP and the deflator.","solution":"Nominal GDP = 25 × 100 + 60 × 80 = ₹7,300.\nReal GDP = 20 × 100 + 50 × 80 = ₹6,000.\nDeflator = 7,300/6,000 × 100 = 121.67 (approximately).\nUse current quantities in both measures.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q88"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"GDPFC = ₹5,200 crore; NDPFC = ₹4,900 crore; indirect taxes ₹200 crore; subsidies ₹260 crore; NFIA ₹80 crore. Find depreciation, GDPMP and NNPMP.","solution":"Depreciation = 5,200 − 4,900 = ₹300 crore.\nNIT = 200 − 260 = −₹60 crore.\nGDPMP = GDPFC + NIT = 5,200 − 60 = ₹5,140 crore.\nNNPMP = GDPMP − Depreciation + NFIA = 5,140 − 300 + 80 = ₹4,920 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q89"},{"chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"A firm has domestic sales ₹900 crore, exports ₹100 crore, opening stock ₹80 crore, closing stock ₹110 crore and intermediate purchases ₹430 crore. Find output and GVA MP. Then find NVA FC if depreciation is ₹50 crore and NIT ₹40 crore.","solution":"Total sales = 900 + 100 = ₹1,000 crore.\nChange in stock = 110 − 80 = ₹30 crore.\nOutput = 1,000 + 30 = ₹1,030 crore.\nGVA MP = 1,030 − 430 = ₹600 crore.\nNVA FC = 600 − 50 − 40 = ₹510 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q90"},{"chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"A farmer sells wheat for ₹100 to a mill; the mill sells flour for ₹170 to a bakery; the bakery sells bread for ₹280 to households. No other intermediate inputs exist. Find each producer’s value added and total GDP.","solution":"Farmer GVA = 100 − 0 = ₹100.\nMill GVA = 170 − 100 = ₹70.\nBakery GVA = 280 − 170 = ₹110.\nTotal = ₹280, equal to final bread value. Adding full sales gives ₹550 and double counts ₹270.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q91"},{"chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"Wages in cash = ₹1,000 crore, wages in kind = ₹100 crore, employer social contributions = ₹80 crore, rent and royalty = ₹200 crore, interest on productive loans = ₹120 crore, corporate tax = ₹50 crore, dividends = ₹70 crore, retained earnings = ₹30 crore, mixed income = ₹250 crore and NFIA = −₹40 crore. Find national income.","solution":"Compensation = 1,000 + 100 + 80 = ₹1,180 crore.\nProfit = 50 + 70 + 30 = ₹150 crore.\nOperating surplus = 200 + 120 + 150 = ₹470 crore.\nNDPFC = 1,180 + 470 + 250 = ₹1,900 crore.\nNNPFC = 1,900 − 40 = ₹1,860 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q92"},{"chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"PFCE ₹1,200 crore; GFCE ₹400 crore; gross domestic fixed capital formation ₹500 crore; change in stock −₹20 crore; exports ₹180 crore; imports ₹230 crore; depreciation ₹100 crore; NIT ₹80 crore; NFIA ₹30 crore. Find GDPMP and national income.","solution":"GDCF = 500 − 20 = ₹480 crore.\nNet exports = 180 − 230 = −₹50 crore.\nGDPMP = 1,200 + 400 + 480 − 50 = ₹2,030 crore.\nNNPFC = 2,030 − 100 − 80 + 30 = ₹1,880 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q93"},{"chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"For an economy, total output ₹2,500 crore and intermediate consumption ₹700 crore. PFCE ₹800 crore, GFCE ₹300 crore, GDCF ₹600 crore, exports ₹250 crore and imports ₹150 crore. Show that production and expenditure methods agree.","solution":"Value-added method: GDPMP = 2,500 − 700 = ₹1,800 crore.\nExpenditure method: GDPMP = 800 + 300 + 600 + (250 − 150) = ₹1,800 crore.\nBoth measure the same final production from different sides. Do not add inventories separately when already included in GDCF.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q94"},{"chapter":"5","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Currency held by the public is ₹600 crore; demand deposits ₹900 crore; other deposits with RBI ₹20 crore; net time deposits ₹1,500 crore; post-office savings deposits ₹100 crore. Find M1, M2 and M3 under the textbook definitions.","solution":"M1 = 600 + 900 + 20 = ₹1,520 crore.\nM2 = 1,520 + 100 = ₹1,620 crore.\nM3 = 1,520 + 1,500 = ₹3,020 crore.\nM3 adds time deposits to M1, not to M2.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q95"},{"chapter":"6","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"An initial deposit of ₹2,000 enters the banking system. The required reserve ratio is 10%, there are no excess reserves and every loan is redeposited. Find total deposits, additional credit and reserves in the final position.","solution":"Multiplier = 1/0.10 = 10.\nTotal deposits = 2,000 × 10 = ₹20,000.\nAdditional credit = 20,000 − 2,000 = ₹18,000.\nTotal reserves = 10% × 20,000 = ₹2,000.\nDistinguish total deposits from new credit.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q96"},{"chapter":"6","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Initial deposit is ₹1,000 and reserve ratio falls from 20% to 10%. Find the maximum total deposits before and after, and their increase.","solution":"Before: 1,000/0.20 = ₹5,000.\nAfter: 1,000/0.10 = ₹10,000.\nIncrease = ₹5,000.\nThis assumes the same initial reserves, full lending and complete redepositing.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q97"},{"chapter":"6","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"A bank lends against collateral worth ₹80 lakh. If the margin rises from 20% to 35%, find the maximum loan at each margin and the reduction.","solution":"At 20% margin: loan = 80 × 0.80 = ₹64 lakh.\nAt 35% margin: loan = 80 × 0.65 = ₹52 lakh.\nReduction = ₹12 lakh.\nHigher margin restricts credit against unchanged security.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q98"},{"chapter":"7","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"C = 100 + 0.8Y. At Y = ₹500 crore, find C, S, APC, APS, MPC and MPS.","solution":"C = 100 + 0.8 × 500 = ₹500 crore.\nS = 500 − 500 = 0.\nAPC = 500/500 = 1; APS = 0.\nMPC = 0.8; MPS = 0.2.\nThis is the break-even point, but not necessarily equilibrium.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q99"},{"chapter":"7","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Income rises from ₹400 crore to ₹600 crore and consumption rises from ₹360 crore to ₹510 crore. Find MPC and MPS. Find APC and APS at the new income.","solution":"ΔC = 150; ΔY = 200.\nMPC = 150/200 = 0.75; MPS = 0.25.\nAt Y = 600: APC = 510/600 = 0.85; S = 90; APS = 90/600 = 0.15.\nMPC + MPS = 1 and APC + APS = 1.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q100"},{"chapter":"7","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Saving function is S = −60 + 0.25Y. Derive consumption function and find break-even income. Calculate saving at Y = ₹160 crore.","solution":"C = Y − S = Y − (−60 + 0.25Y) = 60 + 0.75Y.\nAt break-even S = 0: 0.25Y = 60, so Y = ₹240 crore.\nAt Y = 160: S = −60 + 40 = −₹20 crore (dissaving).","tag":"Numerical","id":"q101"},{"chapter":"7","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"At income ₹1,000 crore, APC is 0.9. If income rises by ₹200 crore and MPS is 0.25, find new consumption, saving, APC and APS.","solution":"Initial C = 0.9 × 1,000 = ₹900 crore.\nMPC = 1 − 0.25 = 0.75; ΔC = 0.75 × 200 = ₹150 crore.\nNew C = ₹1,050 crore; new Y = ₹1,200 crore; new S = ₹150 crore.\nNew APC = 1,050/1,200 = 0.875; APS = 0.125.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q102"},{"chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"C = 80 + 0.75Y and autonomous investment is ₹120 crore. Determine equilibrium income, consumption and saving.","solution":"Y = C + I = 80 + 0.75Y + 120.\n0.25Y = 200 ⇒ Y* = ₹800 crore.\nC = 80 + 0.75 × 800 = ₹680 crore.\nS = 800 − 680 = ₹120 crore = I.\nCheck planned S = planned I.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q103"},{"chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"S = −150 + 0.3Y and planned investment is ₹210 crore. Find equilibrium income. At Y = ₹1,000 crore, explain inventory adjustment.","solution":"Equilibrium: −150 + 0.3Y = 210 ⇒ 0.3Y = 360 ⇒ Y = ₹1,200 crore.\nAt Y = 1,000: planned S = ₹150 crore < I = ₹210 crore.\nAD exceeds output by ₹60 crore; inventories fall unexpectedly and firms expand production.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q104"},{"chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Investment rises by ₹80 crore and equilibrium income rises by ₹400 crore. Find multiplier, MPC, MPS and change in consumption.","solution":"k = 400/80 = 5.\nMPS = 1/5 = 0.2; MPC = 0.8.\nΔC = ΔY − ΔI = 400 − 80 = ₹320 crore.\nFinal additional saving is ₹80 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q105"},{"chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"MPC is 0.6 and investment falls by ₹50 crore. Calculate the final changes in income, consumption and saving.","solution":"k = 1/(1 − 0.6) = 2.5.\nΔY = 2.5 × (−50) = −₹125 crore.\nΔC = 0.6 × (−125) = −₹75 crore.\nΔS = −125 − (−75) = −₹50 crore = ΔI.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q106"},{"chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"An investment injection of ₹100 crore occurs with MPC 0.8. State income generated in the first four rounds and the eventual total.","solution":"Rounds: ₹100 crore, ₹80 crore, ₹64 crore, ₹51.2 crore.\nFirst four rounds total = ₹295.2 crore.\nEventual total = 100/(1 − 0.8) = ₹500 crore.\nLater rounds continue; the first four do not equal the final multiplier effect.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q107"},{"chapter":"9","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"C = 100 + 0.8Y, I = ₹200 crore and full-employment income is ₹2,000 crore. Find equilibrium income, the deflationary gap and the required increase in autonomous expenditure.","solution":"Y* = (100 + 200)/0.2 = ₹1,500 crore.\nAt Yf = 2,000: AD = 100 + 0.8 × 2,000 + 200 = ₹1,900 crore.\nDeflationary gap = 2,000 − 1,900 = ₹100 crore.\nIncome shortfall = ₹500 crore. With k = 5, required autonomous expenditure increase = 500/5 = ₹100 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q108"},{"chapter":"9","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"C = 120 + 0.75Y, I = ₹280 crore and Yf = ₹1,200 crore. Determine the demand situation and the autonomous-expenditure correction.","solution":"Notional Y* = (120 + 280)/0.25 = ₹1,600 crore, above capacity.\nAt Yf: AD = 120 + 0.75 × 1,200 + 280 = ₹1,300 crore.\nInflationary gap = 1,300 − 1,200 = ₹100 crore.\nReduce autonomous expenditure by ₹100 crore. Real output cannot rise indefinitely above full employment in this model.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q109"},{"chapter":"9","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Full-employment income is ₹2,400 crore; current equilibrium income is ₹1,800 crore; MPS is 0.25. Find multiplier and the autonomous investment increase needed for full employment.","solution":"k = 1/0.25 = 4.\nRequired ΔY = 2,400 − 1,800 = ₹600 crore.\nΔI = ΔY/k = 600/4 = ₹150 crore.\nThe expenditure gap is ₹150 crore, while the income gap is ₹600 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q110"},{"chapter":"10","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"Revenue receipts = ₹900 crore; revenue expenditure = ₹1,050 crore; capital expenditure = ₹450 crore; non-debt capital receipts = ₹100 crore; interest payments = ₹120 crore. Calculate revenue, fiscal and primary deficits.","solution":"Revenue deficit = 1,050 − 900 = ₹150 crore.\nTotal expenditure = 1,050 + 450 = ₹1,500 crore.\nFiscal deficit = 1,500 − 900 − 100 = ₹500 crore.\nPrimary deficit = 500 − 120 = ₹380 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q111"},{"chapter":"10","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Total expenditure is ₹2,000 crore, fiscal deficit ₹500 crore, non-tax revenue ₹200 crore and non-debt capital receipts ₹100 crore. Find tax revenue.","solution":"Fiscal deficit = TE − Tax revenue − Non-tax revenue − Non-debt capital receipts.\n500 = 2,000 − T − 200 − 100.\nT = 2,000 − 200 − 100 − 500 = ₹1,200 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q112"},{"chapter":"10","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Revenue deficit is ₹80 crore, revenue expenditure ₹600 crore, fiscal deficit ₹200 crore and non-debt capital receipts ₹50 crore. Find revenue receipts and capital expenditure.","solution":"Revenue receipts = RE − RD = 600 − 80 = ₹520 crore.\nTE = FD + RR + NDCR = 200 + 520 + 50 = ₹770 crore.\nCapital expenditure = 770 − 600 = ₹170 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q113"},{"chapter":"10","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Fiscal deficit is ₹700 crore and primary deficit is ₹460 crore. Find interest payments. If revenue receipts are ₹1,000 crore and non-debt capital receipts ₹150 crore, find total expenditure.","solution":"Interest payments = FD − PD = 700 − 460 = ₹240 crore.\nTE = FD + RR + NDCR = 700 + 1,000 + 150 = ₹1,850 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q114"},{"chapter":"11","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"The exchange rate changes from ₹80/$ to ₹84/$. A $500 import is bought at each rate. Find the rupee cost before and after, and identify the domestic currency movement.","solution":"Before: 500 × 80 = ₹40,000.\nAfter: 500 × 84 = ₹42,000.\nRupee cost rises by ₹2,000. The rupee depreciates under a flexible system.\nThe ₹/$ quote rises 5%; the rupee’s dollar value falls by (1 − 80/84) × 100 ≈ 4.76%.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q115"},{"chapter":"11","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"Dollar demand is D = 200 − 2e and supply is S = 20 + e, where e is ₹/$ and quantities are arbitrary units. Find equilibrium. Demand then becomes D = 230 − 2e. Find the new rate and explain the movement.","solution":"Initially: 200 − 2e = 20 + e ⇒ 3e = 180 ⇒ e = ₹60/$; Q = 80.\nAfter shift: 230 − 2e = 20 + e ⇒ 3e = 210 ⇒ e = ₹70/$; Q = 90.\nGreater dollar demand raises ₹/$ and depreciates the rupee.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q116"},{"chapter":"11","type":"numerical","marks":3,"q":"A central bank fixes the rate at ₹70/$. At this rate dollar demand is $100 million and supply is $75 million. Find the intervention needed and its reserve effect.","solution":"Excess dollar demand = 100 − 75 = $25 million.\nThe central bank sells $25 million from reserves to meet the shortage and defend the rate.\nIts foreign-exchange reserves decrease by $25 million. This is not a market-clearing rate without intervention.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q117"},{"chapter":"12","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"Goods exports ₹600 crore, goods imports ₹800 crore, services receipts ₹250 crore, services payments ₹150 crore, factor income receipts ₹40 crore, factor income payments ₹70 crore, transfers received ₹90 crore and transfers paid ₹20 crore. Find trade and current-account balances.","solution":"Trade balance = 600 − 800 = −₹200 crore.\nNet services = 250 − 150 = ₹100 crore.\nNet factor income = 40 − 70 = −₹30 crore.\nNet transfers = 90 − 20 = ₹70 crore.\nCurrent-account balance = −200 + 100 − 30 + 70 = −₹60 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q118"},{"chapter":"12","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"An economy has a current-account deficit of ₹180 crore and autonomous net capital inflow of ₹130 crore. Find its autonomous BoP balance and reserve change if reserves provide the only financing.","solution":"Autonomous balance = −180 + 130 = −₹50 crore.\nA ₹50 crore deficit must be financed by drawing down official reserves.\nReserve assets decrease by ₹50 crore; the accommodating financing entry closes the accounting balance.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q119"},{"chapter":"12","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Exports of goods ₹900 crore, imports ₹850 crore, net services ₹100 crore, net factor income −₹70 crore, net transfers ₹20 crore and autonomous capital-account balance −₹60 crore. Find the current-account and overall autonomous balances.","solution":"Trade balance = 900 − 850 = ₹50 crore.\nCurrent account = 50 + 100 − 70 + 20 = ₹100 crore surplus.\nAutonomous overall balance = 100 − 60 = ₹40 crore surplus.\nIf absorbed solely into reserves, reserve assets rise by ₹40 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q120"},{"chapter":"1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Meaning, origin and principal concerns’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"1.1 ABOUT MACROECONOMICS\n1. Macroeconomics is defined as that branch of economics which studies economic issues or economic problems at the level of an economy as a whole.\n2. The term Macro comes from Makros, meaning large.\n3. Keynes’ The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money (1936) established macroeconomics as a separate branch.\n4. Its principal concerns are employment, inflation, growth of output and depression.\n5. Keynes stressed government intervention when inadequate demand reduces output and employment.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q121"},{"chapter":"1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Microeconomics versus macroeconomics’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"1.2 HOW MACROECONOMICS DIFFERS FROM MICROECONOMICS?\n1. Microeconomics studies scarcity and choice at the level of an individual, household, firm or industry; allocation of resources is its central issue.\n2. Macroeconomics studies these problems for the economy as a whole; determination of overall output and employment is its central issue.\n3. Micro uses partial equilibrium analysis, studying one market while holding other markets constant.\n4. Macro uses general equilibrium analysis, examining simultaneous equilibrium across markets.\n5. An industry is an aggregate, but studying one industry is still microeconomics.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q122"},{"chapter":"1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Micro–macro paradox and interdependence’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Micro-Macro Paradox · Micro-Macro Interdependence\n1. What is logical at the micro level may not be logical at the macro level.\n2. Saving may improve one person’s future prosperity; if everyone saves more by spending less, aggregate demand, investment, output and employment may fall.\n3. Macro affects micro when an RBI interest-rate change affects one producer’s borrowing.\n4. Micro affects macro when individual increases in spending together raise aggregate demand.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q123"},{"chapter":"1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Scope and significance’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"1.3 SCOPE AND SIGNIFICANCE OF MACROECONOMICS\n1. Macroeconomics studies national income, employment, general prices, growth, money, banking, government budgets and international transactions.\n2. It helps explain an economy’s functioning and formulate policies for growth with stability.\n3. Keep the level of analysis clear: the price of one product is micro; the general price level is macro.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q124"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Final goods, intermediate goods and end use’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"2.1 MEANING OF END-USE CLASSIFICATION OF GOODS\n1. Final goods are those goods which have crossed the boundary line of production and are ready for use by their final users.\n2. They lead to final consumption expenditure or investment expenditure.\n3. Intermediate goods are purchased by one firm from another for resale or for use as raw material in producing other goods.\n4. Classification depends on end use, not the physical nature of the good: milk bought by a household is final; milk bought by an ice-cream producer is intermediate.\n5. Unsold additions to inventory count as investment.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q125"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Consumption goods versus capital goods’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"2.1.1 Final Goods · Consumption Goods · Capital Goods\n1. Consumption goods directly satisfy human wants.\n2. They include durable goods, non-durable goods, semi-durable goods and services.\n3. Capital goods are fixed assets of producers, repeatedly used in production: durable-use producer goods such as plant and machinery.\n4. A household car is a durable consumption good; a taxi used to produce transport services is a capital good.\n5. Both are final goods.\n6. A capital good is not intermediate merely because it helps produce something.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q126"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Consumption expenditure and investment’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"2.2 CONSUMPTION EXPENDITURE · 2.3 INVESTMENT\n1. Consumption expenditure includes expenditure by households, government and non-profit private institutions.\n2. Investment is a process of adding to the stock of capital.\n3. Fixed investment adds to fixed assets; inventory investment changes stocks of raw materials, semi-finished goods and finished goods.\n4. New residential construction is investment.\n5. Buying existing shares transfers financial ownership; it does not itself create new capital goods.\n6. Inventory investment can be negative when closing stock is less than opening stock.\nFormula / relationship:\nInventory investment = Closing stock − Opening stock\nGross investment = Fixed investment + Inventory investment","tag":"Topic review","id":"q127"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Gross investment, net investment and depreciation’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"2.3.3 Gross Investment, Net Investment and the Concept of Depreciation\n1. Net investment refers to the increase in the stock of capital during an accounting year.\n2. Gross investment includes replacement of worn-out capital.\n3. Depreciation, also called consumption of fixed capital, refers to loss of value of fixed assets in use through normal wear and tear, normal accidental damage and expected obsolescence.\n4. Unexpected destruction is a capital loss rather than normal depreciation.\n5. A depreciation reserve fund is kept to cope with depreciation losses.\nFormula / relationship:\nNet investment = Gross investment − Depreciation\nDepreciation per year = (Capital value − Scrap value) ÷ Estimated life","tag":"Topic review","id":"q128"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Stocks and flows’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"2.4 STOCKS AND FLOWS\n1. Stocks are quantities measured at a point of time; flows are quantities measured over a specified period of time.\n2. Wealth, money supply, capital and bank balances are stocks.\n3. Income, consumption, investment and saving are flows.\n4. Capital is a stock; investment is the flow adding to it.\n5. “Money supply on 31 March” is a stock even though money changes hands repeatedly.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q129"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Four sectors and circular flow of income’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"2.5 FOUR SECTORS · 2.6 INTERSECTORAL FLOWS · 2.7 CIRCULAR FLOW OF INCOME\n1. The household sector supplies factor services and consumes goods.\n2. The producing sector produces goods and services.\n3. Government acts as a welfare agency and producer; the external sector links the domestic economy with the rest of the world.\n4. Real flows are flows of goods and factor services; money flows are payments and receipts in the opposite direction.\n5. Circular flow of income refers to the circularity of production, income and expenditure across sectors.\n6. In a simple two-sector model, production generates income and income generates expenditure.\nFormula / relationship:\nProduction → Income generation → Expenditure → Production\nValue of output = Factor income = Final expenditure","tag":"Topic review","id":"q130"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘National income, factor incomes and transfer incomes’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"3.1 CONCEPT OF NATIONAL INCOME\n1. National income is the sum total of factor incomes earned by normal residents of a country during the period of an accounting year.\n2. Factor incomes are rewards for factor services: compensation of employees, rent, interest and profit.\n3. Transfer incomes such as gifts, charity and old-age pensions are unilateral receipts without a current productive service in return; they are excluded.\n4. An employer’s retirement pension earned through service differs from an old-age welfare pension.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q131"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Normal residents and domestic territory’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"3.1.3 Normal Residents · 3.2.2 Domestic Territory\n1. A normal resident ordinarily resides in a country and has a centre of economic interest there.\n2. Citizenship alone does not determine residence.\n3. Tourists and visitors for medical treatment do not become residents merely through travel.\n4. Diplomats remain residents of their home countries; border workers are residents where they live.\n5. Domestic territory is economic territory: Indian embassies abroad belong to India’s domestic territory; foreign embassies in India do not.\n6. Check the Ability Zone for ships, aircraft, fishing vessels and other special cases.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q132"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Domestic income versus national income’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"3.2 DOMESTIC AND NATIONAL CONCEPTS OF INCOME\n1. Domestic income is the sum total of factor incomes generated within the domestic territory, whether earned by residents or non-residents.\n2. National income belongs to normal residents, wherever earned.\n3. Net factor income from abroad is factor income received from the rest of the world minus factor income paid to the rest of the world.\n4. Remittances that are gifts are transfers, not NFIA.\nFormula / relationship:\nNNP at FC = NDP at FC + NFIA\nNFIA = Factor income from abroad − Factor income to abroad","tag":"Topic review","id":"q133"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘The three conversion bridges’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"3.3 GROSS AND NET · 3.4 MARKET PRICE AND FACTOR COST\n1. Use three independent adjustments.\n2. Gross to net: subtract depreciation.\n3. Domestic to national: add NFIA.\n4. Market price to factor cost: subtract net indirect taxes, which equal indirect taxes minus subsidies.\n5. Reverse the sign when travelling backwards.\n6. A negative NFIA reduces national income; negative NIT means subsidies exceed indirect taxes.\n7. Do not deduct indirect taxes again if NIT is already supplied.\nFormula / relationship:\nNNPFC = GDPMP − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA\nGDPMP = NNPFC + Depreciation + NIT − NFIA","tag":"Topic review","id":"q134"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Eight related aggregates’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"3.5 AGGREGATES RELATED TO NATIONAL INCOME\n1. GDPMP, GDPFC, NDPMP and NDPFC are domestic measures; GNPMP, GNPFC, NNPMP and NNPFC are national measures.\n2. Gross measures include consumption of fixed capital; net measures exclude it.\n3. Market-price measures include net indirect taxes; factor-cost measures exclude them.\n4. National income means NNPFC; domestic income means NDPFC.\n5. Identify all three labels before substituting a number.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q135"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Nominal GDP, real GDP and GDP deflator’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"3.6 NOMINAL AND REAL GDP\n1. Nominal GDP is valued at current-year prices; real GDP is valued at base-year prices.\n2. Real GDP helps isolate changes in physical production from changes in prices.\n3. Nominal GDP may rise with unchanged output simply because prices rise.\n4. The GDP deflator is the ratio of nominal GDP to real GDP multiplied by 100.\n5. Use the price index as a percentage index, not as a decimal, in the formula below.\nFormula / relationship:\nNominal GDP = Σ(Current price × Current quantity)\nReal GDP = Σ(Base-year price × Current quantity)\nGDP deflator = Nominal GDP ÷ Real GDP × 100\nReal GDP = Nominal GDP ÷ GDP deflator × 100","tag":"Topic review","id":"q136"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘GDP and welfare: why output is an incomplete measure’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"3.7 GDP AND WELFARE\n1. A rise in real GDP can expand available goods and services, but it does not ensure an equal rise in welfare.\n2. The textbook highlights distribution of income, composition of GDP, non-monetary transactions and externalities.\n3. Unequal distribution can leave most people without gains; more military goods may not improve consumption; unpaid household work is omitted; pollution imposes an unpriced cost.\n4. Positive externalities are benefits not paid for; negative externalities are costs not compensated.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q137"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Value added method: measure production once’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"4.2 VALUE ADDED METHOD (OR PRODUCT METHOD)\n1. Value added is value of output minus intermediate consumption.\n2. Value of output equals sales plus change in stock, with own-account production added where applicable.\n3. Sum GVA at market price across producing units to obtain GDPMP.\n4. Deduct depreciation and NIT, then add NFIA for national income.\n5. Alternatively, sum sectoral NVAFC and add NFIA.\nFormula / relationship:\nValue of output = Sales + Closing stock − Opening stock\nGVA MP = Value of output − Intermediate consumption\nNNPFC = ΣGVA MP − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA","tag":"Topic review","id":"q138"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Double counting and value-added precautions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"4.2.2 Precautions · 4.2.3 Problem of Double Counting\n1. Double counting means counting the value of the same output more than once.\n2. Avoid it by counting final goods only or summing value added at each stage.\n3. If wheat sells for 100, flour for 160 and bread for 250, total value added is 100 + 60 + 90 = 250, not 510.\n4. Include own-account construction and imputed rent of owner-occupied houses.\n5. Exclude resale value of second-hand goods, but include the current broker’s service.\n6. Do not count intermediate goods separately from the final product.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q139"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Income method: compensation, surplus and mixed income’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"4.3 INCOME METHOD\n1. Income method measures domestic income as the sum total of factor incomes generated within domestic territory.\n2. Compensation of employees includes wages and salaries in cash and kind and employers’ social-security contributions.\n3. Operating surplus comprises rent and royalty, interest and profit.\n4. Profit includes corporate tax, dividends and retained earnings; these are components, so do not add them again if total profit is given.\n5. Mixed income of the self-employed combines labour and property income that cannot be separated.\nFormula / relationship:\nNDPFC = Compensation of employees + Operating surplus + Mixed income\nOperating surplus = Rent and royalty + Interest + Profit\nNational income = NDPFC + NFIA","tag":"Topic review","id":"q140"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Income-method precautions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"4.3.2 Precautions Regarding Income Method\n1. Include only incomes earned from current productive services.\n2. Exclude transfer receipts, capital gains and windfall gains.\n3. Exclude interest on consumption loans and interest on public debt when it represents a transfer rather than payment for a productive service, following the textbook treatment.\n4. Include imputed rent and production for self-consumption where measured.\n5. Do not count sale proceeds of shares or second-hand goods as factor income; the current commission or brokerage is a service income.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q141"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Expenditure method and its precautions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"4.4 EXPENDITURE METHOD\n1. Expenditure method measures final expenditure on domestically produced output.\n2. Add private final consumption expenditure, government final consumption expenditure, gross domestic capital formation and net exports.\n3. Gross domestic capital formation includes fixed capital formation and change in stocks.\n4. Exclude financial-asset purchases, transfers and second-hand purchases; include current brokerage.\n5. Subtract imports because imported content may already be included in consumption or investment.\n6. Do not add change in stock twice if gross domestic capital formation already includes it.\nFormula / relationship:\nGDPMP = PFCE + GFCE + GDCF + (X − M)\nNNPFC = PFCE + GFCE + GDCF + (X − M) − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA","tag":"Topic review","id":"q142"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Reconciliation and treatment of tricky items’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"4.5 RECONCILIATION OF THREE METHODS · 4.6 TREATMENT OF DIFFERENT ITEMS\n1. The three methods measure the same current production from different sides: value added creates factor income, and income finances final expenditure.\n2. In an exam, classify each item before calculating.\n3. Government services are counted even when provided free; employer provident-fund contributions belong in compensation.\n4. Scholarships and welfare pensions are transfers.\n5. Foreign factor earnings require a residence/territory check; the purchase of a newly produced house is investment.\n6. Read the supplied transaction table for item-by-item domestic and national treatment.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q143"},{"chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Money, barter and the evolution of money’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"5.1 MEANING AND EVOLUTION OF MONEY\n1. Money is generally defined as a thing that is commonly accepted as a medium of exchange.\n2. Barter exchanges goods directly for goods.\n3. Its difficulties include lack of double coincidence of wants, lack of a common measure of value, difficulty storing wealth and difficulty making deferred payments.\n4. Money separates selling from buying: the seller need not want the precise good the buyer offers.\n5. Evolution moves through commodity money, metallic money, paper money and bank/credit money.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q144"},{"chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Fiat, fiduciary and legal-tender money’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"5.2 FORMS OF MONEY\n1. Fiat money is money issued by authority of the government.\n2. Fiduciary money is accepted on trust between payer and payee.\n3. Legal tender must be accepted in discharge of debts within the applicable legal conditions; a cheque depends on acceptance and is not legal tender.\n4. Full-bodied money has face value equal to intrinsic commodity value; credit money has face value greater than intrinsic value.\n5. Follow the textbook distinctions, not the everyday use of the word “cash”.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q145"},{"chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Primary and secondary functions of money’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"5.3 FUNCTIONS OF MONEY\n1. The primary functions are medium of exchange and measure of value (unit of account).\n2. Secondary functions are standard of deferred payments, store of value and transfer of value.\n3. Buying a book illustrates medium of exchange; quoting its price illustrates measure of value; paying a loan next year illustrates deferred payment; keeping purchasing power for later illustrates store of value.\n4. Inflation reduces the purchasing power stored in money.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q146"},{"chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Money supply and its components’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"5.4 SUPPLY OF MONEY\n1. Money supply is the stock of money held by the public at a particular point of time.\n2. Interbank deposits and money held by the government/banking system are excluded from money held by the public.\n3. M1 comprises currency with the public, demand deposits with commercial banks and other deposits with the RBI.\n4. Demand deposits are withdrawable on demand.\n5. A cheque transfers a deposit; it is not an additional independent stock of money.\nFormula / relationship:\nM1 = Currency with public + Demand deposits with banks + Other deposits with RBI","tag":"Topic review","id":"q147"},{"chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Ability Zone: broader measures and liquidity’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Measures of Money Supply · Liquidity\n1. The textbook also gives M2, M3 and M4 for reference.\n2. M2 adds post-office savings-bank deposits to M1.\n3. M3 adds net time deposits with commercial banks to M1 and is called aggregate monetary resources.\n4. M4 adds total post-office deposits, excluding National Savings Certificates, to M3.\n5. Liquidity means convertibility into cash: money is the most liquid asset.\n6. The textbook’s monetary-system notes discuss paper currency, limited/unlimited legal tender and the minimum reserve system; the original page is available to read exactly.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q148"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Commercial banks and their functions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"6.1 COMMERCIAL BANKS\n1. Commercial banks accept deposits and advance loans with the objective of earning profit.\n2. Deposits include current, savings and fixed deposits.\n3. Primary functions are accepting deposits and lending; other functions include agency services and general-utility services.\n4. Banks mobilise saving and create demand deposits through lending.\n5. A central bank is the apex institution regulating the monetary and financial system; its objective is growth with stability rather than commercial profit.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q149"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Credit creation: follow every round’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"6.2 MONEY CREATION BY THE COMMERCIAL BANKS\n1. Suppose an initial cash deposit of 1,000 enters the banking system and the reserve ratio is 20%.\n2. The first bank keeps 200 and lends 800.\n3. If 800 is redeposited, the next bank keeps 160 and lends 640.\n4. Repeated lending and redepositing expand total deposits to 5,000.\n5. These results assume no cash leakage, no excess reserves and loans redeposited in the banking system.\n6. Total deposits include the initial deposit; additional credit excludes it.\nFormula / relationship:\nDeposit multiplier = 1 ÷ Reserve ratio\nTotal deposits = Initial deposit ÷ Reserve ratio\nAdditional credit = Total deposits − Initial deposit","tag":"Topic review","id":"q150"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Central bank: seven functions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK\n1. The textbook lists issuer of currency, banker to the government, bankers’ bank and supervisor, lender of last resort, custodian of foreign exchange, clearing-house function and control of credit.\n2. As government banker it holds accounts, receives money, makes payments and manages borrowing.\n3. As bankers’ bank it holds bank reserves and supports banks.\n4. Clearing settles interbank claims.\n5. Lender of last resort provides emergency assistance when normal sources cannot meet a bank’s needs.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q151"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Quantitative instruments: rates and open market operations’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"6.4.1 Quantitative Instruments of Credit Control\n1. Bank rate, repo rate and reverse repo rate influence the cost and placement of funds.\n2. The textbook distinguishes bank-rate lending without collateral from repo lending against securities under a repurchase agreement.\n3. Raising lending rates tends to raise borrowing cost and reduce credit demand; lowering them works in the opposite direction.\n4. RBI sales of securities absorb liquidity and reduce credit creation; RBI purchases release liquidity and increase it.\n5. These are textbook mechanisms, not a claim about today’s policy rates.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q152"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Reserve ratios and qualitative instruments’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"6.4.1 Quantitative Instruments · Qualitative Instruments\n1. CRR is the percentage of deposits banks must keep with the RBI.\n2. SLR requires banks to maintain prescribed liquid assets such as cash, gold and approved securities.\n3. Higher reserve requirements constrain lending; lower requirements release lending capacity.\n4. Margin requirement is the difference between collateral value and loan value.\n5. Higher margins reduce permitted loans.\n6. Rationing fixes credit quotas, while moral suasion uses persuasion and pressure to influence bank lending.\n7. Quantitative instruments affect overall credit; qualitative instruments target its allocation.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q153"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Ability Zone: when monetary tools succeed’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Success of Repo Rate · Success of Open Market Operations\n1. Repo-rate policy depends on banks’ reliance on RBI loans, responsiveness of credit demand, the wider structure of interest rates and availability of non-bank funds.\n2. Open market operations require a functioning securities market and an effective link between reserve changes and lending.\n3. Explaining a policy requires the transmission chain: policy change → bank funds or borrowing cost → credit → expenditure → aggregate demand.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q154"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Aggregate demand and aggregate supply’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"7.1 CONCEPT OF AD · 7.2 COMPONENTS OF AD · 7.3 CONCEPT OF AS\n1. Aggregate demand is the sum total of demand for all goods and services during an accounting year, measured as planned expenditure.\n2. In a two-sector economy AD = C + I; a closed economy with government adds G; an open economy adds X − M.\n3. Aggregate supply refers to production planned by producers.\n4. In the textbook’s short-run model with constant prices and spare capacity, AS is identified with income/output and represented by the 45° line.\nFormula / relationship:\nAD = C + I + G + (X − M)\nAS = Y = C + S","tag":"Topic review","id":"q155"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Consumption function: autonomous and induced consumption’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"7.4 CONSUMPTION FUNCTION\n1. Consumption function is the functional relationship between consumption and income.\n2. Autonomous consumption is consumption when income is zero; induced consumption is the part that varies with income.\n3. In C = C̄ + bY, the vertical intercept is C̄ and the slope b is MPC.\n4. Consumption rises with income, but normally by less than the rise in income.\n5. A change in income moves along a given function; a change in autonomous consumption shifts it.\nFormula / relationship:\nC = C̄ + bY\nMPC = ΔC ÷ ΔY\nInduced consumption = bY","tag":"Topic review","id":"q156"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘APC and MPC: totals versus increments’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"APC vs. MPC\n1. Average propensity to consume is total consumption divided by total income; marginal propensity to consume is additional consumption divided by additional income.\n2. APC may exceed one when consumption exceeds income.\n3. MPC is the slope of the consumption curve; APC is not.\n4. With positive autonomous consumption and constant MPC below one, APC falls as income rises.\n5. At zero income APC is undefined.\nFormula / relationship:\nAPC = C ÷ Y\nMPC = ΔC ÷ ΔY","tag":"Topic review","id":"q157"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Saving function and the break-even point’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"SAVING FUNCTION · Break-even Point\n1. Saving is income not consumed.\n2. Derive S by subtracting the entire consumption function from Y.\n3. The saving function has a negative intercept equal to autonomous consumption and a slope equal to MPS.\n4. At break-even income C = Y and S = 0: APC is one and APS is zero.\n5. Below it saving is negative; above it saving is positive.\n6. Break-even is not necessarily equilibrium, because equilibrium also depends on investment.\nFormula / relationship:\nS = Y − C = −C̄ + (1 − b)Y\nBreak-even income = C̄ ÷ (1 − b)","tag":"Topic review","id":"q158"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘APS, MPS and the two identities’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"APS vs. MPS · Relationship between Propensity to Consume and Propensity to Save\n1. Average propensity to save is S/Y; marginal propensity to save is ΔS/ΔY.\n2. APS can be negative when households dissave.\n3. MPS is the slope of the saving function.\n4. Since income is split between consumption and saving, APC + APS = 1 and MPC + MPS = 1.\n5. Do not confuse ΔS (change in saving) with AS (aggregate supply): similar letters represent different concepts.\nFormula / relationship:\nAPS = S ÷ Y; MPS = ΔS ÷ ΔY\nAPC + APS = 1; MPC + MPS = 1","tag":"Topic review","id":"q159"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Investment and deriving one function from the other’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"INVESTMENT · ABILITY ZONE: Derivation of C-function from S-function\n1. In the simple model autonomous investment is independent of income, so its curve is horizontal.\n2. Induced investment responds to changes in income or activity.\n3. The expected profitability of investment and rate of interest influence investment decisions.\n4. To obtain C from S, use C = Y − S; to obtain S from C, use S = Y − C.\n5. The two slopes add to one and their vertical intercepts have equal magnitudes with opposite signs.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q160"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Short run and equilibrium output’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"8.1 CONCEPT OF SHORT RUN · 8.2 CONCEPT OF EQUILIBRIUM OUTPUT\n1. The textbook’s short run assumes constant technology and a given capital stock; changes in employment change output.\n2. Equilibrium output is the level where intended production equals intended expenditure.\n3. Producers have no unplanned change in inventories and no incentive to change output.\n4. In the two-sector economy Y = C + I and Y = C + S, so the same equilibrium condition can be written S = I.\nFormula / relationship:\nY = AD = C + I\nC + S = C + I ⇒ S = I\nY* = (C̄ + I) ÷ (1 − MPC)","tag":"Topic review","id":"q161"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘How inventories restore equilibrium’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Equilibrium GDP · Inventory Adjustment\n1. When AD is below AS, goods remain unsold and actual inventories exceed desired inventories.\n2. Producers cut output and employment until planned saving equals planned investment.\n3. When AD exceeds AS, inventories fall unexpectedly and producers expand output, provided spare capacity exists.\n4. The direction is easy to reverse by mistake: excess supply causes inventory accumulation; excess demand causes inventory depletion.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q162"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Ex-ante versus ex-post saving and investment’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Ex-ante Saving and Ex-ante Investment\n1. Ex-ante means planned; ex-post means realised.\n2. Planned saving and planned investment need not be equal away from equilibrium.\n3. Actual saving and actual investment are equal by accounting identity because realised investment includes unintended inventory changes.\n4. Equilibrium requires equality of planned S and planned I, not merely the ex-post identity.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q163"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Effective demand and underemployment equilibrium’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Effective Demand · Underemployment Equilibrium\n1. Effective demand is aggregate demand at the equilibrium level of income/output.\n2. Equilibrium can occur below full employment: firms’ production plans can match spending plans while workers and productive capacity remain idle.\n3. An increase in autonomous expenditure shifts AD upward and raises equilibrium output in the model with spare capacity; a fall lowers it.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q164"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Investment multiplier: size and derivation’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"8.5 INVESTMENT MULTIPLIER\n1. Investment multiplier is the ratio of change in income to change in investment.\n2. Starting with ΔY = ΔC + ΔI and ΔC = MPC × ΔY gives ΔY(1 − MPC) = ΔI.\n3. Higher MPC means more income is respent, so the multiplier is larger.\n4. Higher MPS means more income leaks into saving, so the multiplier is smaller.\n5. With MPC zero, k = 1; as MPC approaches one, k becomes very large.\nFormula / relationship:\nk = ΔY ÷ ΔI = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ MPS\nΔY = k × ΔI","tag":"Topic review","id":"q165"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Multiplier mechanism, assumptions and reverse operation’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Multiplier Mechanism\n1. An investment injection is someone’s income.\n2. They consume a fraction, which becomes someone else’s income; successive rounds generate ΔI, MPC × ΔI, MPC² × ΔI and so on.\n3. Saving reduces each next round.\n4. The simple multiplier assumes constant MPC, available spare capacity, constant prices, and no additional leakages such as imports or taxes.\n5. A fall in investment works in reverse, producing a multiplied fall in income.\n6. The final increase in saving equals the initial increase in investment.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q166"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Full employment and unemployment’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"9.1 SOME CRITICAL CONCEPTS\n1. Full employment means those able and willing to work at the existing wage obtain work.\n2. Voluntary unemployment reflects unwillingness to work at the existing wage; involuntary unemployment means willing workers cannot find jobs.\n3. The textbook also distinguishes natural unemployment: frictional unemployment during job transitions and structural unemployment caused by changing industries, skills or production techniques.\n4. Underemployment equilibrium has AD = AS with unutilised capacity.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q167"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Deficient demand and deflationary gap’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"DEFICIENT DEMAND · Deflationary Gap\n1. Deficient demand occurs when aggregate demand is less than the aggregate supply corresponding to full employment.\n2. The deflationary gap is the shortfall of AD from the level required to maintain full employment; measure it vertically at full-employment output.\n3. It reduces output and employment and creates involuntary unemployment.\n4. The gap in expenditure is not the same as the shortfall in equilibrium income: the multiplier links the two.\nFormula / relationship:\nDeflationary gap = Yf − AD at Yf\nRequired increase in autonomous expenditure = (Yf − Y*) ÷ k","tag":"Topic review","id":"q168"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Excess demand and inflationary gap’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"EXCESS DEMAND · Inflationary Gap\n1. Excess demand occurs when AD exceeds the demand required to sustain full employment.\n2. The inflationary gap is the excess of AD over full-employment AS, measured at Yf.\n3. Once resources are fully employed, real output cannot expand in the simple model; excess spending raises the general price level.\n4. A calculated equilibrium above Yf is a signal of inflationary pressure, not evidence that the economy can actually produce beyond its capacity.\nFormula / relationship:\nInflationary gap = AD at Yf − Yf","tag":"Topic review","id":"q169"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Fiscal measures: expenditure, taxation and borrowing’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Fiscal Policy\n1. To correct deficient demand, raise government expenditure, reduce taxes, reduce public borrowing and increase borrowing from the central bank, following the textbook framework.\n2. These measures raise spending power or public expenditure.\n3. For excess demand, reverse the direction.\n4. Explain the chain: lower taxes → higher disposable income → more consumption → higher AD.\n5. Borrowing from the public can withdraw purchasing power; borrowing from the central bank can expand it.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q170"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Monetary measures: cost and availability of credit’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Monetary Policy\n1. For deficient demand, lower bank/repo/reverse-repo rates, lower CRR and SLR, buy securities, lower margins, relax credit rationing and encourage lending.\n2. For excess demand, raise rates and reserve requirements, sell securities, raise margins and restrict lending.\n3. Monetary policy is the central bank’s policy; fiscal policy is the government’s revenue and expenditure policy.\n4. Always connect the instrument to credit and then to aggregate demand.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q171"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Government budget and its objectives’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"10.1 CONCEPT OF GOVERNMENT BUDGET · 10.2 OBJECTIVES\n1. Government budget is a statement of expected receipts and expected expenditure of the government for the coming financial year, revealing its budgetary policy.\n2. Objectives include GDP growth, allocation of resources, redistribution of income and wealth, economic stability, management of public enterprises and employment generation.\n3. Public infrastructure can encourage private investment; progressive taxation and social spending can reduce inequality.\n4. Fiscal discipline means managing public finances responsibly to avoid unsustainable deficits and debt.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q172"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Revenue receipts versus capital receipts’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"10.3.1 Budget Receipts\n1. Revenue receipts neither create a liability nor reduce assets.\n2. They include tax revenue and non-tax revenue such as fees, fines, interest, profits/dividends and grants.\n3. Capital receipts create a liability or reduce assets: borrowing creates liabilities, while loan recovery and disinvestment reduce assets.\n4. Do not classify a receipt by size or regularity alone; use the liability/asset test.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q173"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Direct taxes, indirect taxes and non-tax revenue’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Tax Revenue · Non-tax Revenue\n1. Direct taxes have impact and incidence on the same person, such as income tax and corporation tax.\n2. Indirect taxes allow the burden to be shifted, such as GST.\n3. A fee is a payment for a specific government service; a fine penalises an offence.\n4. A tax is compulsory and does not promise a direct equivalent service.\n5. Progressive taxes take a larger proportion as income rises; proportional taxes keep the same proportion; regressive burdens fall relatively more heavily on lower incomes.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q174"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Revenue expenditure versus capital expenditure’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"10.3.2 Budget Expenditure\n1. Revenue expenditure neither creates assets nor reduces liabilities.\n2. Salaries, pensions, subsidies and interest payments are examples.\n3. Capital expenditure creates assets or reduces liabilities: construction of a highway, purchase of equipment, lending by government and repayment of loan principal.\n4. Repayment of principal is capital expenditure; interest is revenue expenditure.\n5. Grants to states remain revenue expenditure for the granting government even if the recipient uses them to build assets, following the textbook convention.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q175"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Revenue deficit, fiscal deficit and primary deficit’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"10.4 BUDGET DEFICIT: REVENUE DEFICIT, FISCAL DEFICIT AND PRIMARY DEFICIT\n1. Revenue deficit is the excess of revenue expenditure over revenue receipts.\n2. Fiscal deficit is total expenditure minus total receipts excluding borrowings; it measures borrowing requirements.\n3. Primary deficit deducts interest payments from fiscal deficit and indicates the current borrowing requirement excluding past-debt interest.\n4. A zero revenue deficit does not guarantee zero fiscal deficit.\n5. A zero primary deficit means borrowing covers interest payments.\nFormula / relationship:\nRevenue deficit = Revenue expenditure − Revenue receipts\nFiscal deficit = Total expenditure − Revenue receipts − Non-debt capital receipts\nPrimary deficit = Fiscal deficit − Interest payments","tag":"Topic review","id":"q176"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Implications, balanced and unbalanced budgets’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"10.5 BALANCED AND UNBALANCED BUDGET\n1. A revenue deficit indicates government dissaving: borrowing may finance current consumption rather than asset creation.\n2. Fiscal deficits may raise debt and interest burdens and crowd out private borrowing; financing expenditure can also support demand and productive capacity, depending on its use.\n3. A balanced budget has receipts equal to expenditure; a surplus budget has receipts above expenditure and can restrain inflation; a deficit budget can support demand in recession.\n4. Consider both the size and composition of expenditure.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q177"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Ability Zone: public, private, common and club goods’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Public Goods, Private Goods, Common Goods and Club Goods\n1. Public goods are non-rival and non-excludable, such as national defence.\n2. Private goods are rival and excludable.\n3. Common goods are rival but non-excludable, such as fish in an open pond.\n4. Club goods are excludable but non-rival until congestion, such as a subscription service.\n5. Rivalry means one person’s use reduces availability to others; excludability means users can be denied access.\n6. Public expenditure supports infrastructure, redistribution, stability and an investment-friendly environment.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q178"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Foreign exchange and the quotation convention’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"11.1 FOREIGN EXCHANGE AND FOREIGN EXCHANGE RATE\n1. Foreign exchange refers to foreign currency.\n2. Foreign exchange rate is the price paid in domestic currency for a unit of foreign currency.\n3. Throughout these lessons, quote the rate as ₹ per US dollar.\n4. A rise from ₹80/$ to ₹84/$ means the rupee buys fewer dollars and has depreciated; a fall to ₹76/$ means rupee appreciation.\n5. Always identify which currency is in the numerator before describing a movement.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q179"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Demand, supply and flexible exchange-rate determination’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"11.2.1 Flexible Exchange Rate\n1. Flexible exchange rate is determined by demand and supply in the foreign exchange market.\n2. Demand for foreign exchange comes from imports, overseas investment, foreign travel, loan repayment, income payments, grants and speculation.\n3. Supply comes from exports, foreign investment, inward loans, tourism receipts, income receipts, grants and remittances.\n4. With ₹/$ on the vertical axis, demand slopes downward and supply upward; their intersection is the equilibrium rate.\n5. A higher exchange rate makes foreign goods costlier in rupees and normally reduces import-related foreign-exchange demand.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q180"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Movements versus shifts, appreciation versus depreciation’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Appreciation and Depreciation of Domestic Currency\n1. A change in the exchange rate causes movement along a given demand or supply curve.\n2. A change in imports, investment preferences, income or foreign demand shifts a curve.\n3. Greater demand for dollars shifts demand right, raising ₹/$ and depreciating the rupee.\n4. Greater supply of dollars shifts supply right, lowering ₹/$ and appreciating the rupee.\n5. Depreciation can make exports cheaper for foreigners and imports costlier for residents; actual quantity responses depend on demand conditions.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q181"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Fixed rate, devaluation and revaluation’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"11.2.2 Fixed Exchange Rate\n1. Fixed exchange rate is set and maintained by the monetary authority.\n2. An official rise in ₹/$ is devaluation of the rupee; an official fall is revaluation.\n3. Depreciation/appreciation describe market-driven changes under a flexible system.\n4. To defend a fixed rate when demand for dollars exceeds supply, the authority sells foreign currency from reserves; when dollar supply exceeds demand it buys foreign currency.\n5. An overvalued domestic currency tends to create excess demand for foreign currency at the official rate.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q182"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Managed floating and merits/demerits’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"11.3 MANAGED FLOATING · 11.4 MERITS AND DEMERITS\n1. Managed floating combines market determination with occasional central-bank intervention to limit undesirable movements; the textbook also calls it dirty floating.\n2. Fixed rates reduce exchange uncertainty and can support trade but require reserves and may misallocate resources.\n3. Flexible rates need less reserve defence and adjust through markets but create uncertainty and exposure to external shocks.\n4. The original comparison covers the full list of merits and demerits.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q183"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Foreign exchange market: transfer, credit and hedging’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"11.7 FOREIGN EXCHANGE MARKET\n1. The foreign exchange market is the market for national currencies.\n2. The transfer function moves purchasing power between countries.\n3. The credit function supports trade through financing arrangements.\n4. Hedging reduces exchange-rate risk.\n5. Spot transactions exchange currency for current delivery; forward transactions agree today on a rate for delivery at a future date.\n6. A forward contract fixes an exchange rate; it does not guarantee that the future spot rate will equal that rate.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q184"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Balance of payments: account and recording rule’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"12.1 BoP (or BoP ACCOUNTS)\n1. Balance of payments is a statement of accounts showing all monetary transactions of a country with the rest of the world during a period of time, generally one year.\n2. It records goods, services, income, transfers and asset-related transactions between residents and non-residents.\n3. Foreign-exchange receipts are credit items; payments are debit items.\n4. The textbook uses current, capital and official-reserves accounts.\n5. Keep its school-level account terminology when answering.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q185"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Current account, trade balance and invisibles’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"12.2.1 Current Account\n1. Current account records exports and imports of goods and services, factor income and current transfers.\n2. Goods are visible trade or merchandise; services, factor incomes and transfers are invisibles.\n3. Balance of trade covers goods only.\n4. A gift from abroad is a current transfer; wages for a service are factor income.\n5. The supplied purple box treats direct tourist purchases as invisible trade: preserve that convention for this textbook.\nFormula / relationship:\nTrade balance = Goods exports − Goods imports\nCurrent-account balance = Trade balance + Net services + Net factor income + Net current transfers","tag":"Topic review","id":"q186"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Capital account and asset/liability changes’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"12.2.2 Capital Account\n1. Capital account records transactions that change a country’s assets or liabilities in relation to the rest of the world.\n2. The textbook includes borrowing, foreign investment, NRI deposits, banking capital and short-term debt.\n3. Inward FDI or an external loan is a credit; an outward investment or repayment of foreign loan principal is a debit.\n4. Interest on a foreign loan belongs in the current account; the principal belongs in the capital account.\n5. FDI involves a lasting productive interest/control; portfolio investment purchases financial securities.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q187"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Official reserves, deficit and surplus’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"12.2.3 Official Reserves Account\n1. If autonomous foreign-exchange payments exceed autonomous receipts, there is a BoP deficit, financed by accommodating transactions such as a drawdown of reserves or official borrowing.\n2. A surplus can increase official reserves.\n3. A decrease in reserve assets finances a shortfall; an increase uses foreign exchange.\n4. Follow the sign convention explicitly stated in a numerical instead of confusing a positive stock increase with a credit entry.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q188"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Autonomous versus accommodating transactions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"12.3.1 Autonomous and Accommodating Items of BoP Account\n1. Autonomous transactions are undertaken for independent economic motives such as profit, rather than to correct a BoP imbalance; they are above-the-line items.\n2. Accommodating transactions are undertaken to finance or correct the imbalance and are below the line.\n3. A foreign loan may be autonomous when raised commercially or accommodating when arranged specifically to finance a deficit.\n4. Classification depends on purpose, not merely the type of transaction.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q189"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Accounting balance versus operational balance’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Balance of Payments: Accounting Sense and Operational Sense\n1. BoP balances in the accounting sense because every transaction has a counterpart and financing entries close the accounts.\n2. This does not mean autonomous receipts equal autonomous payments or that the economy has no external problem.\n3. A current-account deficit financed by foreign borrowing is consistent with accounting balance and increasing indebtedness.\n4. Read the Ability Zone worked table to see a current-account deficit offset by capital inflows.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q190"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Causes and significance of disequilibrium’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"12.3.2 Significance of BoP Accounts · Causes of Disequilibrium\n1. Economic causes include large development imports, business cycles, domestic inflation, import substitution and changes in trading partners’ costs.\n2. Political instability can discourage foreign investment; policy changes can increase imports.\n3. Social causes include changes in tastes and cross-border preferences.\n4. BoP data show external strengths, weaknesses, debt and reserve pressures and help formulate policy.\n5. A trade deficit alone does not tell you the whole BoP position.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q191"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"“An increase in GDP necessarily raises welfare.” Defend or refute with four textbook points.","solution":"Refute.\n1. Distribution of income: growth concentrated among a few may not improve most people’s welfare.\n2. Composition of GDP: output may rise through goods that do not improve ordinary consumption.\n3. Non-monetary transactions: unpaid household services are omitted.\n4. Externalities: pollution costs and unpaid external benefits are not fully reflected in GDP.\nUse “limitations of GDP as an index of welfare” as the answer heading.","tag":"Theory","id":"q192"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"State six precautions when estimating national income by the expenditure method.","solution":"Precautions regarding Expenditure Method\n1. Count final expenditure only; exclude intermediate expenditure.\n2. Exclude purchases of second-hand goods; include current brokerage.\n3. Exclude financial-asset purchases such as existing shares and bonds.\n4. Exclude transfer payments; the recipient’s later purchase of output is separate.\n5. Include own-account investment and imputed services where measured.\n6. Subtract imports and include exports to isolate domestic production.\nAvoid adding change in stock twice if GDCF already includes it.","tag":"Theory","id":"q193"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain how an increase in CRR can control excess demand.","solution":"Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR)\n1. CRR is the required percentage of bank deposits kept with the RBI.\n2. Higher CRR means more required reserves against the same deposits.\n3. Banks’ capacity to extend credit and create deposits falls.\n4. Lower credit availability restrains consumption and investment expenditure, reducing AD and inflationary pressure.","tag":"Theory","id":"q194"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"“Ex-post saving equals ex-post investment, so every economy is always in equilibrium.” Is the conclusion valid?","solution":"Refute the conclusion.\nEx-post Saving and Investment: realised saving equals realised investment because actual investment includes unplanned inventory changes.\nEx-ante Saving and Investment: equilibrium requires equality of planned saving and planned investment.\nIf output exceeds planned expenditure, inventories accumulate unexpectedly; firms reduce production. The ex-post identity holds even while firms are adjusting away from disequilibrium.","tag":"Theory","id":"q195"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Distinguish deficient demand and excess demand under the headings meaning, gap, effects and correction.","solution":"1. Meaning: deficient demand is AD below the full-employment requirement; excess demand is AD above it.\n2. Gap: deficient demand creates a deflationary gap; excess demand creates an inflationary gap, each measured at full-employment output.\n3. Effects: deficient demand lowers output and employment; excess demand raises prices when output is at capacity.\n4. Correction: expand public spending and ease credit for deficient demand; restrain public spending and tighten credit for excess demand.","tag":"Theory","id":"q196"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Distinguish revenue receipts and capital receipts, giving two examples of each.","solution":"Revenue Receipts\nNeither create a liability nor reduce assets. Examples: income tax and government fees.\nCapital Receipts\nCreate a liability or reduce assets. Examples: borrowing creates a liability; recovery of government loans reduces a financial asset.\nThe distinction rests on the asset/liability effect, not the amount or frequency of the receipt.","tag":"Theory","id":"q197"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"An increase in foreign investment enters an economy with a flexible exchange rate. Explain the foreign-exchange market effect using ₹ per dollar as the quotation.","solution":"Supply of Foreign Exchange\nForeign investment brings an inflow of foreign currency, shifting dollar supply to the right.\nDetermination of Flexible Exchange Rate\nWith dollar demand unchanged, the equilibrium ₹/$ rate falls and the equilibrium quantity of dollars increases.\nAppreciation of Domestic Currency\nFewer rupees are required for one dollar; the rupee appreciates. Draw ₹/$ vertically, dollars horizontally, a downward D curve and S shifting right.","tag":"Case-based","id":"q198"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"A country finances a current-account deficit through overseas borrowing. Explain why BoP accounting balance does not prove a sound external position.","solution":"Balance of Payments: Accounting Sense and Operational Sense\n1. The loan produces a capital-account inflow that can offset the current-account deficit.\n2. Double-entry recording and financing entries make the accounts balance.\n3. The borrowing increases external liabilities and future interest/principal obligations.\n4. Accounting balance does not establish equality of autonomous receipts and payments or absence of external vulnerability.","tag":"Case-based","id":"q199"},{"id":"q200","chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Types of consumption goods using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"2.1.1 (a) Consumption Goods or Consumer Goods\nDurable-use consumer goods serve consumers repeatedly over a long period, such as cars and refrigerators. Semi-durable goods serve over a shorter period, such as clothes. Non-durable goods are used up in a single act of consumption, such as food. Services are intangible acts satisfying wants, such as teaching or medical treatment. Durability alone does not make an item a capital good: capital goods are used by producers in production.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q201","chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Why final goods are counted and intermediate goods are not using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Significance of the Distinction between Intermediate Goods and Final Goods\nIntermediate inputs are embodied in final output. Counting both adds the same value more than once. If cloth worth ₹5,000 becomes shirts worth ₹7,000, final output is ₹7,000, not ₹12,000. Under the value-added method, the cloth producer’s value and the shirt producer’s additional ₹2,000 are each counted once. It is incorrect to say intermediate goods contribute nothing: their contribution is included in final value.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q202","chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Special cases of residents and territory using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Normal Residents of a Country · Components of Domestic Territory\nNormal Residents: citizens of another country can be residents here when their centre of economic interest is here; officials and diplomats serving abroad remain residents of their home country; border workers are residents where they live. Domestic Territory: include a country’s embassies, consulates and military establishments abroad, ships and aircraft operated by its residents between countries, and fishing vessels and floating platforms operated by residents in international waters under the accounting conventions. Exclude foreign embassies and international organisations located inside its political borders. Residence and territory are separate tests.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q203","chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Steps under the three measurement methods using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"4.2.1 Steps · 4.3.1 Steps · 4.4.1 Steps\nValue Added Method: identify and classify producing units; estimate each unit’s output and intermediate consumption; obtain sectoral value added; aggregate and make depreciation, tax and NFIA adjustments to national income. Income Method: identify producing enterprises; classify factor incomes into compensation, operating surplus and mixed income; sum them to obtain domestic income; add NFIA. Expenditure Method: identify final expenditure by sectors; classify consumption, investment and net exports; sum to GDPMP; convert to NNPFC. Keep the starting aggregate clear because the adjustments depend on whether it is gross/net and market price/factor cost.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q204","chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Why money solves barter’s difficulties using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"5.1.3 Barter System of Exchange · 5.3 Functions of Money\nLack of Double Coincidence of Wants: money is accepted as an intermediary, so you can sell to one person and buy from another. Lack of a Common Measure of Value: prices expressed in money make values comparable. Difficulty in Storing Wealth: money offers a convenient store, though inflation can erode it. Difficulty in Deferred Payments: future payments can be fixed in monetary units. Difficulty in Transfer of Value: purchasing power can be transferred without moving bulky goods. In theory answers, state the barter difficulty first and then the function that addresses it.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q205","chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Issuer of currency and banker to government using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK · Functions\nIssuer of Currency: the central bank has the principal note-issuing authority; in India, the ₹1 note and coins are issued by the Government of India. Centralised issue promotes uniformity and control. Banker to Government: the RBI keeps government deposits, receives payments, makes disbursements, advances funds within the applicable arrangements and manages public debt. Financial adviser is a related role. Distinguish issuing authority from circulation: government-issued coins enter circulation through the monetary system.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q206","chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Bankers’ bank, lender of last resort and clearing house using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK · Functions\nBankers’ Bank and Supervisory Role: commercial banks maintain required cash balances with the central bank, which regulates their functioning. Lender of Last Resort: the central bank offers financial accommodation in emergencies when other sources are unavailable. Clearing House: it settles interbank claims by adjusting banks’ accounts and facilitates fund transfers. Custodian of Foreign Exchange: it manages the country’s foreign-exchange reserves and can intervene in the exchange market. Control of Credit: it regulates the cost and availability of credit to support growth with stability.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q207","chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Ranges of APC, MPC, APS and MPS using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Comparative Table of APC, MPC, APS and MPS · Critical Questions\nAPC can exceed one when C exceeds Y; it equals one at break-even and is below one when saving is positive. APS is negative when saving is negative, zero at break-even and positive when saving is positive; with positive consumption it remains below one. MPC and MPS range from zero to one in the textbook model and add to one. At the endpoints, MPC = 1 implies MPS = 0 and all additional income is consumed; MPC = 0 implies MPS = 1 and all additional income is saved. Do not infer a marginal ratio from a single total-income observation.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q208","chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Break-even income versus equilibrium income using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Consumption Function · Equilibrium Output\nBreak-even Income: consumption equals income, so saving is zero; use C = Y. Equilibrium Income: planned output equals planned expenditure, so saving equals investment; use Y = C + I or S = I. With positive autonomous investment and positive MPS, equilibrium income exceeds break-even income. The two coincide only when autonomous investment is zero in the simple model. If you are asked for dissaving at zero income, solve for autonomous consumption and report saving as its negative.\nBreak-even Y = C̄/MPS\nEquilibrium Y = (C̄ + I)/MPS","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q209","chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Budget objectives: the headings to reproduce using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"10.2 OBJECTIVES OF GOVERNMENT BUDGET\nGDP Growth: create infrastructure and induce private investment through suitable incentives. Allocation of Resources: influence production through taxes, subsidies and public provision. Provision of Public Goods: provide collective services that markets may undersupply. Redistribution of Income and Wealth: use progressive taxation and welfare spending. Balanced Regional Growth: direct infrastructure and incentives to less-developed regions. Employment Opportunities: expand activity and labour demand. Economic Stability: raise spending during deficient demand and restrain it during excess demand. Select and explain the number of objectives requested, using these headings.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q210","chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Free-rider problem using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Free Rider Problem\nA free rider enjoys the benefits of goods and services without contributing the full or partial cost of providing them. Non-excludability makes it difficult to withhold benefits from non-payers. Private providers may be unable to collect enough revenue to cover costs, so the market can fail to provide a needed public good. National defence and street lighting illustrate why public provision can be necessary.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q211","chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Tax rates and tax burden are different using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Progressive and Regressive Nature of Taxation is Different from Progressive and Regressive Rates of Taxation\nProgressive Rate: the tax rate rises with income. Regressive Rate: the tax rate falls with income. Proportionate Rate: the tax rate stays constant as income changes. Progressive Nature: the real burden is greater on the rich. Regressive Nature: the real burden is greater on the poor. The textbook stresses that a proportionate tax can still have a regressive real burden. When answering, specify whether you mean the numerical rate or the economic burden.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q212","chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Does a balanced budget leave AD unchanged? using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Does Balanced Budget Leave Aggregate Demand Unaffected in the Economy?\nNo. Equal tax collection and government expenditure do not necessarily have equal first-round effects on AD. Government expenditure enters AD in full; a tax increase reduces disposable income, and consumption falls by MPC times the tax rise. If G and taxes each rise by ₹100 and MPC is 0.5, expenditure raises AD by ₹100 while the initial consumption loss is ₹50, giving a net initial rise of ₹50. Under the simple lump-sum-tax model, later rounds imply a balanced-budget multiplier of one. Distinguish the initial AD change from the final income change.\nInitial ΔAD = ΔG − MPC × ΔT\nIf ΔG = ΔT: initial ΔAD = (1 − MPC)ΔG","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q213","chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Fixed versus flexible rates: named merits and demerits using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"11.4 MERITS AND DEMERITS OF FIXED AND FLEXIBLE EXCHANGE RATE\nFixed Rate—Merits: market stability and mobility of capital; growth of international trade; stable macroeconomic policies; devaluation as a policy tool; a check on inflation. Fixed Rate—Demerits: large reserve requirements; inefficient allocation of resources; limited forex market; discouraged venture capital; possible divergence from equilibrium. Flexible Rate—Merits: fewer reserves required for rate defence; market allocation of resources; larger forex market; international mobility of liquidity; support for venture capital. Flexible Rate—Demerits: weak-currency marginalisation; market uncertainty; difficulty maintaining stable monetary policy; difficulty with bilateral trade agreements; external shocks. These are the textbook’s headings: explain the relevant mechanism instead of only listing them.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q214","chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"In a two-sector economy, MPS is 0.25, planned investment ₹150 crore and equilibrium income ₹1,000 crore. Find autonomous consumption and dissaving at zero income.","solution":"At equilibrium S = I.\nS = −C̄ + 0.25Y.\n150 = −C̄ + 0.25 × 1,000 ⇒ C̄ = ₹100 crore.\nAt Y = 0, saving = −₹100 crore; the magnitude of dissaving is ₹100 crore.","tag":"Numerical"},{"id":"q215","chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Two firms A and B have equal GVA MP. B’s output is ₹1,200 crore and its purchases from A are ₹300 crore. A purchases ₹350 crore of inputs from B, exports ₹200 crore and adds ₹100 crore to stock. Assuming no other intermediate inputs, find A’s domestic sales.","solution":"GVA of B = 1,200 − 300 = ₹900 crore.\nGVA of A also equals ₹900 crore.\nOutput of A = GVA + Intermediate consumption = 900 + 350 = ₹1,250 crore.\nSales of A = Output − Change in stock = 1,250 − 100 = ₹1,150 crore.\nDomestic sales = Total sales − Exports = 1,150 − 200 = ₹950 crore.","tag":"Numerical"},{"id":"q216","chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"A business borrows abroad to buy equipment; the government separately obtains an overseas loan specifically to finance a BoP deficit. Classify each as autonomous or accommodating and explain why the account type alone does not decide this.","solution":"Autonomous Items\nThe business loan is autonomous because it is taken for an independent investment/profit motive.\nAccommodating Items\nThe government loan is accommodating because its stated purpose is financing the external imbalance.\nBoth may involve capital-account borrowing, but the purpose determines the autonomous/accommodating classification.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-0","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain before british rule and the colonial transformation. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Before British rule and the colonial transformation\n1. Before British rule: strong handicrafts, especially textiles, alongside a mainly agricultural economy.\n2. Colonial policy: turn India into a raw-material supplier and a market for British manufactured goods.\n3. Result: slow growth, low productivity, weak industry and widespread poverty.\nThe economy was organised around British needs, which explains the weaknesses in every sector.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-1","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain why agriculture stagnated. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Why agriculture stagnated\n1. Exploitative land settlement: high rent and insecure tenure left cultivators with little money or reason to improve land.\n2. Low investment: poor irrigation, old techniques and limited inputs kept yields low.\n3. Small, fragmented holdings: scattered plots made efficient cultivation difficult.\nMost people depended on farming, but dependence is not the same as productivity.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-2","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain commercialisation, subsistence and vulnerability. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Commercialisation, subsistence and vulnerability\n1. Subsistence farming: grow mainly for the family’s own use.\n2. Commercial farming: grow mainly for sale, including colonial cash crops such as cotton and jute.\n3. The problem: pressure to grow cash crops could reduce food cultivation without improving farmers’ incomes.\nCommercialisation describes where the crop goes; it does not guarantee that the farmer benefits.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-3","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain decline of handicrafts: causes and consequences. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Decline of handicrafts: causes and consequences\n1. British machine-made imports competed with Indian handmade goods.\n2. Discriminatory duties weakened Indian goods in foreign markets while favouring British goods in India.\n3. Loss of royal patronage reduced demand for specialised crafts.\n4. Displaced artisans moved towards agriculture because modern industry could not absorb them.\nDe-industrialisation = handicrafts decline without enough modern industry to replace them.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-4","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain a limited modern industrial base. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"A limited modern industrial base\n1. Cotton mills developed mainly in western India; jute mills were concentrated in Bengal.\n2. Iron and steel emerged later, including TISCO in 1907.\n3. Capital-goods industries remained weak, limiting the ability to make machinery.\n4. Modern industry grew too slowly to employ displaced artisans.\nA few successful industries existed, but the overall industrial base remained narrow.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-5","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain foreign trade and the drain of wealth. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Foreign trade and the drain of wealth\n1. Trade pattern: export raw materials; import manufactured goods.\n2. British control: trade served Britain’s factories and markets; the Suez Canal strengthened these links.\n3. Drain of wealth: export earnings financed British administration, wars and payments abroad.\nExport surplus means exports exceed imports. It helped India only if the resulting resources stayed and supported development.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-6","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain population conditions and the year 1921. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Population conditions and the year 1921\n1. Poor health: high death and infant-mortality rates, low life expectancy, disease and inadequate sanitation.\n2. Low literacy: education reached few people, especially women.\n3. 1921: the Year of the Great Divide—population growth became more consistently positive afterwards.\nHigh birth and death rates initially kept population growth modest; falling death rates later widened the gap.\nHistorical indicators in your textbook: birth rate ≈48 per 1,000; death rate ≈40 per 1,000; infant mortality ≈218 per 1,000 live births; life expectancy ≈32 years; literacy below 16%; female literacy ≈7%.\nInfant mortality concerns deaths below age one per 1,000 live births—not per 1,000 total people.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-7","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain occupational imbalance. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Occupational imbalance\n1. Occupational structure = how workers are distributed across sectors.\n2. About 70–75% worked in the primary sector; industry employed only about 10% in the textbook’s colonial summary.\n3. Weak non-farm opportunities pushed extra workers onto land, lowering average output per worker.\nEmployment share tells you where people work; GDP share tells you where output is produced.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-8","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain railways and other infrastructure: purpose and effects. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Railways and other infrastructure: purpose and effects\n1. Colonial purpose: move raw materials, sell British goods, transport troops and administer India.\n2. Infrastructure built: railways, ports, post and telegraph; rural roads and social facilities remained inadequate.\n3. Side effects: better transport, communication and links between regions.\nA balanced answer recognises the benefits while explaining that colonial interests determined the priorities.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-0","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain capitalist, socialist and mixed economies. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Capitalist, socialist and mixed economies\n1. Capitalist economy: private ownership; markets and profit guide production.\n2. Socialist economy: government ownership and planning guide resource use.\n3. Mixed economy: public and private sectors coexist; India used public leadership with regulated private enterprise.\nIndia wanted industrial growth while also pursuing social welfare and equity.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-1","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain economic planning and five year plans. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Economic planning and Five Year Plans\n1. Planning = deliberately allocating resources towards stated goals over a fixed period.\n2. India’s First Five Year Plan began in 1951.\n3. Plans coordinated investment in agriculture, industry and infrastructure when resources were scarce.\nCommon goals stayed broadly similar, but the priority given to each sector could change between plans.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-2","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain growth, modernisation, self-reliance and equity. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Growth, modernisation, self-reliance and equity\n1. Growth: increase productive capacity and output.\n2. Modernisation: adopt better technology and a progressive social outlook, including women’s participation.\n3. Self-reliance: reduce dependence on foreign resources for essential needs.\n4. Equity: ensure benefits reach all sections and basic needs are met.\nMore GDP does not automatically mean fairer distribution; growth and equity are separate goals.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-3","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain land reforms: changing agrarian relations. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Land reforms: changing agrarian relations\n1. Abolition of intermediaries: remove rent-collecting middlemen and connect cultivators directly with the state.\n2. Land ceiling: limit holdings and redistribute surplus land.\n3. Consolidation: combine scattered plots; cooperative farming: pool resources.\n4. Limitation: loopholes, litigation and weak implementation reduced the gains.\nInstitutional reforms change ownership and tenure; technical reforms change production methods.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-4","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain the green revolution package. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"The Green Revolution package\n1. Green Revolution = a sharp rise in agricultural output through HYV technology.\n2. The package: HYV seeds + irrigation + fertilisers + pesticides + credit.\n3. Early gains concentrated in wheat and well-irrigated regions; later gains spread to more crops and areas.\nSeeds alone cannot deliver the promised yield when irrigation and supporting inputs are missing.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-5","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain green revolution: gains. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Green Revolution: gains\n1. Higher yields raised foodgrain production and reduced dependence on imports.\n2. More marketable surplus supplied people outside agriculture.\n3. Better food supplies supported buffer stocks and price stability.\n4. Higher farm incomes increased demand for industrial goods.\nMarketable surplus = production left for sale after the farmer’s own requirements.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-6","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain green revolution: limitations. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Green Revolution: limitations\n1. Crop imbalance: early gains mainly benefited wheat and rice.\n2. Regional imbalance: well-irrigated regions advanced faster.\n3. Income inequality: larger farmers could afford the input package more easily.\n4. Environmental damage: excessive chemicals and groundwater use harmed soil and water.\nEvaluate both the increase in food output and how its gains and costs were distributed.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-7","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain the subsidy debate. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"The subsidy debate\n1. In favour: subsidies make inputs affordable, encourage new technology and support small farmers.\n2. Against: benefits may favour large farmers; government spending rises; cheap inputs can encourage waste.\n3. Better design: target farmers who need support and discourage excessive input use.\nThe issue is who receives the subsidy and what behaviour it encourages.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-8","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain why the public sector led industrialisation. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Why the public sector led industrialisation\n1. Basic and heavy industries needed large investments and long waiting periods before returns.\n2. Private firms often lacked the capital or incentive to undertake them.\n3. The public sector supplied infrastructure, strategic industries and investment in backward regions.\nPublic-sector leadership supported a mixed economy; it did not mean private firms disappeared.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-9","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain ipr 1956 and industrial licensing. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"IPR 1956 and industrial licensing\n1. IPR 1956: one group reserved for the state; a second with state leadership and private participation; the remainder open to regulated private enterprise.\n2. Industrial licensing controlled new factories and expansion.\n3. Purpose: direct scarce resources and promote regional balance; drawback: too many controls weakened competition.\nRemember the three groups by the degree of state involvement, rather than as an unexplained list.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-10","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain small-scale industries and the karve committee. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Small-scale industries and the Karve Committee\n1. Karve Committee, 1955: emphasised small industries’ role in rural development.\n2. Why protect them? They create jobs with relatively little capital and spread production across regions.\n3. Support: reserved products, concessions and credit.\n4. Weaknesses: limited finance, outdated technology and marketing difficulties.\nTheir main advantage was labour-intensive, decentralised growth.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-11","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain import substitution, tariffs and quotas. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Import substitution, tariffs and quotas\n1. Import substitution = make domestically what was previously imported.\n2. Tariff: tax imports, making them costlier. Quota: limit their quantity.\n3. Benefit: protect young domestic industries and conserve foreign exchange.\n4. Cost: prolonged protection weakens pressure to improve quality and efficiency.\nProtection can help an industry get started; permanent shelter can make it complacent.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-12","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain appraising the pre-reform strategy. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Appraising the pre-reform strategy\n1. Strategy: public-sector leadership, licensing, small-industry protection and import substitution.\n2. Achievements: a wider industrial base, infrastructure and greater food security.\n3. Limitations: inefficient enterprises, restrictive controls, weak job creation and persistent poverty.\nThe pre-reform strategy built capacity, but its limitations created pressure for change.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-0","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain why reforms became necessary in 1991. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Why reforms became necessary in 1991\n1. Fiscal pressure: spending exceeded revenue, increasing borrowing and debt.\n2. External crisis: foreign-exchange reserves fell to roughly two weeks of imports.\n3. Other problems: inflation and weak public-enterprise returns.\n4. Response: assistance from IMF/World Bank and a shift towards a more competitive economy.\n1991 combined an immediate payments crisis with deeper weaknesses in the old growth strategy.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-1","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain liberalisation: meaning and industrial reforms. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Liberalisation: meaning and industrial reforms\n1. Liberalisation = reduce direct government controls over producing units.\n2. Industrial changes: reduce licensing, open more activities to private firms, ease expansion and imports of capital goods.\n3. Mechanism: easier entry → more competition → pressure to improve efficiency.\nFewer controls does not mean no regulation; safety, competition and other safeguards still matter.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-2","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain financial-sector reforms. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Financial-sector reforms\n1. Increase competition by allowing more private and foreign participation in finance.\n2. Give banks more operational freedom within safeguards.\n3. Shift RBI’s emphasis from detailed control towards facilitation and regulation.\nRBI becoming a facilitator does not mean it stopped supervising banks.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-3","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain tax reforms, gst and demonetisation. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Tax reforms, GST and demonetisation\n1. Tax reforms: simplify taxes, rationalise rates, widen the tax base and improve compliance.\n2. GST, 1 July 2017: brought many indirect taxes into a common framework.\n3. Demonetisation, November 2016: withdrew legal-tender status of the then ₹500/₹1,000 notes, aiming to address unaccounted money and encourage formal/digital activity.\nKeep a policy’s objective separate from whether it fully achieved that objective.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-4","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain devaluation and trade reforms. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Devaluation and trade reforms\n1. 1991 devaluation: officially lower the rupee’s value to improve export competitiveness and tackle the external crisis.\n2. Trade reforms: reduce tariffs, quantitative restrictions and licensing barriers.\n3. Effect: cheaper exports in foreign-currency terms, costlier imports in rupees and greater competition for domestic firms.\nDevaluation is an official change; depreciation is a market-driven fall in currency value.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-5","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain privatisation and disinvestment. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Privatisation and disinvestment\n1. Privatisation: greater private ownership or management of public enterprises.\n2. Disinvestment: sale of government equity; a minority sale need not transfer control.\n3. Possible gains: efficiency, commercial discipline and revenue.\n4. Concerns: undervaluation, job losses and loss of future public income.\nAsk whether ownership or control actually changed before calling a sale full privatisation.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-6","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain autonomy and public-enterprise status. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Autonomy and public-enterprise status\n1. Selected public enterprises received greater financial and managerial freedom.\n2. Navratna/Maharatna arrangements aim to support quicker commercial decisions.\n3. The enterprise can remain government-owned while gaining autonomy.\nAutonomy changes decision-making freedom; privatisation changes the private sector’s role in ownership or management.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-7","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain globalisation and outsourcing. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Globalisation and outsourcing\n1. Globalisation = deeper integration through trade, investment, technology and services.\n2. Outsourcing = hiring an external provider for particular work.\n3. India’s advantages: skilled English-speaking workers, relatively low costs, IT links and time-zone differences.\nAn overseas company using an Indian accounting service is outsourcing; the wider cross-border integration is globalisation.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-8","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain wto: purpose and debate. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"WTO: purpose and debate\n1. WTO began in 1995, succeeding GATT.\n2. Role: multilateral trade rules, negotiations and dispute settlement.\n3. Debate: developing countries face unequal bargaining power and competition from subsidised producers in richer countries.\nExplain its intended rules-based role, then assess whether the gains are distributed fairly.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-9","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain reforms: favourable effects. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Reforms: favourable effects\n1. Competition and choice: firms face pressure to improve quality; consumers get more options.\n2. Investment and technology: foreign links can bring finance, knowledge and market access.\n3. Growth and external strength: the textbook highlights faster growth and stronger forex reserves over particular periods.\nUse a cause-and-effect chain, not just “reforms were good”: competition → efficiency → better products.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-10","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain reforms: criticisms and uneven gains. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Reforms: criticisms and uneven gains\n1. Agriculture: reduced support and foreign competition hurt some producers.\n2. Industry and jobs: import competition and capital-intensive growth limited gains for some firms/workers.\n3. Unequal development: benefits concentrated in certain sectors, cities and income groups.\n4. Other concerns: disinvestment, fiscal priorities, economic dependence and consumerism.\nReforms can raise total output while leaving particular people or sectors behind.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-0","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain human resource, human capital and formation. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Human resource, human capital and formation\n1. Human resource: people’s abilities to contribute to production.\n2. Human capital: the stock of their skills, knowledge and health.\n3. Human capital formation: adding to that stock through education, training and health investment.\nPopulation counts people; human capital asks what they are able to do.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-1","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain human capital versus human development. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Human capital versus human development\n1. Human capital view: education and health are means to higher productivity and income.\n2. Human development view: education, health, freedom and well-being are valuable ends in themselves.\n3. Implication: people deserve health and education even without an immediate measured productivity gain.\nSame services, different ultimate purpose: productive returns versus a better human life.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-2","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain physical capital versus human capital. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Physical capital versus human capital\n1. Physical capital: tangible, separable from its owner and saleable as an asset.\n2. Human capital: embodied in a person; only its services can be sold.\n3. Both depreciate, but learning and health investment can counter the loss of human capability.\n4. Human capital also creates social benefits, such as informed citizenship.\nA machine can be sold separately; a driver’s skill cannot be separated from the driver.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-3","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain education and health expenditure. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Education and health expenditure\n1. Education builds knowledge, skills and earning capacity.\n2. Health increases efficiency and reduces working time lost to illness.\n3. Health spending includes preventive care, treatment, awareness, hygiene and sanitation.\nThey complement each other: a sick worker cannot fully use even excellent skills.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-4","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain training, migration and information. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Training, migration and information\n1. On-the-job training: learn skills needed in actual work.\n2. Adult study: update knowledge and capabilities after initial schooling.\n3. Migration: bear travel/settlement costs to reach better opportunities.\n4. Information: spend to find suitable education and jobs.\nEach is an investment when its cost is expected to produce future benefits.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-5","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain how human capital contributes to growth. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"How human capital contributes to growth\n1. Change in emotional/material environment: education encourages new ideas and techniques.\n2. Higher productivity of physical capital: skilled workers use machines better.\n3. Higher participation and equality: more people can join and benefit from growth.\nThe relationship runs both ways: capabilities support growth, and growth finances better education and health.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-6","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain problems of human capital formation. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Problems of human capital formation\n1. Rising population stretches education and health resources.\n2. Brain drain reduces domestic access to skilled people.\n3. Deficient manpower planning creates a skills–jobs mismatch.\n4. Insufficient primary-sector training and low academic standards weaken practical capability.\nQualifications improve human capital only when they bring usable knowledge and skills.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-7","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain education: wider objectives. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Education: wider objectives\n1. Good citizenship and broader horizons: people make more informed choices.\n2. Science, technology and resource use: better knowledge improves production.\n3. Culture and personality: education develops people beyond their jobs.\n4. Participation: more people can contribute to development.\nEducation generates both private returns and benefits for society.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-8","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain why government intervenes in education and health. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Why government intervenes in education and health\n1. Public intervention widens access when private prices exclude poorer households.\n2. Government also finances costly facilities and sets quality standards.\n3. Kothari Commission: education spending of 6% of GDP.\n4. RTE Act, 2009: free and compulsory education for ages 6–14.\nSpending as a share of GDP and as a share of the government budget have different denominators.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-9","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain achievements, knowledge economy and remaining gaps. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Achievements, knowledge economy and remaining gaps\n1. Achievements: more schools, higher education, literacy and technical skills support a knowledge economy.\n2. Access gaps: illiteracy, gender bias, low rural access and expensive private provision.\n3. Quality gap: weak vocationalisation and insufficient public spending.\n4. Steep education pyramid: fewer people reach each higher level.\nAccess gets people into education; quality and relevant skills determine what they gain.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-0","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain rural development and the action plan. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Rural development and the action plan\n1. Rural development addresses economic, social and environmental problems together.\n2. Priorities: education/health, land and productive resources, infrastructure, credit and marketing.\n3. Diversification and poverty reduction create livelihoods beyond seasonal crop work.\nImproving rural life is broader than simply increasing farm output.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-1","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain why rural credit is needed. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Why rural credit is needed\n1. Short-term credit pays for seeds, fertilisers, wages and needs before harvest income arrives.\n2. Long-term credit pays for machinery, irrigation and land improvement.\n3. Institutional sources: cooperatives, banks, RRBs and land-development banks.\n4. Non-institutional sources: moneylenders, landlords and traders.\nThe timing gap between spending on a crop and earning from it explains the need for credit.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-2","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain nabard, self-help groups and micro-credit. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"NABARD, self-help groups and micro-credit\n1. NABARD, 1982: apex institution supporting and coordinating rural credit/development.\n2. SHGs pool members’ small savings and lend within the group, often linking to banks.\n3. Micro-credit can reach borrowers without conventional collateral and support women’s participation.\nSmall loans help inclusion; viable work, skills and market access determine whether incomes improve.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-3","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain weaknesses of rural credit. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Weaknesses of rural credit\n1. Unequal access: small farmers may still depend on informal lenders.\n2. Delays, paperwork and collateral make institutional credit hard to obtain.\n3. Crop failure and unproductive borrowing make repayment difficult.\n4. Overdues weaken lenders and restrict future credit.\nGood credit needs the right timing, terms and productive use—not just a larger loan.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-4","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain agricultural marketing and its defects. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Agricultural marketing and its defects\n1. Marketing includes gathering, processing, grading, packing, storage, transport and sale.\n2. Defects: faulty weights, poor information, weak transport/storage and large intermediary margins.\n3. Distress sale: urgent cash needs force farmers to sell cheaply after harvest.\nCredit and marketing are linked: debt pressure plus no storage weakens the farmer’s bargaining power.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-5","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain improving agricultural marketing. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Improving agricultural marketing\n1. Regulated markets improve fairness; roads, storage and information reduce losses.\n2. Cooperatives pool produce and strengthen bargaining.\n3. MSP, procurement, buffer stocks and PDS support the food-marketing system.\n4. Direct markets reduce intermediaries; contracts offer markets but can create bargaining risks.\nDifferent measures fix different problems: storage addresses timing; market information addresses bargaining.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-6","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain why rural diversification matters. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Why rural diversification matters\n1. Crop diversification changes the mix of crops; broader diversification adds allied/non-farm work.\n2. Why? Spread risk, earn outside the crop season and absorb surplus farm labour.\n3. Options: animal husbandry, fisheries, horticulture, processing and rural services.\nDiversification turns a single seasonal income source into several livelihood opportunities.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-7","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain animal husbandry, fisheries and operation flood. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Animal husbandry, fisheries and Operation Flood\n1. Animal husbandry supplies milk, eggs and meat, adding income alongside crops.\n2. Operation Flood: milk cooperatives linked producers and consumers, supporting the White Revolution.\n3. Fisheries create jobs but need finance, equipment, storage and protection from pollution.\n4. Veterinary care, feed and better breeds raise livestock productivity.\nAllied activities reduce dependence on crops, but still require reliable support services and markets.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-8","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain horticulture and information technology. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Horticulture and information technology\n1. Horticulture: fruits, vegetables, flowers and plantation crops can raise incomes and improve nutrition.\n2. Perishability makes cold chains, transport and processing important.\n3. IT provides weather, prices, advice and links to work/markets.\n4. Electricity, connectivity and skills determine who benefits from IT.\nProducing a valuable crop is only half the task; getting it to buyers without spoilage is the other half.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-9","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain organic farming: meaning and advantages. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Organic farming: meaning and advantages\n1. Organic farming uses organic inputs and ecological methods rather than synthetic chemicals.\n2. Benefits: healthier soil, less pollution and lower dependence on non-renewable chemical inputs.\n3. Textbook headings also emphasise healthier food, inexpensive technology and potential export premiums.\n4. India’s abundant labour can support labour-intensive organic methods.\nKeep the environmental benefits clear; premium prices and lower costs are possibilities, not guarantees.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-10","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain organic farming: constraints and the pranam box. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Organic farming: constraints and the PRANAM box\n1. Constraints: lower transition yields, high cost per unit and shorter shelf life.\n2. Farmers need training, suitable inputs, certification and marketing.\n3. PRANAM box: encourage alternative nutrients and balanced fertiliser use to protect soil health.\nOrganic farming becomes viable when production support is matched by a reliable market.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-0","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain workers and economic activity. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Workers and economic activity\n1. A worker participates in an economic activity producing goods or services.\n2. Self-employed and hired workers both count.\n3. Unpaid family helpers in a farm/business can count even without a separate wage.\n4. Own-family domestic services are generally outside measured employment.\nDo not confuse “no wage” with “no economic production”.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-1","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain self-employed, regular and casual workers. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Self-employed, regular and casual workers\n1. Self-employed: operate their own enterprise, such as a shop owner.\n2. Regular salaried: continuing employment with regular pay.\n3. Casual wage labourer: hired for short periods/tasks, such as a daily-wage construction worker.\nEmployment status tells you the work relationship; construction or agriculture tells you the sector.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-2","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain labour force, workforce and participation. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Labour force, workforce and participation\n1. Workforce: people actually employed.\n2. Labour force: employed people plus unemployed people willing and available for work.\n3. Labour supply: the hours/services people offer at a wage.\n4. Someone not seeking or available for work is outside the labour force, rather than automatically unemployed.\nWorker-population ratio uses population; unemployment rate uses labour force. Always check the denominator.\nLabour force = Workforce + Unemployed\nWorker-population ratio = Workers ÷ Population × 100\nUnemployment rate = Unemployed ÷ Labour force × 100","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-3","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain rural–urban and male–female participation. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Rural–urban and male–female participation\n1. Rural work participation reflects farming, family labour and the need to earn even in low-productivity work.\n2. Urban patterns differ because of education and the available jobs.\n3. Women face care responsibilities, social barriers and under-recording of economic activity.\nA lower measured participation rate does not mean women make no productive contribution.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-4","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain workforce across sectors. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Workforce across sectors\n1. Primary: agriculture and allied activities.\n2. Secondary: manufacturing and construction. Tertiary: services.\n3. Rural workers are more concentrated in farming; urban work is more concentrated outside it.\n4. A high farm employment share with a low farm GDP share signals low average output per worker.\nDevelopment needs productive non-farm jobs, not just movement out of agriculture.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-5","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain jobless growth and technology. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Jobless growth and technology\n1. Jobless growth: GDP rises faster than employment opportunities.\n2. Capital-intensive technology and productivity gains can raise output without many new jobs.\n3. Responses: labour-intensive activities, small enterprises, relevant skills and rural diversification.\nIt means inadequate job creation relative to growth—not necessarily zero new jobs in every year.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-6","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain casualisation of the workforce. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Casualisation of the workforce\n1. Casualisation: a rising share of casual wage work rather than secure regular jobs/self-employment.\n2. Surplus rural labour migrates; limited jobs and weak bargaining power encourage daily-wage hiring.\n3. Consequences: insecurity and fewer benefits.\nThe concept describes a process; check actual table years before claiming the casual share always rose.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-7","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain formal and informal employment. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Formal and informal employment\n1. Formal sector: government/public enterprises and private establishments with ten or more workers in the textbook definition.\n2. Informal sector: smaller private enterprises, farming and self-employment.\n3. Informalisation: more employment without formal protection and social-security benefits.\nCasualisation concerns job status; informalisation concerns organisation and protection. They can overlap.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-8","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain open, disguised and seasonal unemployment. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Open, disguised and seasonal unemployment\n1. Open: willing and able to work, but unable to get a job.\n2. Disguised: extra workers can leave without reducing output; their marginal productivity is zero.\n3. Seasonal: work disappears outside the working season.\n4. Educated/structural: qualifications or skills do not match available opportunities.\nIf two people leave a family farm and output stays the same, those two were disguisedly unemployed.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-9","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain why unemployment persists. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Why unemployment persists\n1. Slow job growth and rapid population growth widen the employment gap.\n2. Seasonal farming and inadequate irrigation leave people without year-round work.\n3. Joint-family farming can conceal surplus labour; declining cottage industries reduce alternatives.\n4. Low investment, skills mismatch and limited mobility restrict opportunities.\nExplain the mechanism after the heading: inadequate irrigation → fewer crop seasons → seasonal unemployment.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-10","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain government measures and mgnrega. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Government measures and MGNREGA\n1. Direct employment: government hires workers itself. Indirect: public activity raises demand and jobs in other firms.\n2. MGNREGA: at least 100 days of unskilled manual wage work per financial year for a rural household whose adults volunteer.\n3. Work is to be provided within 15 days of demand; eligible failure attracts unemployment allowance.\n4. Skills, rural livelihoods, roads and small-business support create other opportunities.\nThe MGNREGA entitlement is per household, not 100 days separately for every adult.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-mcq-1","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The principal purpose of colonial economic policy in India was to:","options":["Achieve Indian industrial self-reliance","Serve Britain’s economic interests","Develop Indian consumer welfare","Eliminate agricultural exports"],"answer":1,"explanation":"India supplied raw materials and became a market for British manufactured goods.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-2","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An export surplus under British rule did not necessarily benefit India because:","options":["India exported only services","All export earnings went to cultivators","The surplus financed colonial payments abroad","India had no imports"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The surplus financed administrative, war and other British expenses: the drain of wealth.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-3","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Statement I: Agriculture dominated colonial employment. Statement II: This necessarily implied high agricultural productivity.","options":["I is false; II is true","I is true; II is false","Both are false","Both are true"],"answer":1,"explanation":"High dependence on agriculture coexisted with low productivity and stagnation.","tag":"Two statements"},{"id":"ied-mcq-4","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a cause of agricultural stagnation under British rule?","options":["Abundant institutional credit","Exploitative land settlement","Heavy investment in irrigation","Universal ownership rights for tenants"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Insecure tenure and high rent discouraged investment, alongside low technology and inadequate irrigation.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-5","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"De-industrialisation means:","options":["A fall in agricultural employment only","Decline of handicrafts without adequate modern industrial replacement","Growth of every manufacturing industry","A ban on all imports"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Artisans lost livelihoods while modern industry grew too slowly to absorb them.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-6","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"1921 is called the Year of the Great Divide in the context of:","options":["Industrial licensing","GST","Population growth","Public-sector disinvestment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It marks the transition from irregular/stagnant population growth towards sustained growth.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-7","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Commercialisation of agriculture primarily concerns:","options":["Abolishing land revenue","Producing for market sale","Replacing every crop with machinery","Producing only for family consumption"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Its defining feature is production for sale; improved farmer welfare does not automatically follow.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-8","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Assertion: Railways had some beneficial effects on India. Reason: Their primary colonial objective was Indian social welfare.","options":["Assertion true; reason false","Both true; reason explains assertion","Both true; reason does not explain assertion","Assertion false; reason true"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Transport and communication benefits existed, but colonial strategic and commercial interests were the primary motive.","tag":"Assertion–reason"},{"id":"ied-mcq-9","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Occupational structure measures:","options":["Distribution of workers across activities","Only exports by commodity","Distribution of tax rates","Distribution of government debt"],"answer":0,"explanation":"It concerns where people work, rather than sectors’ GDP shares.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-10","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The decline of royal courts mainly weakened:","options":["The government’s GST collections","The supply of British machine-made imports","Modern service exports","Demand for traditional handicrafts"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Royal patronage had supported specialised handicrafts.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-11","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is NOT one of the four common goals of planning?","options":["Growth","Modernisation","Permanent dependence on imports","Equity"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The fourth goal is self-reliance.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-12","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Replacing imports with domestically produced goods is:","options":["Import substitution","Disinvestment","Outsourcing","Informalisation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An inward-looking strategy supported domestic industry through protection.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-13","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A quota differs from a tariff because a quota:","options":["Always makes imports free","Is only a subsidy","Is a tax on income","Sets a quantitative limit on imports"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A tariff is a tax on imports; a quota restricts their quantity.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-14","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Abolition of intermediaries is an example of:","options":["Foreign-exchange reform","A monetary-policy instrument","Institutional land reform","Technical reform"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It changes agrarian ownership and tenure relations.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-15","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"HYV seeds produced strong results when combined with:","options":["Irrigation, fertilisers and supporting inputs","Only rain and no capital","A ban on farm credit","Removal of all storage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Green Revolution was an input package, not seeds alone.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-16","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Marketable surplus is:","options":["Only imported food","Government tax revenue","Output remaining for sale after the farmer’s own requirements","Total farm output"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It is the portion available for the market.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-17","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The Karve Committee (1955) is associated with:","options":["Demonetisation","WTO establishment","Currency devaluation","Small-scale industries"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It recognised small industries’ contribution to rural development and employment.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-18","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Equity as a planning goal emphasises:","options":["Only imported machinery","Eliminating all private firms","Only a rise in total output","Benefits reaching all sections and meeting basic needs"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Growth and equity are distinct: higher GDP alone does not ensure fair distribution.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-19","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Assertion: Initial Green Revolution gains were regionally uneven. Reason: Access to irrigation and complementary inputs varied across regions.","options":["Both true; reason does not explain assertion","Assertion false; reason true","Both true; reason explains assertion","Assertion true; reason false"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Regions with irrigation and resources could adopt the package more effectively.","tag":"Assertion–reason"},{"id":"ied-mcq-20","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is an argument AGAINST indiscriminate input subsidies?","options":["They eliminate all inequality","They can encourage excessive water and chemical use","They always raise government revenue","They make every crop organic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fiscal burdens and ecological damage are central criticisms, alongside unequal benefits.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-21","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A mixed economy has:","options":["Both public and private sectors","No planning or markets","Only private ownership","Only government ownership"],"answer":0,"explanation":"India combined public-sector leadership with regulated private enterprise.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-22","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Modernisation includes:","options":["Only land ceilings","Complete withdrawal from foreign trade","Only machinery, with no change in social outlook","Technology and progressive changes in social outlook"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The textbook includes social attitudes, such as women’s participation, alongside technology.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-23","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The severe external crisis of 1991 was marked by:","options":["No import requirements","Very low reserves and a balance-of-payments problem","Foreign-exchange reserves sufficient for decades","The introduction of MGNREGA"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reserves were barely enough for about two weeks of imports.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-24","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"LPG stands for:","options":["Liberalisation, privatisation, globalisation","Licensing, planning, grants","Loans, production, government","Labour, prices, growth"],"answer":0,"explanation":"These are the three main elements of the New Economic Policy.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-25","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Selling a minority government shareholding without transferring control is:","options":["Import substitution","Necessarily full privatisation","Nationalisation","Disinvestment"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Disinvestment can occur without a transfer of management control.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-26","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Statement I: Liberalisation reduces certain direct controls. Statement II: It eliminates all government regulation.","options":["Both false","I false; II true","I true; II false","Both true"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Prudential, safety and competition regulation can remain.","tag":"Two statements"},{"id":"ied-mcq-27","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An official reduction in currency value in a fixed or managed arrangement is:","options":["Appreciation","Informalisation","Revaluation","Devaluation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Distinguish devaluation from market-driven depreciation.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-28","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An Indian firm providing accounting services to a foreign company illustrates:","options":["Import substitution","Buffer stocking","Land ceiling","Outsourcing"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The client contracts an activity to an external provider.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-29","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"WTO was established in:","options":["1951","1947","1991","1995"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It succeeded GATT and provides a framework for multilateral trade rules.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-30","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a criticism of reform outcomes?","options":["The absence of foreign competition","Guaranteed equal benefits for all farmers","The abolition of every private enterprise","Uneven gains across sectors and regions"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Agriculture, employment and regional imbalance are important appraisal points.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-31","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"GST was introduced in India on:","options":["8 November 2016","1 January 1991","1 July 2017","26 January 1950"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Demonetisation occurred in November 2016; GST followed in July 2017.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-32","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Consumer sovereignty increased with reforms mainly through:","options":["A universal ban on imports","Greater choice and competition","Elimination of product variety","Government ownership of every shop"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Competition and entry expanded consumers’ range of goods and services.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-33","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Public-enterprise autonomy necessarily means the enterprise has been sold to private owners. This statement is:","options":["False; autonomy can expand while public ownership remains","True only for schools","True in every case","The definition of a tariff"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Managerial autonomy and ownership transfer are distinct.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-34","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Human capital is best described as:","options":["Knowledge, skills and health embodied in people","Only the population count","The number of buildings","Government financial debt"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Capabilities, rather than headcount alone, determine human capital.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-35","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Human development treats education and health as:","options":["Physical machinery","Ends valuable for people’s well-being","Only means to raise productivity","A form of indirect tax"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Human capital stresses productive returns; human development values well-being itself.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-36","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is NOT a source of human capital formation?","options":["Information about jobs","On-the-job training","Health expenditure","Buying an existing machine without training"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The machine is physical capital; the other options improve people’s capabilities.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-37","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Vaccination is an example of:","options":["Disinvestment","Seasonal unemployment","Only curative treatment","Preventive medicine"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Preventive care reduces the likelihood of illness.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-38","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Assertion: Migration can be an investment in human capital. Reason: Its costs may be undertaken to obtain higher future earnings.","options":["Both true; reason does not explain assertion","Both true; reason explains assertion","Assertion true; reason false","Assertion false; reason true"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Travel and settlement costs are weighed against expected returns.","tag":"Assertion–reason"},{"id":"ied-mcq-39","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which distinction is correct?","options":["Human capital is separable from its owner","Physical capital is intangible","Human capital cannot ever lose value","Human capital is inseparable from its owner"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A person supplies the services of human capital; the capability is embodied in that person.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-40","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The Kothari Commission recommendation discussed in the textbook is education expenditure of:","options":["60% of all wages","6% of GDP","6% of population","0.6% of exports"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Be precise about the denominator: GDP, not total government spending.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-41","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The RTE Act, 2009 concerns free and compulsory education for children aged:","options":["15–21","0–3","6–14","Only above 18"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The age group is 6–14 years.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-42","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Years of schooling alone may inadequately measure human capital because:","options":["Education has no benefits","Health is always irrelevant","Quality and acquired skills also matter","Every school is identical"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Quantity of schooling cannot fully capture effective capability.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-43","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Brain drain means:","options":["Migration of skilled people abroad","A rise in groundwater","A tariff on books","A fall in machinery imports"],"answer":0,"explanation":"It can reduce the domestic availability of skilled human resources.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-44","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Rural development is:","options":["Only the construction of urban roads","The abolition of all farming","Only an increase in wheat output","A comprehensive improvement in rural economic and social conditions"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It includes credit, marketing, infrastructure, human resources and diversification.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-45","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"NABARD was established in:","options":["1982","2017","1995","1955"],"answer":0,"explanation":"It is the apex institution discussed for rural credit and development.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-46","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a non-institutional source of rural credit?","options":["Commercial bank","Regional rural bank","Moneylender","Cooperative credit society"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Moneylenders, traders and landlords are non-institutional sources.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-47","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"SHGs generally begin by:","options":["Selling central-bank reserves","Fixing import quotas","Guaranteeing unlimited corporate loans","Pooling small savings of members"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Pooled savings support small loans and can facilitate bank linkage.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-48","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A distress sale occurs when a farmer:","options":["Receives a guaranteed premium","Sells urgently at an unfavourable price due to immediate needs","Waits indefinitely for the best price","Always exports organic crops"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Debt and lack of storage may force sale immediately after harvest.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-49","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Agricultural marketing includes:","options":["Only obtaining a bank loan","Only retail sale","Only growing seeds","Processing, grading, storage, transport and sale"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It covers the chain between production and consumption.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-50","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Operation Flood is associated with:","options":["Currency devaluation","Wheat imports","Milk cooperatives and the White Revolution","Coal nationalisation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It linked milk producers and consumers through cooperative organisation.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-51","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Diversification can reduce seasonal unemployment by:","options":["Creating allied and non-farm work","Banning rural services","Eliminating storage","Making every farmer grow only one seasonal crop"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alternative activities provide work outside the crop season.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-52","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a constraint of organic farming?","options":["Guaranteed premium prices for all farmers","Certification and marketing difficulties","No need for any knowledge","Automatic high yields in every transition"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Training, yields, shelf life and market access also matter.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-53","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Rythu Bazaars illustrate:","options":["Direct agricultural marketing","Foreign-exchange dealing","Disinvestment","Industrial licensing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Direct links can reduce intermediaries and improve farmers’ returns.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-54","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Horticulture needs cold chains mainly because:","options":["Its output is always metal","It never needs transport","It is unrelated to agriculture","Its output is often perishable"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Storage and transport reduce spoilage and improve market access.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-55","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A family member helping produce crops without a separate wage is:","options":["Always outside the workforce","An unpaid family worker engaged in economic activity","Necessarily openly unemployed","Only a consumer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A separate wage is not required for participation in economic production.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-56","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Labour force equals:","options":["Only people receiving salaries","Workforce plus unemployed people seeking/available for work","Total population in every case","Only casual workers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Workforce counts employed people; labour force also includes the unemployed.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-57","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A household has six farm workers; two leave and output is unchanged. The two were:","options":["Necessarily formally employed","Seasonally unemployed by definition","Disguisedly unemployed","Not members of the labour force"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Their marginal productivity was zero.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-mcq-58","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Casualisation concerns:","options":["A rise only in formal-sector social security","Only the growth of exports","An increase in university buildings","Movement towards casual wage work"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It is about employment status; informalisation concerns organisation and protection.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-59","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The textbook’s formal-sector definition includes:","options":["Every household service","Government departments and private establishments with ten or more workers","Only own-account farmers","Only private firms with fewer than ten workers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Public enterprises are also included in the formal sector.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-60","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Jobless growth means:","options":["Every person has a government job","Zero output growth in every year","All workers become farmers","Output growth without adequate corresponding employment growth"],"answer":3,"explanation":"GDP may grow much faster than employment.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-61","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The MGNREGA employment guarantee is primarily:","options":["100 days for every adult separately","A permanent salaried urban job","A subsidy only for machinery","100 days per rural household in a financial year"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Adult household members volunteer for unskilled manual work; the entitlement is household-based.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-62","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Worker-population ratio uses which denominator?","options":["Number of factories","Labour force","Only unemployed people","Population"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Workers/population ×100. The unemployment rate instead uses labour force.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-63","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A daily-wage construction worker is classified by employment status as:","options":["Outside all economic activity","Casual wage labourer","Necessarily a regular salaried employee","Always self-employed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Construction is the sector; casual wage labour is the employment status.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-64","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Statement I: Informal workers generally lack many formal social-security benefits. Statement II: Informalisation and casualisation mean exactly the same thing.","options":["I true; II false","I false; II true","Both false","Both true"],"answer":0,"explanation":"One concerns protection/organisation; the other concerns employment status.","tag":"Two statements"},{"id":"ied-mcq-65","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If agriculture employs a much larger share of workers than its share of GDP, it suggests:","options":["The GDP and employment shares must be equal","Agriculture produces all GDP","Every worker has zero output","Relatively low average output per agricultural worker"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The comparison indicates a productivity gap, not zero productivity for every worker.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-written-1","chapter":"i6","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"A village has 2,000 people, 900 employed people and 100 unemployed people seeking work. Calculate its labour force, worker-population ratio and unemployment rate.","solution":"Labour force = 900 + 100 = 1,000.\nWorker-population ratio = 900/2,000 ×100 = 45%.\nUnemployment rate = 100/1,000 ×100 = 10%.\nThe two rates use different denominators.","tag":"Application"},{"id":"ied-written-2","chapter":"i6","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"A region’s population is 5 lakh and its worker-population ratio is 40%. Its labour force is 2.5 lakh. Calculate workers, unemployed people and the unemployment rate.","solution":"Workers = 5 ×0.40 = 2 lakh.\nUnemployed = 2.5 −2 = 0.5 lakh.\nUnemployment rate = 0.5/2.5 ×100 = 20%.","tag":"Application"},{"id":"ied-written-3","chapter":"i2","type":"numerical","marks":3,"q":"A farm produces 120 quintals of wheat, retaining 35 quintals for family consumption and seed requirements. Find the marketable surplus.","solution":"Marketable surplus = Total output − Retained requirements.\n= 120 −35 = 85 quintals.\nMarketable surplus is not total farm production.","tag":"Application"},{"id":"ied-written-4","chapter":"i4","type":"numerical","marks":3,"q":"Education spending is ₹60 crore, GDP is ₹1,000 crore and total government expenditure is ₹300 crore. Express education spending as a percentage of each denominator.","solution":"Share of GDP = 60/1,000 ×100 = 6%.\nShare of government spending = 60/300 ×100 = 20%.\nBoth describe the same spending, but they answer different questions.","tag":"Application"},{"id":"ied-written-5","chapter":"i6","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"An economy produces output worth 1,000 units, of which agriculture contributes 200. Agriculture employs 50 of the economy’s 100 workers. Compare average output per worker in agriculture and the rest of the economy.","solution":"Agriculture: 200/50 = 4 output units per worker.\nOther sectors: (1,000−200)/(100−50) = 800/50 = 16 units per worker.\nAverage output per worker outside agriculture is four times as high. Employment shares and output shares are not interchangeable.","tag":"Application"},{"id":"ied-written-6","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"“Railways prove that colonial economic policy primarily aimed to develop India.” Evaluate this statement.","solution":"Colonial infrastructure: purpose and effects\nThe statement confuses beneficial effects with the primary objective.\nRailways moved raw materials, expanded markets for British manufactures and served military/administrative needs.\nThey also improved transport, movement of people and commercial links.\nThese benefits should be acknowledged, but colonial interests determined priorities and design.","tag":"Evaluate a statement"},{"id":"ied-written-7","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"A small farmer cannot adopt HYV seeds because irrigation and affordable credit are missing. Explain why distributing seeds alone may fail, and identify two suitable measures.","solution":"Green Revolution: complementary inputs\nHYV technology requires irrigation, fertilisers, plant protection and supporting finance. Seeds alone cannot secure the expected yield.\nExpand reliable irrigation and provide timely institutional credit on suitable terms.\nTraining and access to inputs/marketing can support adoption.\nTargeting small farmers can reduce unequal gains.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-written-8","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"The government sells 20% of its equity in an enterprise but retains control. Another public enterprise receives greater managerial autonomy. Are both necessarily privatisation? Explain.","solution":"Disinvestment, privatisation and autonomy\nThe equity sale is disinvestment. A minority sale with government control retained is not necessarily full privatisation.\nManagerial autonomy increases freedom to make decisions; it does not itself transfer ownership.\nPrivatisation involves a greater private role through ownership or management transfer.\nIdentify the actual change in control rather than relying only on the word reform.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-written-9","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"A health programme is justified solely by its effect on worker productivity. Explain the human-capital view and add the human-development perspective.","solution":"Human capital versus human development\nThe human-capital view treats health as an investment improving efficiency, attendance and productive earnings.\nThe human-development view regards health and a better quality of life as ends in themselves.\nIt supports health access even when immediate measured productivity gains are limited.\nThe perspectives overlap in valuing health, but differ in the ultimate purpose.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-written-10","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"A farmer sells immediately after harvest at a low price to repay a moneylender. Explain the linked credit and marketing problems, and suggest three measures.","solution":"Rural credit and agricultural marketing\nImmediate repayment needs and inadequate storage create a distress sale, weakening bargaining power.\nTimely affordable institutional credit can reduce dependence on the moneylender.\nStorage and warehouse facilities allow the farmer to delay sale.\nCooperative/direct marketing, better price information and fair regulated transactions improve returns.\nExplain how each measure addresses a specific difficulty.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-written-11","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Output rises rapidly while secure jobs grow slowly and more workers take daily-wage work. Identify and distinguish the two processes.","solution":"Jobless growth and casualisation\nJobless growth: output increases without adequate corresponding employment growth. Capital-intensive techniques can contribute.\nCasualisation: movement towards casual wage work rather than regular salaried work or self-employment.\nThe first compares output and job growth; the second concerns employment status and security.\nDo not assume they are identical or that every casual worker is openly unemployed.","tag":"Case-based"}]};
